{"results":[{"url":"https://www.washingtoncountyor.gov/elections/documents/november-3-2026-voters-pamphlet/download?inline","final_url":"https://www.washingtoncountyor.gov/elections/documents/november-3-2026-voters-pamphlet/download?inline","title":"Washington County Oregon_2026 November.indd","description":null,"language":null,"text":"Elections Division \n2925 NE Aloclek Dr #170 \nHillsboro, OR 97124-7523 \n \nwww.washcovotes.org\n  \nWashington \nCounty \nVoters’   Pamphlet \n \nGENERAL \n ELECTION \nNovember 3, 2026 \n \n \n  \n \n \n         \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nWashington County \nBoard of County \nCommissioners  \n \nKathryn Harrington, Chair \nNafisa Fai, District 1 \nPam Treece, District 2 \nJason Snider, District 3 \nJerry Willey, District 4 \nATTENTION \nThis is your county voters’ pamphlet. Washington County \nElections prints information as submitted. We  do not \ncorrect spelling, punctuation, grammar, syntax,  errors, or \ninaccurate information. This pamphlet contains \ninformation for several districts and there may be \ncandidates/measures that are not included on your ballot. \nIf you have any questions, call 503 -846-5800.\n\nWC-2\n \nSelected portions of this county voters’ pamphlet are available in Arabic, \nSimplified and Traditional Chinese, Farsi, Japanese, Khmer, Korean, \nRussian, Spanish, Tagalog and Vietnamese. These translations can be found \nonline at oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP \n \n \n \n ﻰﻠﻋ تﻧرﺗﻧﻹا رﺑﻋ تﺎﻣﺟرﺗﻟا هذھ ﻰﻠﻋ روﺛﻌﻟا نﻛﻣﯾ .ﺔﯾﺑرﻌﻟا ﺔﻐﻠﻟﺎﺑ ﺔﻌطﺎﻘﻣﻟا هذھ ﻲﻓ نﯾﺑﺧﺎﻧﻟا بﯾﺗﻛ نﻣ ةرﺎﺗﺧﻣ ءازﺟأ رﻓوﺗﺗ \noregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP \n \n本县选民手册中的选定内容提供简体中文版本。可访oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP 获取这些翻译\n版本。 \n \n本縣選民手冊的選定部分提供繁體中文版本。可訪問 oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP 獲取這些翻譯\n版本。 \n ﺖﺳا دﻮﺟﻮﻣ ﯽﺳرﺎﻓ نﺎﺑز ﻪﺑ ﯽﺘﻧﺎﮐ نﺎﮔﺪﻨھدیأر رﻮﺷوﺮﺑ زا ﯽﺒﺨﺘﻨﻣ یﺎھﺶﺨﺑ \nﺖﯾﺎﺳ بو رد ﻦﯾﻼﻧآ ترﻮﺻﻪﺑ ناﻮﺗﯽﻣ ار ﺎھﻪﻤﺟﺮﺗ ﻦﯾا  WashingtonVPoregonvotes.gov/  ﺖﻓﺎﯾ \n \n \nこの郡有権者向けパンフレットの⼀部は⽇本語でもご利⽤いただけます。これらの翻訳は、\noregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVPでオンラインでご覧いただけます。 \n \nែផ#កែដល'នេ*ជ,សេរ/សៃនខិត4ប័ណ8ស*9ប់អ#កេ'ះេ=#ត*ស>កេនះ គឺAចរក'នCDEែខFរ។ Hរបកែ*បIំងេនះAចរកេឃ,ញេល,បN O ញេP \noregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP \n \n카운티 유권자 팸플릿 내 선택된 부분은 한국어로 준비되어 있습니다. 번역은 온라인 \noregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP에서 찾아보실 수 있습니다. \n \nОтдельные части брошюры избирателей этого округа доступны на русском языке. Эти \nпереводы можно найти в Интернете по адресу oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP \n \nAlgunas secciones de este folleto para votantes del condado están disponibles en español. Estas \ntraducciones están disponibles en línea en oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP \n \nMababasa sa wikang Tagalog ang piling mga bahagi ng pamplet na ito para sa mga botante ng \ncounty. Makikita online ang mga pagsasaling-wikang ito sa oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP  \n \nCác phần được chọn trong tập thông tin cử tri quận này có bản ngôn ngữ tiếng Việt. Bản dịch có thể \nxem trực tuyến tại  oregonvotes.gov/WashingtonVP\n\nPage\nTranslation Letter         WC-2\nCANDIDATES\nWashington County         WC-4-5\nCity of Banks         WC-6-8\nCity of Beaverton         WC-9\nCity of Cornelius        WC-10\nCity of Forest Grove       WC-11-13\nCity of Gaston        WC-14\nCity of Hillsboro        WC-15-18\nCity of King City        WC-19-21\nCity of Lake Oswego      WC-22\nCity of North Plains        WC-23-25\nCity of Portland        WC-26-30\nCity of Rivergrove        WC-31-33\nCity of Sherwood        WC-34\nCity of Tigard        WC-35-39\nCity of Tualatin        WC-40-41\nCity of Wilsonville       WC-42-43\nTualatin Soil & Water Conservation District     WC-44-45\nWest Multnomah Soil & Water Conservation District   WC-46\nMEASURES\n3-641  City of Wilsonville      WC-47-50\n26-267  City of Portland      WC-51-54\n34-352  City of Banks      WC-55\n34-353  City of Forest Grove     WC-56\n34-354  City of King City      WC-57-58\n34-355  Hillsboro School District 1J    WC-59-65\n \nLIST OF BALLOT DROP SITES      WC-67\n\nWC-4\nCommissioner At-Large\nWashington CountyWashington County\nCommissioner At-Large\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nPam Treece\nOccupation: County \nCommissioner\nOccupational Background: \nEconomic Development, \nPublic School Teacher, Small \nBusiness Owner\nEducational Background: \nPSU; Lewis & Clark, MAT\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Transportation \nFunding Task Force, Metro \nEquity Strategy Committee\nCommunity Involvement: \nPlanned Parenthood, Centro Cultural, Bicycle Transportation \nAlliance\nDear Neighbors, \nAs your Chair, in this time of national insecurity, I will guarantee \ncounty operations are stable, accountable, effective, and \nequitable so we can provide the services we all deserve and \ntoo many desperately need. My career in the private and public \nsectors ensures success. \nNo ideology,  just results.\nMy Priorities\n• Growing our local economy and creating good-paying \njobs.\n• Putting the brakes on taxing residents and hitting the \ngas on building housing.\n• Real investments in public safety, including safe \nschools, parks, and trails. \n• Protecting our residents from ICE. \n• Protecting public health.\n• Transportation infrastructure to reduce traffic \ncongestion.\n“Pam is the Chair we need to address our most pressing\nchallenges, and she has the record of results and the \nexperience to help us succeed.” Beaverton Mayor Lacey Beaty\n“Pam does the hard work to achieve real results for our \ncommunities, and she is the leader we need now.” Washington \nCounty Commissioners Jason Snider & Jerry Willey\nJoin Us In Supporting Pam\n \nChampions for Working Families\nNW Oregon Labor Council AFL-CIO\nUA Local 290, IBEW 48\nCarpenters Union\nIndependent Party of Oregon\n \nFirst Responders\nWashington County and Hillsboro Police Officers Associations\nTualatin Valley Firefighters Local 1660\nHillsboro Firefighters Local 2210\nPat Garrett, Former Washington County Sheriff\nBob Hermann, Former Washington County DA\n \nWashington County Mayors  \nLacey Beaty, Beaverton; Beach Pace, Hillsboro;\nFrank Bubenik, Tualatin; Tim Rosener, Sherwood;\nMalynda Wenzl, Forest Grove;\nYi-Kang Hu, Tigard; Jef Dalin, Cornelius;\nMarsha Kirk, Banks; Bill Martin, Gaston; \nJoshua Drake, Durham\nLocal Business Leaders\nWashington County Chamber PAC\nBeaverton Chamber of Commerce\nTigard Chamber of Commerce\n& Community Leaders\nFormer Governors Barbara Roberts & Ted Kulongoski\nChair Kathryn Harrington \nHumane Voters Oregon\nCommissioner-elect Felicita Monteblanco\nSenators Kate Lieber, Janeen Sollman, Lisa Reynolds\nRepresentatives Ken Helm, Hai Pham, Sue Rieke-Smith\nwww.PamForWashingtonCounty.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Pam Treece\nNafisa Fai\nOccupation: Washington \nCounty Commissioner\nOccupational Background: \nPublic Health, Public Policy & \nCommunity Building\nEducational Background: \nPortland State, BS\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Oregon Maternal \nMortality and Morbidity Review \nCommittee, Portland Police \nCitizen Academy, Clean Water \nServices Advisory Commission, Metro JPACT\nNafisa Fai\nA Leader Who Works for You-Not for the Status Quo\nThirty years ago, I came to Oregon as an immigrant and a \nrefugee. As County Commissioner since 2021, I fight to ensure \nevery resident has a home, a job and a thriving community.\nMy Record:\n• Led the funding and building of the Center for Addiction \nTriage and Treatment\n• Ended homeless encampment through housing and \nservices, prevented eviction\n• Strengthened public safety and supported first responders\n• Expanded Affordable housing\n• Delivered the County’s First Climate Action Plan\n• Fought to protect farmland and diversified local economy\n• Secured millions for safer roads, transit, and multimodal \ntransportation\nAs Chair, I will keep our communities safe, support economic \nand job growth, protect affordability, make Data Centers \npay fair share and safeguard tax dollars. I oppose federal \noverreach, abuse of power and will defend the rule of law.\n“Nafisa Fai puts community voices first and hosts regular \ncommunity conversations. We strongly support Nafisa.” Ronnie \nand Michele Wise, Washington County Residents\nProudly endorsed by:\nWashington County Democrats\nOregon Working Families Party\nWashington County Ignite Action Fund\nOregon AFSCME\nTeamsters Joint Council No. 37\nSEIU Oregon\nOregon League of Conservation Voters\nThe Mother PAC\nSmart Growth\nBasic Rights Oregon\nLatino Network Action Fund\nBeaverton Teacher Political Action Committee ( B-PAC)\nFormer Washington County Commissioners: Dick Schouten \nand Greg Malinowski\nFormer Governor: John Kitzhaber\nCongresswoman Maxine Dexter\nOregon Senate Majority Leader Kayse Jama\nFormer Senators: Mark Hass & Chuck Riley\nFormer State Representative Jeff Barker\nState Representatives: Susan McLain, Hai Pham, Mari \nWatanabe\nMetro Council President Juan Carlos Gonzalez\nTHPRD Barbie Minor\nTigard Mayor Yi-Kang Hu\nFormer Hillsboro Mayor Tom Hughes\nCity Councilors: (Beaverton) Nadia Hasan, Edward Kimmi \n(Cornelius) Angeles Gordinez Valencia (Hillsboro) Olivia \nAlcaire, Saba Anvery (Sherwood) Taylor Giles (Tualatin) \nBridget Brooks\nNafisaforwashingtoncounty.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Nafisa Fai\n\nWC-5\nCommissioner, District 4\nWashington CountyWashington County\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSteve Callaway\nOccupation: Hillsboro Mayor, \n(2017-2024)\nOccupational Background: \nHillsboro School District \nPrincipal; Banks School District \nVice Principal; Teacher\nEducational Background: \nPoint Loma College; Lewis & \nClark College\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Hillsboro Mayor; \nCity Council President; League of Oregon Cities President; \nMetro, Washington County and ODOT committees; Jackson \nBottom Wetlands Preserve; Hillsboro Library & Schools \nFoundations; Community Action Organization\nTHE DECISIONS WE MAKE NOW WILL SHAPE WESTERN \nWASHINGTON COUNTY FOR YEARS TO COME.\nWashington County is an incredible place to live, work, and \nraise a family. As we grow, we must protect what makes our \ncommunities special while planning responsibly for the future. \nThat means addressing rising costs, keeping communities safe, \nand using taxpayer dollars wisely.\nThe biggest decisions require the biggest conversations—\nand experienced, steady leadership.\nI’ve spent 35 years serving Western Washington County as a \nteacher, coach, principal, City Councilor, and Mayor.\nAs your County Commissioner, I’ll work to:\n• Make Washington County more affordable.\n• Protect taxpayers through responsible budgeting and \naccountability.\n• Keep communities safe and healthy with solutions to \nhomelessness, addiction, and behavioral health.\n• Protect farmland and natural resources.\n• Carefully evaluate major developments, like data \ncenters, and their impacts on our residents, communities, \nand quality of life.\nTRUSTED EXPERIENCE FOR THE DECISIONS AHEAD\nProudly supported by:\nPublic Safety & Labor: Tualatin Valley Firefighters IAFF 1660; \nHillsboro Professional Firefighters; Hillsboro Police Officers \nAssociation; Northwest Oregon Labor Council\nOrganizations: Independent Party of Oregon; Willamette \nWomen Democrats; Washington County Chamber PAC; Building \nPower for Communities of Color\nElected & Public Leaders: DA Kevin Barton; Commissioner \nJerry Willey; Commissioner-Elect Felicita Monteblanco; Senator \nBruce Starr; Metro President Juan Carlos Gonzalez; Mayors \nMalynda Wenzl and Jeffrey Dalin; Henry Reimann, former \nForest Grove Police Chief; Leslee Sipp, Banks School Board\nCommunity & Business Leaders: Loren Behrman; Cecilia \nGiron, Oregon LULAC; Hector Hinojosa; Terrance and Patti \nBurns, Loch Lolly Christmas Forest; Jaime Miranda, M&M \nMarketplace; Deborah Garman; and hundreds more.\nLeadership starts with listening. I’m ready to turn conversations \ninto action—and I’d be honored to earn your trust and your \nvote. \nElectSteveCallaway.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Steve Callaway\nCommissioner, District 4\nKipperlyn Sinclair\nOccupation: Hillsboro City \nCouncilor, Founder/Director, Eat \nDrink Washington County.\nOccupational Background: \nEight years civic leadership; \nSpecialty food business owner.\nEducational Background: NA\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Hillsboro \nCity Councilor; President, \nHillsboro Transportation Committee; Tri-Met TV Highway \nCommittee; Regional Disaster Preparedness Org; Small \nBusiness Entrepreneurs Council; Hillsboro Public Engagement \nCommittee.\nElect a leader for People.\nLocal government is about people, place, and policy. Home is \nour community’s greatest investment. Washington County faces \n$20.5M deficits; working families shouldn’t bear the burden. The \npolitical pipeline will continue business-as-usual, sacrificing \nprime farmland for corporate data centers and raising utility \nrates. We need leaders for people, not corporations.\nWhen administrators bypassed my council to give millions in \ntax breaks to 17 data centers, I called for a moratorium and \ntook legal action with Oregon Teachers Association and local \nleaders to protect public dollars.\nIn 2023, my opponent lobbied for SB4 corporate \nexpansions, pushing to dismantle public engagement, strip \nfarmland protections, eliminate reversion safeguards, and block \nlegal appeals.\nPriorities:\n• Stop Sprawl & Protect Prime Soil: End data center tax \nbreaks; halt expansion onto farmland. Tax existing facilities \nto reduce deficits, protect the power grid, and lower utility \ncosts. Require corporations to pay their fair share and fund \nschools.\n• Invest in Local Business & Farm Infrastructure: \nBack independent businesses and transit-oriented \ngrowth, opening farm-to-market hubs that boost our $8B \nagricultural sector and secure food supplies.\n• Protect Due Process & Public Safety: My commitment to \nconstitutional rights is absolute. I will uphold due process, \nsafeguard privacy from surveillance, protect public data, \nand deliver transparent public safety.\nVote kipperlynsinclair.com\nEndorsements: Washington County Democrats, Sierra Club, \nFriends of Smart Growth, Oregon Working Families Party, \nSEIU Oregon, For All Families Oregon Action, Oregon Data \nCenter Defiance; Representative Susan McLain; Commissioner \nNafisa Fai; Councilor Olivia Alcaire; Senate Candidate Myrna \nMunoz; Dean Moberg, Tualatin Soil & Water Conservation \nDistrict; Stoneboat Farm; Vanasche Farm; Duyke Family \nFarms; Spiesschaert Farm; La Finquita del Buho CSA; James \nSullivan, Working Theory Farm; Helvetia Gardens; Comida KIN; \nFoodways at Nana Cardoon.\nThis Information Furnished By: Kipperlyn Sinclair\n\nWC-6\nMarsha Kirk\nOccupation: Mayor of \nBanks; School Bus Driver; \nTransportation Administration\nOccupational Background: \nPublic-sector administration \nand records; Code Compliance \nInspector; Small Business \nOwner\nEducational Background: \nPortland Community College; \nAssociate Degree, Criminal Justice/Applied Science\nPrior Governmental Experience: Mayor, 2025–present; \nCouncil, 2019–2024; Planning, 2016–2018; EDC, 2015–2018; \nNWACT & COL-PAC, 2016–present\nFor more than a decade, I have volunteered my time serving the \nBanks community.\nMany things I’ve done happen quietly behind the scenes. \nProjects involving transportation, trails, infrastructure, economic \ndevelopment and partnerships can take years to move forward. \nSome of those efforts are finally becoming visible. I don’t feel I \nneed credit for every idea or accomplishment. What matters is \ndoing what’s best for the Banks community.\nBanks has planned for growth, and now it’s here. With two \ndevelopments moving forward at the same time, our community \nis changing quickly—faster than I would have chosen. Our \nchallenge now is keeping pace while protecting our small-town \nfeel and character.\nWe’ve seen some of that small-town connection fade over \nthe years, but the embers are still here. Let’s fan them. Let’s \nwelcome new families by showing them what being part of \nBanks means: neighbors knowing neighbors, volunteering, \nsupporting local businesses and organizations, and working \ntogether.\nThose connections also make us safer. Strong neighborhoods \nand emergency preparedness make our community more \nresilient and secure.\nMy regional involvement strengthens Banks’ voice beyond City \nHall and helps keep our needs part of county, regional and state \nconversations.\nThings worth accomplishing don’t always happen overnight or fit \nneatly into a two-year term. I want to continue the work already \nunderway.\nI don’t ask Banks residents or businesses for public \nendorsements. Our relationships in our small city are too \nimportant to ask friends and neighbors to publicly choose sides.\nGrowth will change the size of Banks. It doesn’t have to change \nour heart, too.\nI respectfully ask for your vote to continue serving as Mayor.\nMore on experience & endorsements at MarshaKirk.online\nThis Information Furnished By: Marsha Kirk\nMayor Mayor\nCity of BanksCity of Banks\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJimmy Bogroff\nOccupation: Founder, Fearless \nInfluence, a marketing agency; \nVarsity Soccer Coach and JV2 \nBoys Basketball Coach, Banks \nSchool District.\nOccupational Background: \nDirector, Digital Strategy, \nOregonian Media Group.\nEducational Background: \nBanks High School, 1995; \nWillamette University, Bachelor’s degrees in Economics and \nHistory, 1999.\nPrior Governmental Experience: City of Banks Planning \nCommission, 2025–present; Banks School District Long-Range \nPlanning Committee, 2012–2013, 2015–2016, 2021–2025 (co-\nchair).\nHi Neighbors, I’m asking for your vote to become the next \nMayor of Banks.\nBanks is special to me. I was raised here, graduated from \nBanks High School, and I’m raising my family here. \nServing Banks is important to me.  \n• I’ve coached hundreds of Banks kids, \n• Volunteered thousands of hours in the community, \n• Helped plan our new high school, and\n• Served on the Planning Commission, where I read every \npage and ask the hard questions.\nContinuing to serve Banks is important to me. As new \nhousing breaks ground, we have an opportunity to carry \nour town’s legacy and identity forward while welcoming new \nneighbors and businesses the Banks way: friendly, hardworking, \nand involved.\nAs your Mayor, here are six things I’m focused on:\n1. Increase volunteers and fill 100% of our open seats on city \ncommittees\n2. Partner with our City Councilors and City staff, who serve \nus with experience and integrity\n3. Help local businesses grow and keep our dollars in Banks\n4. Plan our future proactively and together: zoning, historic \ninventory, and shared spaces\n5. Make room for respectful disagreement; it brings clarity to \ngood decisions\n6. Bring our broader community outside city limits into the \nconversation\nWe have far more in common than we disagree about. Let’s \nbuild on that.  \nDon’t agree? I’d love to listen. \nEndorsed by\nBanks City Councilors: Peter Edison (former Mayor), Stephanie \nJones (former Mayor), Pearcia Bogroff, Niki Walters, April \nRandall\nFind more city and community endorsements at www.\njimmybogroff.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Jimmy Bogroff\n\nWC-7\nCouncil Member, Position 1 Council Member, Position 1\nCity of BanksCity of Banks\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nStephanie Jones\nOccupation: Director, \nNorthwest Science Expo\nOccupational Background: \nScience Teacher, Hillsboro \nSchool District; Substitute \nteacher, multiple Washington \nCounty districts\nEducational Background: \nPortland State University, MS \nin Teaching Science; Western \nOregon University, BA in Integrated Studies\nPrior Governmental Experience: Banks City Council; Mayor; \nBudget Committee Chair; Public Advisory Board; Public Safety \nCoordinating Committee; MACC; Banks Library Board\nVolunteering:\nFriends of the Banks Library President; Banks High School \nVolunteer\nValues:\nMy husband and I have called Banks home since 1998, moving \nhere after 2 years of marriage. We recently celebrated our 30th \nwedding anniversary, making our roots in this community even \nmore meaningful.\nOur children grew up in the Banks School District, and I have \nenjoyed volunteering and supporting their activities. These \ndays, that means being a proud theater mom, helping with \ncostumes and props.\nIn my spare time, I make quilts for Project Linus.\nExperience:\nServing as a Banks elected official for 10 years, I developed a \nstrong understanding of state, county, and local laws that guide \nour city, and how property tax revenue affects our ability to fund \nessential services. I believe in responsible planning, including \nensuring water resources and infrastructure are available to \nsupport thoughtful, sustainable growth.\nI will encourage growth that strengthens our community, \nsupports local families and businesses, and creates long-term \neconomic opportunities. I am strongly opposed to data centers \nin Banks because I believe we can attract development that \nmakes better use of our resources and provides meaningful \nopportunities. As Banks grows, I will advocate for development \nthat supports residents while preserving the character that \nmakes Banks a great place to call home.\nI have and will continue to lead with integrity, courage, and \nconviction, standing up for what I believe is right. Those values \nwill guide me as I serve the people of Banks.\nEndorsed by:\nDenise Holmes; Peter Edison; April Randall; Niki Walters; Dan \nStreblow; James Bogroff;  Pam Treece; Kathryn Harrington, \nChair, Washington County Commission\nThis Information Furnished By: Stephanie Jones\nStewart Terrall\nOccupation: Project Engineer\nOccupational Background: \nMechanical and Chemical \nEngineer, Construction \nContractor, Industrial Technician\nEducational Background: MS \nand BS Mechanical Engineering, \nPSU; AS Mechanical \nEngineering Technician, PCC\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nVolunteering\nSafe Families, HOA Board Member, Overseas Humanitarian Aid\nI moved to Banks to raise my family in a small town with a \nwholesome environment that values agriculture and shared \ntraditions. Throughout my childhood, I watched the steady \nexpansion of Hillsboro eating up farmland around West Union. \nIf elected, I will bring my long history of volunteering and \nengineering expertise to council at a time when Banks requires \ncareful infrastructure expansion. I will work to restore integrity to \nthe Banks City Council while championing residents’ interests \nand supporting local businesses.\n• Preservation of Banks Traditions\nWhat makes Banks unique must be stewarded or our \ntown identity will be lost. I will protect town traditions like \nSunset Speedway, Banks BBQ, and Christmas events \nand strengthen our stellar services like the fire station and \nlibrary.\n• Responsible City Planning\nWe can’t change plans already in motion, but we can \nscrutinize decisions to ensure any future growth is slow \nand intentional. The expansion of utilities needs to be \ncarefully considered so that existing residents receive \nadequate service while keeping costs low. Land planning \nshould support our local needs, not bring in outside \ncorporations and faceless franchises.\n• Term Limits\nBanks residents have been sidelined by long-ruling \ncouncilors for too long. Enacting term limits will encourage \ntown engagement and ensure fresh perspective is always \nat the table.\n• City Accountability\nCity officials are paid by our taxes, yet they behave with a \nlevel of entitlement inappropriate for government service. \nThe city needs to learn how to spend within their budget \nwithout increasing the tax burden on working families and \nretired, fixed-income households.\nMy technical acumen will be an asset to represent working \nfamilies, preserve Banks’ identity, and ensure careful planning \noccurs for all. No campaign manager, no political ambitions, just \na dad who cares about our future.\nThis Information Furnished By: Stewart Terrall\n\nWC-8\nAM I REGISTERED\nTO VOTE? \nNow you can check \nyour registration online:\nwww.oregonvotes.gov\nFor more information \nabout voting in Oregon\nCALL\n1-866-ORE-VOTE/673-8683\nVISIT\nwww.oregonvotes.gov\nTTY\n1-800-735-2900\nfor the hearing impaired\nCouncil Member, Position 5\nCity of BanksCity of Banks\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nPearcia Bogroff\nOccupation: Chief Operating \nOfficer, Fearless Influence; \nRealRoots Guide\nOccupational Background: \nRegional Director, Premier \nDesigns Jewelry; Executive \nPastor, Resound Church\nEducational Background: \nGolden Gate Seminary, MA \nEducational Leadership; Western Oregon University, BA Special \nEducation/Deaf Studies; Vernonia High School\nPrior Governmental Experience: City Councilor; Urban \nRenewal Agency; Budget Committee\nI believe the heart of Banks is its people.\nOur small-town feel does not come from having a small \npopulation. It comes from neighbors, business owners, workers, \nstudents and families who care about where they live and invest \nin their community.\nBanks is growing. While we may not all agree about how we \narrived here or how quickly we are changing, that growth is \nour reality. I believe we can meet it with optimism and purpose \nrather than allowing it to divide us.\nI want new residents to feel welcomed into the culture that \nmakes Banks special and to develop a sense of ownership in \nour community. I want longtime residents to feel their values are \nbeing respected. I want all of us to be contributors, not simply \nconsumers, while supporting local businesses and creating new \nopportunities for them to thrive.\nI do not believe good local government is about choosing \nsides. It is about listening carefully, seeking facts, asking \ndifficult questions and making decisions based on what is \nbest for our community. As a councilor, I work to represent our \nentire community, including people whose opinions differ from \nmy own. On the Urban Renewal Agency, I helped navigate \ndisagreements and find common ground. I have also worked \ntowards practical improvements like year-round bathrooms at \nGreenville Park now being included in our ten-year plan.\nI believe we can preserve what is special about Banks while \nbuilding positively for the future. New families, new ideas and \ngreater diversity can enrich our community without changing the \nheart of who we are.\nI want to help cultivate a Banks that people are proud to call \nhome, proud to contribute to, and proud to pass on.\nThis Information Furnished By: Pearcia Bogroff\n\nWC-9\nCouncil Member, Position 1 Council Member, Position 1\nCity of BeavertonCity of Beaverton\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nEvelyn Kocher\nOccupation: Communications \nManager, Latino Network\nOccupational Background: \nExecutive Director, Oregon \nStudent Association; teacher; \nreferee\nEducational Background: \nH.B.A., OSU\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Member, Higher \nEducation Coordinating \nCommission, City of Beaverton \nTransportation System Plan Community Advisory Committee\nImmigrant | Trans Woman | Lifelong Beavertonian\nI am focused on building a Beaverton that is safe and affordable \nfor everyone. As the only renter on Council, I’ll bring \nvaluable perspective to our majority-renter city and streamline \nprocesses to build mixed-use, multi-family development, \nlowering costs and promoting our city’s growth. I want to \nbuild our MAX stations so everyone can live in walkable \nneighborhoods.  \n \nI will make sure that ALL new tax proposals that City Council \nbrings forward are voted on by the people, not passed in \nbackrooms, and work to make city government transparent and \naccountable.\nI will make sure we are ALL protected by our city’s government \nby protecting Beavertonians from ICE and getting data-\nharvesting Flock cameras out of our city.\nI will expand infrastructure citywide with bus lines in South \nCooper Mountain so everyone in Beaverton can contribute to \nthe economic growth of the city.\nI want to give back to the town that raised me and support its \nfuture.\nWill you join me?\nProudly Endorsed By: \nWorking Families Party \nUFCW 555 \nSEIU Oregon\nPCC Faculty Federation and Academic Professionals\nOregon League of Conservation Voters \nWashington County Democrats\nWashington County Ignite\nOregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\nBasic Rights Oregon PAC\nPineros y Campesinos Unidos del Noroeste \nSierra Club\nAPANO Action Fund\nFarrah Chaichi, State Representative \nLamar Wise,State Representative \nState Senator Khanh Pham\nPortland City Councilor Angelita Morillo\nBeaverton School Board member Dr. Tammy Carpenter\nTHPRD board member Barbie Minor \nNWESD Zone 3 Director Dorian Russell\nwww.evelynforbeaverton.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Evelyn for Beaverton\nRachel Philip\nOccupation: Software \nDeveloper\nOccupational Background: \nSoftware Developer; Auditor\nEducational Background: \nMasters of Science, Portland \nState University; Bachelors \nof Business Administration, \nUniversity of Portland\nPrior Government Experience: \nBeaverton Budget Committee; \nTransportation Ambassador; \nDowntown Loop Community Committee; Visioning Committee; \nCentral Beaverton Neighborhood Association Chair\nEXPERIENCED. PROVEN. READY.\nBeaverton is home. It is where my husband and I are raising our \ntwo children. For years, I’ve done the work: reviewing the city \nbudget, shaping transportation and neighborhood plans, and \norganizing neighbors. As a daughter of immigrants, software \ndeveloper, and former auditor, I listen carefully, follow the \ndetails, and work with people to get things done.\nMY PRIORITIES FOR BEAVERTON\nAffordability: Expand housing choices, including apartments, \ntownhomes, and starter homes, so families, seniors, and \nworking people can stay in Beaverton.\nSafe streets: My son was hit by a car while biking home from \nschool. It’s personal for me. I will prioritize safer routes to \nschools and calming dangerous streets.\nFiscal responsibility: Protect essential services, set clear \npriorities, and make budget decisions transparent.\nA greener, more resilient city: Protect trees and green \nspaces and prepare for extreme heat, wildfire smoke, and other \nemergencies.\nProtect Beaverton: Defend our city from federal overreach and \nensure our local resources are used to protect our neighbors \nand services.\nTHE LEADERSHIP WE NEED\n“Rachel is exactly the kind of leader Beaverton needs: \nexperienced, compassionate, and ready to serve. She isn’t \nlearning on the job, she’s leading from day one.” — Mayor \nLacey Beaty\nFEATURED ENDORSEMENTS:\nSecretary of State Tobias Read\nBOLI Commissioner Christina Stephenson\nMayor Lacey Beaty\nCouncilors Hartmeier-Prigg, Teater, Tivnon, Dugger\nState Senators Lieber, Reynolds\nState Representatives Bowman, Helm, Pham, Walters\nTHPRD Directors Moreno, Patil\nBSD School Director: Dr. Pérez, Truong, Potter, Garg\nCounty Commissioners Harrington, Treece, Snider\nMetro Councilors Lewis, Palacios\n \nWashington County Democrats \nDemocratic Municipal Officials \nIndependent Party of Oregon\nOregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\nRun For Something\n314 Action Fund \nTualatin Valley Firefighters IAFF Local 1660 \nNorthwest Oregon Labor Council \nUFCW555\nBeaverton Chamber of Commerce\nSee more: rachelforbeaverton.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Rachel Philip\n\nWC-10\nMayor Council Member\nCity of CorneliusCity of Cornelius\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJeffrey Dalin\nOccupation: Engineering \nConsultant \nOccupational Background: 32 \nyears of experience in project \nmanagement & engineering \nEducational Background: \nBachelor of Science in \nManufacturing Engineering \nTechnology from OIT \nPrior Governmental Experience: Nov. 2011 to present: Mayor, \nCity of Cornelius; July 2003 to Nov. 2011: City Councilor \nWhy should I continue to be Mayor? \nBecause when you speak, I listen. Then I take the time to figure \nout, with the council, how we get from where we are to where \nyou want to be. This needs to be done in a planned manner. I \ndon’t believe in causing a storm or tearing our community apart \nto make change. As Mayor, my job is to listen to your concerns, \nwants, and desires and determine the best way possible to \nturn them into city policies. I also represent you in meetings \nwith other cities, the county, Metro, the state and our federal \nrepresentatives.\nMy family and I have shared this community with you since \n1996. Our community comes from a diverse and varied \nbackground. I focus on what we have in common. We love our \nfamilies. We want our kids to grow up safe and be contributing \nmembers of our community. Everyone should feel welcome in \nthe community.\nIssues you have told me that we need to continue to work on \nare: \n• Improve safety along Hwy 8 / TV Hwy \n• Plan growth of our community, including more family wage \njobs \n• Improve the city’s financial stability \n• Increase government efficiency \n• Increase the number of community building activities \nI will continue to reach out to you through the monthly \nnewsletter; English & Spanish speaking Town Hall meetings; \nand public appearances for community events. My phone and \nemail continue to be open to you as they have since taking \noffice in 2003. \nThank you for the opportunity to continue to serve our \ncommunity.\nThis Information Furnished By: Jeffrey Dalin\nEdgar Baker\nOccupation: Warehouse \nAssociate\nOccupational Background: \nLegislative Intern, Seasonal \nField Worker\nEducational Background: \nPortland State University, \nPortland Community College, \nForest Grove High School\nPrior Governmental Experience: Cornelius City Council, \nBudget Committee, Planning Commission, Cornelius 2045 \nVision Committee\nAs a lifelong resident of Cornelius, I have seen the city change. \nI have been involved in Cub Scouts and learned about civic \nengagement, from serving on the Cornelius 2045 Vision \nCommittee to hearing what our community wants to see in the \nfuture—and now serving on the city council.\nI plan to serve in this position by listening to and respecting \nmembers of our community, advocating for resources to \nimprove our city at different levels of government, and keeping \nmembers of our community informed about what’s going on in \nthe city.\nI don’t want members to feel left out, ignored, and \ndisrespected in the governing process. Governing works best \nby finding solutions with our community and not against it.\nWhat I want to achieve...\n• Improving our downtown. \n• Continuing and Improving Community Events.\n• Lobbying for state funding for the future Community Center\n• Achieving Items on The Cornelius 2045 Plan.\nLet’s improve our community! Thank you.\nThis Information Furnished By: Edgar Baker\n\nWC-11\nMayor Council Member\nCity of Forest GroveCity of Forest Grove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nMalynda Wenzl\nOccupation: Teacher \nOccupational Background: 24 \nyears as a Forest Grove School \nDistrict Educator \nEducation Background: \nSouthern Oregon University, BS \nand Master’s in Art of Teaching \nPrior Governmental Experience: Mayor, 2022–present; City \nCouncil President, 2020–2022; City Council, 2014–2022\nFamily: Married 18 years; mother to two amazing humans, and \nTutu to a wonderful granddaughter. \nIt is an honor serving Forest Grove. As a lifelong community \nmember, I’m grateful for your trust. The City’s achievements \nreflect strong community engagement, collaboration with \npartners — governmental, service, and volunteer organizations \n— and an exceptionally skilled city staff.\nTogether we have achieved:\n• Forest Grove Forward 2040 Vision Plan: This strategic \nplan sets the framework for future goals, objectives, \nand budgets, ensuring Forest Grove grows according to \ncommunity values.\n• Transportation: We have completed or secured funding \nfor  projects that bring safer streets, lower speeds, and \nbetter walking and biking infrastructure to neighborhoods \nand schools across the community.\n• Recreation: Increased park space (Stites Park and a new \nEastside Park) and expanded recreation (Camp Grove and \npickleball).\n• Immigration: The council has acted to stay responsive to \nfederal and state policy shifts, guided by our oath to the \nConstitution and Forest Grove residents.\nI will work to:\n• Complete the Comprehensive Plan update, using key \nprinciples of smart growth.\n• Complete and implement an updated Economic Strategic \nPlan.\n• Continue Downtown revitalization through Urban Renewal \nand placemaking.\n• Complete transportation projects that increase safety and \nsupport multimodal travel.\n• Remain responsive to changes in Federal and State policy.\n• Continue helping  our city build and strengthen \nrelationships.\nForest Grove’s progress comes from our community working \ntogether — and I’m committed to keeping that spirit at the \ncenter of our City’s future. I would be honored to continue \nserving as your Mayor.\nThis Information Furnished By: Malynda Wenzl\nMatthew S. Knight\nOccupation: Business Owner, \nKnight LLC.\nOccupational Background: \nSmall Business Owner; Capital \nEquipment Sales, 2009-2021\nEducational Background: \nPioneer Pacific College, \nCertificate in Corrections; \nPortland Community College coursework; High School Diploma\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nForest Grove is home. I grew up here, graduated from Forest \nGrove High School, and today my wife, Valentina, and I are \nproud to call Forest Grove home. I have watched our community \ngrow and change over the years, and I am running for City \nCouncil because I care deeply about where we go next.\nAs a local business owner, I understand what it means to live \nwithin a budget, make difficult decisions, plan for the future, and \nbe accountable for the results. I believe our city should operate \nwith that same responsibility. We must live within our means, \nestablish clear priorities, and use taxpayer dollars wisely while \nproviding the essential services our community depends on.\nI want Forest Grove to be a community where families can \nbuild their futures, existing businesses can succeed, and new \nbusinesses want to invest. We can encourage responsible \ngrowth while protecting the character and quality of life that \nmakes Forest Grove special.\nGood decisions also require understanding both our local \nneeds and the needs of the broader community. Transportation, \nhousing, infrastructure, economic development, and public \nsafety don’t stop at city boundaries. We need to work effectively \nwith our neighbors and regional partners while making sure \nForest Grove’s voice and interests are represented.\nMost importantly, I believe in a collaborative approach to \nproblem-solving. None of us has all the answers. We may not \nalways agree, but we can listen to one another, respect different \nperspectives, find common ground, and make thoughtful \ndecisions.\nForest Grove helped shape who I am. I want to give back to \nthe community my wife and I call home and help ensure Forest \nGrove remains a place we are proud to pass on to the next \ngeneration.\nStronger Together.\nThis Information Furnished By: Matthew S Knight\n\nWC-12\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of Forest GroveCity of Forest Grove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSandra Pereyda\nOccupation: Educator and \nwaitress as a local Forest Grove \nRestaurant.\nOccupational Background: \nPharmacy Technician, \nEducation and Hospitality.\nEducational Background: \nHigh School Diploma\nPrior Governmental Experience: N/A\nI believe that as an elected official, I work to serve every single \nperson in the community and that their voice has a place in \nevery part of the process. The community deserves the right \nto a safe, welcoming, and healthy Forest Grove for all. Si Se \nPuede! -Sandra Pereyda \nAs an activist, Sandra has been fighting for our rights to clean \nair, clean water, and a community that puts the quality of life \nof its people above corporate interests. To Sandra, this means \nprotecting our land from data centers, holding corporations \naccountable, and taxing corporations accordingly. Sandra has \nwitnessed firsthand how tax exemptions for large corporations \naffect the quality of education that students receive. She \nbelieves our resources should be invested in the people who \nwork, live, and raise their families here. Sandra will continue \nto protect the most vulnerable members of our community- \nchildren, working families, and everyone else in between.\nSandra has also been fighting to ensure that every single \nperson has access to their constitutional rights, regardless of \nstatus. She believes our rights should not be infringed upon, \nand that includes the right to privacy, due process, and free \nspeech. One thing Sandra wants to work on is addressing \nfederal overreach and mass surveillance through Flock cameras \nand other ALPR systems. Her worry is the lack of federal and \nstate regulations on mass surveillance. \nSandra also wants to thank her parents, who came here in \nsearch of a better life. Her work ethic comes from watching her \nfamily work extremely hard to achieve their goals, and wants \nto make sure everyone has the opportunity to achieve their \nown goals. Every single person has the right to the pursuit \nof happiness. Sandra is only here to make those goals more \nachievable to families like her own. \nThis Information Furnished By: Sandra Pereyda\nMariana E.  \nValenzuela\nOccupation: Assistant \nProfessor at Pacific University; \nGrandmother of Isla, Río, and \nMarco, and mother of Cristina \nand Pablo.\nOccupational Background: \nCentro Cultural of Washington \nCounty; Portland Community \nCollege; Clark College;  Idaho \nState University; New Mexico \nState University\nEducational Background: Pacific University, MBA; New \nMexico State University, BA & MA.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Forest Grove City Council, \n2020-present\nIt has been an honor and a privilege to serve our generous \ncommunity as a city councilor since 2020.  In that time our city \nhas weathered serious challenges to public health and safety, \nhousing and economic development. Through the tremendous \ndedication of our great city staff, the leadership of our mayor \nand council, and the involvement of many of our residents we \nhave come through it.  I believe Forest Grove is well positioned \nto continue to grow and thrive in the coming years. \n  \nWe still have work to do:\n- encouraging the further development of new business and \nvitality in our downtown;\n- ensuring the well-being and security of all our residents.\n- maintaining the high quality of city services delivered by our \nsuperb municipal employees; and\n- presenting Forest Grove as an attractive place to live, work, \nstudy and grow.\nThere is still much I can contribute to the council and the city. \nI am seeking a full term to have the opportunity to continue \nadvocating on behalf of our youth, our elderly, and the \nhardworking families of our city, and to broadenthe range of \nvoices and perspectives represented there.  I respectfullyask\nfor your vote and your support. \nEndorsed by: \nMalynda Wenzl, Mayor, City of Forest Grove\nBrian Schimmel, City Councilor, City of Forest Grove\nJuan Carlos González, Metro Council President, Metro Regional \nGovernment\nNarce Rodríguez, Board Member, Centro Cultural of Washington \nCounty\nThis Information Furnished By: Mariana E. Valenzuela\n\nWC-13\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of Forest GroveCity of Forest Grove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nKaren Martinez\nOccupation: Registered Nurse\nOccupational Background: \nComputer Operator, Stay-at-\nhome Mom, Certified Nursing \nAssistant, Registered Nurse\nEducational Background: \nWashington High School, Tacoma, \nWA; PCC; University of Portland \nSchool of Nursing Bachelor\nPrior Government Experience: FG City Council 2023-Present\nCareer Accomplishments: KP Unit Based Team Co-Lead \n2019-2021; Kaiser Permanente Extraordinary Nursing Care \nAward 2020\nVolunteer Opportunities: Salem Free Clinic 2019-2021; Hear \nthe Cry-MamaBaby Haiti Midwife Clinic 2019\nForest Grove has been my home for over 33 years. I have \nactively participated in my council role the last 3.5 yrs. I speak \nmy mind even when my views are unpopular with fellow \ncouncilors. Varying beliefs should be brought to the table and \ndiscussed. I believe in honesty, integrity and commitment to \nserving the people of Forest Grove.\nSolutions for Homeless Issues: Participated in petitioning \nWashington County for closure of B St & Hwy 47 camps. Crime \nnumbers in 2024 dropped significantly. Closing the camps \nbrought greater safety to Forest Grove.\nPark & Rec: Supported NO field usage fees for youth sports.\nIndividual Liberty: The First Amendment of our constitution \ngives US citizens the right to have their voices heard without \nretribution. I listen and interact during discussion with fellow \ncouncilors and constituency. Whether paid or volunteer, public \nofficials are accountable to those they represent.\nPublic Safety:  Protect police and fire department funding. \nPolice officers and firefighters deserve our support for putting \ntheir lives on the line to protect our community. I’m currently \nserving as liaison on the Rural Fire Board observing firsthand \nthe work our personnel are doing to improve response times. I \nsupport the bond for the new police station. A few of the many \nissues: the current station needs major repair, too small for the \nnumber of employees and doesn’t provide a secure and safe \nplace for victim interviews.\nFiscal responsibility: Account for every expense. Taxpayer \nmoney should benefit the public. Continue transparency and \naccountability.\nThis Information Furnished By: Karen Martinez\nMallory Hiefield\nOccupation: Assistant Athletic \nDirector\nOccupational Background: \nIntramurals and Recreation, \nFitness Instructor, Healthcare \nAdministration\nEducational Background: \nPacific University, Master of \nBusiness Administration\nPacific University, BS in Biology, \nMinor in Spanish and Outdoor Leadership\nPrior Governmental Experience: City of Forest Grove Budget \nCommittee Member\nCity of Forest Grove Parks and Recreation Commission Member\nI am running for Forest Grove City Council because I care \ndeeply about our community and want to help shape its future \nfor long term success. Born in Forest Grove (back when \nthere was a hospital), raised in Portland, and now back in \nForest Grove, I take pride in our community. Forest Grove is \nchanging. The decisions we make today will have a lasting \nimpact on generations to come. We need leadership that \nbalances housing, infrastructure, economic opportunity, and \nenvironmental sustainability while preserving the character and \nsense of community that make Forest Grove special. My top 3 \npriorities are:\nEnvironmental Sustainability\nAs our community grows we have a responsibility to protect \nour natural resources and preserve agricultural lands that help \ndefine our community. Sustainability and growth do not have to \nbe competing goals.\nActual Affordable Housing\nWe need a range of housing options so that young people \nstarting their careers, families, seniors and the people who work \nin our community can afford to live here.\nEconomic Development\nA strong local economy creates opportunities for residents to \nwork and live in the same city. I would work hard to support our \nlocal businesses and invest in infrastructure that promotes job \ngrowth and benefits the entire community.\nI would be honored to earn your vote and serve on the Council. \nHaving served on the Forest Grove Budget Committee and \nParks and Recreation Commission, along with my MBA degree, \nI bring a thoughtful, collaborative and practical approach to my \nleadership. Thank you for your consideration.\nEndorsements:\nWashington County Democrats\nOregon State Senator nominee, Myrna Munoz\nForest Grove Mayor, Malynda Wenzl\nForest Grove City Councilors: Mike Marshall, Brian Schimmel, \nAngel Falconer\nvotemalloryhiefield.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Mallory Hiefield\n\nWC-14\nContact Washington County Elections \nat (503) 846-5800 or \nelections@washingtoncountyor.gov \nto request a replacement ballot if:\nYou do not have to vote on all contests. \nThose you do vote on will still count. \nCheck for errors\nCheck your ballot carefully\nYou can not change your vote after \nyou have returned your ballot. \nIf you vote for more than one option, \nyour vote will not count for that \ncandidate or measure. \nComplete your ballot\nTo vote, completely \nﬁll in the oval next to \nyour choice. \nCarefully read and follow all \ninstructions printed on your ballot. \n \nTo write-in a candidate:\n Clearly print their \n n ame on the blank \n l ine provided on \n the b allot \n-and-\nMargaret\nSeymour\n1\nE d na\n2\n Fill in the oval next \n to the name you \n wrote-in\nvote for one\nYes\nNo\n!\n you make a mistake\n you lose your ballot\n your ballot is damaged or spoiled\n or for any other reason.\nMayor\nCity of GastonCity of Gaston\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nWilliam Martin\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nTruck driver\nEducational Background: \nForest Grove High\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Mayor\nCity Council\nDear Friends and Neighbors,\nIt has been an incredible honor to serve the City of Gaston over \nthe past 3 years as Mayor. I am asking for your support as I \nseek re-election, so I can continue working to keep Gaston a \nsafe, welcoming, and thriving community for everyone. I have \nbeen on city council since 2017. I was a council member for \n4 years and then became council president. I am a retired \nwidower and find joy in volunteering for the city.\nMy commitment to Gaston comes from more than public \nservice-it comes from calling this town home for the past 25 \nyears. During that time, I have watched our community grow \nwhile maintaining the small-town values that make Gaston such \na special place to live.\nWhile we’ve accomplished a great deal there is still important \nwork ahead. My commitment is to continue supporting \nresponsible growth, investing in public safety and infrastructure, \nand preserving the character that makes Gaston such a \nwonderful place to live and raise a family. I volunteer 20-30 \nhours a week for the city. I help mow the park and run the work \ncrew when they come out. I also handle many of the issues that \narise during the week. I love helping our small community. \nWhen I am not volunteering, I like to fish and play golf. I would \nbe grateful for your vote and the opportunity to continue serving \nas your mayor. Together, we can keep Gaston moving forward.\nThank you for your trust, your support, and your commitment to \nour community.\nMayor Martin\nThis Information Furnished By: Wiliam Martin\n\nWC-15\nCouncil Member, Ward 1, Position A Council Member, Ward 1, Position A\nCity of HillsboroCity of Hillsboro\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nCristian Salgado\nOccupation: Small Business \nOwner / Consultant\nOccupational Background: \nLocal Government; Technology \nand Infrastructure; Nonprofit \nand Corporate Leadership\nEducational Background: \nAssociate Transfer Degree, \nPolitical Science emphasis, \nLane Community College; \nBachelor of Science, \nPolitical Science, University of Oregon; Master of Business \nAdministration (MBA) for Professionals, Executive Leadership \nconcentration, Willamette University, Atkinson Graduate School \nof Management.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Hillsboro City Councilor, \nappointed.\nPublic service should be practical, accountable, and rooted in \nthe people we serve.\nHillsboro is growing. That growth brings opportunity, but it \nalso raises real questions about affordability, infrastructure, \nneighborhoods, and whether residents share in the benefits.\nAs a small business owner and a father raising my family in \nHillsboro, these questions are personal to me. I want our city \nto remain a place where families can afford to build a life, small \nbusinesses can succeed, neighborhoods are supported, and \ngrowth creates opportunity for more people.\nDuring my time on Council, I have brought a community-\ncentered perspective to these decisions. I have pushed for \nthoughtful data center growth, stronger consideration of \nneighborhood and infrastructure impacts, utility affordability, \nsmall business support, public transparency, and practical \nsupport for residents facing difficult circumstances.\nMy priorities are clear:\nResponsible Growth. Welcome investment while ensuring \ngrowth strengthens neighborhoods, protects critical \ninfrastructure, and creates measurable community benefit.\nStrong Neighborhoods. Invest in safe streets, sidewalks, \ncrossings, parks, reliable services, and easier access to local \ngovernment.\nOpportunity That Reaches People. Connect Hillsboro’s \nprosperity to local jobs, workforce training, small businesses, \nand opportunity for families.\nAs a child, I immigrated to the United States from Mexico with \nmy family. That experience shaped my view of the importance \nof public service and the privilege it is to serve; systems should \nwork for people, opportunity should be accessible, and every \nresident should feel heard.\nI don’t believe public service means having every answer. It \nmeans listening carefully, asking hard questions, doing the \nwork, and being willing to act.\nI’m asking for your vote so I can continue serving Hillsboro with \npractical, people-centered leadership.\nThis Information Furnished By: Cristian Salgado for Hillsboro\nLuis Garcia\nOccupation: Health Information \nSystems Designer, Kaiser \nPermanente \nOccupational Background: 30 \nyears in Computer Science and \nTechnology; Process Design, \nCisco Systems  \n \nEducational Background: \nCal State East Bay, Computer \nScience \nPrior Governmental Experience: City of Hillsboro Police \nChief’s Advisory Committee; Government Systems Data \nManagement, Cisco Systems \nCommunity: Habitat for Humanity West Tuality, Volunteer \nHomebuilder; Your Food Oasis Hillsboro, Food Pantry Volunteer; \n20 years coaching youth soccer  \nHillsboro - For the People - For the Future\nHillsboro is full of potential, and our future depends on how well \nwe prepare for the challenges ahead. \nHaving spent my life working at the forefront of technology and \nlarge systems, I recognize the changes that are coming, and \ndeeply believe Hillsboro can and should lead the way so that our \npeople can prosper. \nTogether, we can fix what’s broken, improve what works, and \ninvest wisely in the future.\nMy focus is simple: \n• STENGTHEN the systems that support our families\n• PROTECT our natural resources\n• ENSURE Hillsboro grows responsibly.\nI will lead with STEWARDSHIP AND ACCOUNTABILITY to \nprioritize:  \n• The basics: good roads, clean water and affordable \nhousing \n• Our way of life: protect farmland from the impacts of \nsemiconductors and data centers\n• Informed decisions: based on science and data, not \nassumptions and fear  \nHillsboro deserves thoughtful leadership that focuses on \npractical solutions, respects taxpayer dollars, plans for the next \ngeneration and loves our people. \n \nFor Hillsboro! \n-  LUIS\nENDORSEMENTS \nHillsboro Mayor Beach Pace \nSenator Janeen Sollman (D-Hillsboro)\nHillsboro Police Officers Association \nWashington County Chamber of Commerce \nJensen Yip, Owner 808 Grinds \nMark Hemenway, Coyote’s Bar and Grill\nPatrick Ribellia, Former Hillsboro Planning Director  \nThis Information Furnished By: Luis Garcia\n\nWC-16\nCouncil Member, Ward 1, Position A Council Member, Ward 1, Position A\nCity of HillsboroCity of Hillsboro\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSarah Marugg\nOccupation: Revenue & Tax \nSpecialist\nOccupational Background: \nMunicipal Tax Compliance; \nInformation Systems Support; \nSupply Chain Management; \nProgram Coordination and \nAdministration.\nEducational Background: \nUniversity of Washington, B.A.; \nMarylhurst University, M.B.A.\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nwww.maruggforhillsboro.com\nAffordability & Housing\nAffordability is more than the price of housing, it includes food \nand the Hillutility bills families pay every month. I support \nresponsible housing growth, family-wage jobs, and practical \nsolutions for people who are precariously housed. Affordability, \nsafety, and quality of life are the foundation of a strong \ncommunity. As your Councilor, I will listen to residents, promote \ntransparency in City Council decisions, and make choices \nthat reflect the needs of our community while protecting our \nenvironment.\nEnvironment, Farmland & Public Services\nHillsboro’s growth should strengthen our community without \nsacrificing clean air, clean water, farmland, wetlands, or the \npublic services families depend on. I will protect farmland, hold \nmajor polluters accountable, and make sure data centers pay \ntheir fair share for the infrastructure and services they use. \nResponsible growth means protecting our environment while \ninvesting in safe neighborhoods, reliable public services, and a \nhealthy future for the next generation. I will also work to protect \nour community from the air and noise pollution associated with \nluxury jet traffic.\nInvesting in Hillsboro & Protecting Civil Rights\nI will invest in the people who make Hillsboro strong - our \nfamilies, workers, students, seniors, and local businesses. That \nmeans supporting teachers & education, workforce training, \nlanguage access, disability and neurodiversity inclusion, \nLGBTQIA+ rights, and equal access to opportunity. I will defend \ncivil rights, uphold Hillsboro’s sanctuary city values, and make \nsure every resident has a voice in local government.\nSafe Streets Connect Community\nI will support safe sidewalks, especially around schools, \nsports fields, and parks, while working toward safer streets for \nchildren, pedestrians, and cyclists. I will also advocate for e-bike \nsafety plus transportation improvements that make our streets, \nbus stops, and MAX platforms safer for everyone.\nEndorsements:\nHillsboro Professional Firefighters\nHillsboro Police\nThis Information Furnished By: Sarah Marugg\nDiana Jackson\nOccupation: Legal Assistant, \nMetropolitan Public Defender’s \nOffice\nOccupational Background: \nParalegal, Rall & Ortiz; Driver, \nGrubhub; Cleaner, Handy \nSandi’s\nEducational Background: \nPortland Community College, \nA.A. Paralegal Studies\nPrior Government Experience: Hillsboro Planning Commission\nA neighbor. A mom. A fighter for Hillsboro.\nDiana is a nine-year resident of Hillsboro, working downtown for \na nonprofit public defense law firm, a proud member of Oregon \nAFSCME Local 3668, and she currently serves on the Hillsboro \nPlanning Commission.\nFirst and foremost, Diana is a mother. It is the best and most \nimportant job she has, and her kids are the inspiration behind \neverything she does.\nDiana knows what it means to work hard, but she also knows \nwhat it means to struggle to afford the roof over my family’s \nhead.\nIn 2018, Diana’s rent was $1,300 a month. By this year, that \nexact same apartment cost $2,400 a month. Today, rent eats up \n76% of her take-home pay.\nDiana is running to make Hillsboro more affordable.\nDiana’s Priorities:\n• We deserve to afford to live where we work. Housing is \na human right, not a privilege\n• A diversified economy is a strong, healthy economy.  \nWe need to grow small businesses, not give tax breaks \nto datacenters \n• Fix the areas where people fall through the cracks \nwhen accessing life saving services\n• ICE out of Hillsboro\nDiana is proudly endorsed by:\nHIllsboro Seeds of Change - Karim Delgado and Dorian Russell\nAFSCME Local 15\nOregon Working Families Party\nWashington County Democrats\nFriends of Smart Growth\nSunrisePDX\nOregon Datacenter Defiance\nRepresentative Susan McLain\nRepresentative Farrah Chaichi\nRepresentative Lesly Munoz\nCounty Commissioner Nafisa Fai\nHillsboro City Councilor Kipperlyn Sinclair\nHillsboro City Councilor Olivia Alcaire\nFormer Hillsboro City Councilor Anthony Martin\nBeaverton City Councilor Nadia Hasan\nSyed Qasim, Beaverton School Board\nBarbie Minor, Tualatin Hills Parks and Recreation District\nMyrna Munoz, Candidate for Oregon SD 15\nTammy Carpenter, Candidate for Oregon HD 27\nEvelyn Kocher, Candidate for Beaverton City Council\nLearn More At\nwww.dianaforhillsboro.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Diana Jackson\n\nWC-17\nCouncil Member, Ward 2, Position A Council Member, Ward 2, Position A\nCity of HillsboroCity of Hillsboro\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nIvette Pantoja\nOccupation: Preschool Owner, \nBriar Rose School\nOccupational Background: \nEarly Childhood Education\nEducational Background: \nGlencoe High School, Portland \nCommunity College\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Hillsboro School \nDistrict Board (Chair 2024-\n2026); Child Care Resources \n& Referral Advisory Board of \nWashington County\nBorn and Raised in Hillsboro\nIvette is the proud daughter of Mexican immigrants and the \ngranddaughter of migrant farmworkers. Her lifelong roots in \nHillsboro as an employer, teacher, and neighbor run deep. \nProven Leadership in Hillsboro\n• As Hillsboro’s youngest School Board Chair, Ivette guided \nour school district to its highest graduation rate in history\n• When Trump’s ICE agents descended on Hillsboro, Ivette \nled HSD’s response to protect students and families\n• As a bilingual preschool owner, Ivette partnered with local \ngovernment during COVID to provide emergency childcare \nfor first responders \nPriorities in Hillsboro\n• Affordability and Prosperity: good jobs and good \nemployers make working class families and small \nbusinesses thrive \n• Housing: affordable homes are the backbone of a strong \ncommunity \n• Public Safety: everyone deserves to feel safe, free and \nlive with dignity \n“The entire Hillsboro School Board endorses Ivette Pantoja \nfor City Council because we’ve worked with her and trust her \nleadership.”  \n- HILLSBORO SCHOOL DISTRICT BOARD CHAIR KATIE \nRHYNE\n“Ivette is the thoughtful, experienced leader Hillsboro needs \nright now.” \nHILLSBORO COUNCILORS ROB HARRIS, SABA ANVERY & \nELIZABETH CASE\nENDORSEMENTS \nCity of Hillsboro\nBeach Pace, Mayor\nRob Harris, Council President\nSaba Anvery, Councilor\nElizabeth Case, Councilor\n \nHillsboro School District Board\nKatie Ryne, Chair\nMark Watson, Vice-Chair\nNancy Thomas\nSee Eun Kim\nYessica Hardin-Mercado\nPatrick Maguire\nWashington County\nPam Treece, Commissioner \nFelicita Monteblanco, Commissioner-elect\nJerry Willey, Commissioner \nKristine Adams-Wannberg, Auditor \nGina Roletto, PCC Board Member\nCommunity  Leaders \nSteve Callaway, former Hillsboro Mayor\nMike and Laura McMurray, CEO & CFO Hillsboro Hops\nDemocratic Oregon Legislators\nRepresentative Hai Pham \nSenator Janeen Sollman\nSenator Lisa Reynolds \nOrganizations\nHillsboro Professional Firefighters \nDemocratic Municipal Officials\nHillsboro Police Officers Association \nWashington County Chamber PAC\nHillsboro Classified United 4671\nwww.ivettepantoja.com \nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Ivette Pantoja\nKarim Delgado\nOccupation: Owner, 649 Bar \n \nOccupational Background: \ncombat videographer; \nschoolteacher; public relations \n \nEducational Background: \nColumbia University, B.A., \nPhilosophy \n \nPrior Governmental \nExperience: U.S. Marine Corps, \nenlisted \n \nCommunity Involvement: Global Refuge, Resettlement Host; \nLiberation Free Fridge, Founder; 649 Free Soup Kitchen; Migra \nWatch; Washington County Democrats Latino Caucus, Co-Chair\nHillsboro’s growth should serve the people who live here, not \njust the corporations that profit here. I’m a Marine veteran who \nhas spent years doing the work the government left undone: \nfeeding unhoused neighbors, opening my home to families \ndisplaced by war, and blocking masked federal agents from \nseizing neighbors.\nNo Blank Checks for Corporations. $7.2 billion in data center \nproperty value is tax-exempt, while their water and power \ndemands drive up residents’ utility costs and schools absorb \n$20 million in cuts. At my business, I provide strong wages, \nhealthcare, retirement, and paid time off, because the people \ndoing the work are the ones who build its value. I’ll bring that \nstandard to council: public support must produce greater public \nbenefit, starting with housing people can afford.\nProtect Our Neighbors. When corporations want special \ntreatment, the council finds a way. When families need \nprotection, officials say their “hands are tied.” I know what tied \nhands cost a family; my uncle spent a year in ICE detention. \nI’ll fund legal and emergency aid, enforce Hillsboro’s sanctuary \nlaw, and establish a Human Rights Office connecting impacted \nfamilies with resources.\nYour Voice in Every Vote. I’ll hold weekly town halls and virtual \noffice hours, seeking opinions early on, when they can still \nchange the vote.\nEndorsements:\nWashington County Democrats \nOregon Working Families Party \nLatino Network Action Fund \nSEIU Oregon \nOregon League of Conservation Voters \nFriends of Smart Growth \nNext Up Action Fund\nKipperlyn Sinclair, Hillsboro City Councilor \nOlivia Alcaire, Hillsboro City Councilor \nJuan Carlos González, Metro Council President \nNafisa Fai, Washington County Commissioner \nFarrah Chaichi, State Representative \nLesly Muñoz, State Representative \nSusan McLain, State Representative \nMyrna Muñoz, State Senate Nominee\nProgressive Slate: Diana Jackson; Dorian Russell\nFull endorsements at www.karimdelgado.com.\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Karim Delgado\n\nWC-18\nCouncil Member, Ward 3, Position A Council Member, Ward 3, Position A\nCity of HillsboroCity of Hillsboro\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nTitonian     \nWallace Sr.\nOccupation: Small Business \nOwner and Ride-Share Driver\nOccupational Background: \nCommunity College Instructor, \nPCC Small Business \nDevelopment Center; Health \nServices, State of Kansas\nEducational Background: BA, \nWashburn University (Topeka, \nKS); Marketing Certificate, Fort \nHays State University (Fort \nHays, KS)\nPrior Governmental Experience: Hillsboro School Board \nBond Development Committee; Washington County Democrats \nPrecinct Committee Person\nStatement:\nAs a father of four and small business owner, I’ll never stop \nworking to uplift Hillsboro and support my family. I believe \nin Hillsboro – a thriving community of working-class small \nbusiness owners where families can afford to stay and every \nresident knows they belong.\nAs your City Councilor, I will prioritize:\n• Supporting local economic development and jobs that \nbenefit our community, making responsible decisions about \ngrowth and infrastructure\n• Investing in safe neighborhoods, transportation, and public \nspaces\n• Expanding housing opportunities, so working families and \nseniors can continue to call Hillsboro home\n“We are at a crossroads and need leaders who put Hillsboro \nfirst. That’s why I urge you to vote for Titonian.”\n-  ROB HARRIS, CITY COUNCIL PRESIDENT\n“As an educator and local leader, I’ve dedicated my life \nto serving Hillsboro. These difficult times require a leader \nlike Titonian – focused on solutions and dedicated to our \ncommunity.”\n-  STEVE CALLAWAY, FORMER MAYOR\nENDORSEMENTS\nCity of Hillsboro\nBeach Pace, Mayor\nRob Harris, Council President \nElizabeth Case, Councilor\nOregon Legislature\nSenator  Janeen  Sollman  (D-Hillsboro) \nSenator  Lisa  Reynolds  (D-Beaverton) \nRepresentative  Hai  Pham  (D-Hillsboro) \nRepresentative  Nathan  Sosa  (D-Hillsboro)\nHillsboro School Board\nKatie Rhyne, Chair \nMark Watson, Vice-Chair\nIvette Pantoja, Former Chair \nYessica Hardin-Mercado \nNancy Thomas\nWashington County \nPam Treece, Commissioner \nJerry Willey, Commissioner \nJason Snider, Commissioner\nFelicita Monteblanco, Commissioner-elect \nKristine Adams-Wannberg, Auditor\nCommunity & Business Leaders\nMike McMurray, CEO Hillsboro Hops \nSteve Callaway, former Hillsboro Mayor \nTom Hughes, former Hillsboro Mayor\nHector R. Hinojosa, Community Leader\nOrganizations\nHillsboro Professional Firefighters \nDemocratic Municipal Officials \nHillsboro Police Officers Association \nHillsboro Classified United 4671 \nJoint Council of Teamsters No. 37 \nWashington County Chamber PAC \nIndependent Party of Oregon \nHumane Voters Oregon\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Titonian\nDorian Russell\nOccupation: Executive Director, \nHomePlate Youth Services; \nArtist.\nOccupational Background: \nScientific research, policy, and \ncivil rights advocacy.\nEducational Background: \nEnvironmental Science M.S., \nInterdisciplinary B.A. in science, \neconomics, & policy, and \nAdvanced Leadership Studies \nCertificate from American \nUniversity, Washington, D.C.; \nFBI Multicultural Youth Leadership Academy, Portland; Portland \nCommunity College and Liberty High School Valedictorian.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Career: AmeriCorps; \nU.S. Departments of Justice and Energy; National Science \nFoundation; Smithsonian Institution; City of Hillsboro; \nWashington County. Service: Board Director, Northwest \nRegional Education Service District; Hillsboro Library Board \n(past Chair); Hillsboro Youth Advisory Council (past Chair).\nMake Our Government Work for the Working Class \nAs a teen in Hillsboro, Dorian got food from church pantries \nand showers at the Shute Park pool. Dorian’s work addressing \nour affordability and homelessness crises is personal: we need \nDorian’s rare combination of lived experience and expertise on \nCouncil.\nDorian believes: \n• There is enough wealth, grit, and humanity in our Silicon \nForest to ensure every child is safe, warm, and fed. \n• Local governments are the front line of democracy and \nmust comply with community priorities, not illegal ICE \nkidnappings and unconstitutional executive orders.\n• Preserving our access to nature and family farms creates \nwhole communities; unchecked data center expansion \nmakes the rich richer at the expense of our air, water, land, \nand jobs.\n• Being a “business leader” means sharing prosperity \nthrough living wages, benefits, stable retirement, and \nlivelihoods we can be proud of.\nSelected Endorsements:\nWashington County Democrats\nOregon Working Families Party\nBasic Rights Oregon\nNextUp Action Fund\nOregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\nOregon League of Conservation Voters\nData Center Defiance \nSunrisePDX\nFriends of Smart Growth\nNafisa Fai, County Commissioner \nJuan Carlos González, Metro Council President \nBarry Johnson-Smith, Advisory Council for Racial Equity Chair\nKristi Wilson, former PCC Board Chair\nLesly Muñoz, Oregon House District 22\nSusan McLain, Oregon House District 29\nOlivia Alcaire & Kipperlyn Sinclair, Hillsboro City Councilors \nStephanie Hernández, Library Board Chair\nKarim Delgado & Diana Jackson, Seeds of Change \nMyrna A. Muñoz\nvotedorianrussell.com\nhillsboroseedsofchange.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Dorian Russell\n\nWC-19\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of King CityCity of King City\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nHoward \nSuesserman\nOccupation: Retired \nOccupational Background: \nUnited States Postal Service\nEducational Background: \nCerritos Junior college\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: 35 years with \ndistinction 10 years as Postal Supervisor overseeing 70-90 FTE\nStatement of Candidate: \nHello, my name is Howard Suesserman, and I am running \nfor King City Council to bring common-sense solutions and \naccountability back to our local government.\nOver the last 2½ years, King City has cycled through 15 city \ncouncilors, 4 mayors, and 2 city managers. This unprecedented \nturnover has led to government waste and decisions that ignore \nthe voice of the residents. As your councilman, I will focus on:\n• Fiscal Responsibility: The current council authorized \n$100,000 of your tax dollars for a “Relook” of the existing \nTransportation System Plan (TSP)—an established \nordinance that will cost even more time and money to \nchange. We must stop spending without public input.\n• Direct Representation: The November ballot includes \nMeasure 34-354 to update the city charter, but it fails \nto let citizens directly elect their mayor. Currently, just \nfour council members control that choice. That needs to \nchange.\n• Local Control: Our planning commission is now \nrepresented by 4 out of 7 individuals from outside the city. \nI believe the commission must reflect the residents and \nHOAs that actually exist within our city boundaries.\nThrough my years as a postal employee, I have walked these \nneighborhoods, delivered your mail, and listened to your \nconcerns. Decisions that affect us all cannot be determined by \na few special interests. I know the pulse of this community and \nwould be honored to represent you.\nVote Howard Suesserman for King City Council.\nThis Information Furnished By: Howard Suesserman\nGretchen \nBuehner\nOccupation: Attorney \nMediator and Arbitrator\nOccupational Background: \nAttorney, Real Estate and Land \nUse, and Estate Planning, 1981 \nto 2023\nMediator and Arbitrator 1988 to \npresent\nOwner, Contract Advice Online \n2024 to present\nEducational Background: Lewis & Clark Law School, JD, 1981\nMills College, BA, Biochemistry and History, Oakland, CA, 1972\nPrior Governmental Experience: King City Planning \nCommission, 2015 to 2017, 2023 to present\nChair, King City Planning Commission, 2024 to present; \nKing City Council, 2017 to 2020\nTigard City Council 2007 to 2014\nProven Leadership for King City’s Future\nHello, I’m Gretchen Buehner, and I’m running for King City \nCouncil because experience matters when decisions get hard. \nEvery resident deserves a Council member who listens first.\nWhat I’ll do for you:\n• Advocate for a voter elected mayor\n• Manage growth to keep King City livable\n• Protect neighborhood character in every land-use decision\n• Keep City Hall transparent and accessible\nKing City is growing fast. That growth must be managed with \nreal land-use expertise and genuine accountability to residents, \nnot left to staff and outside interests alone.\nPlease tell me what matters to you as a King City resident.\nThank you for considering me for another term of service to King \nCity.\nSincerely, \nGretchen E. Buehner\nThis Information Furnished By: Gretchen Buehner\n\nWC-20\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of King CityCity of King City\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSteve Gearhart\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nSales Management, 12 years\nEngineering, Leupold Scopes, \n13 years\nOwner, Coyote Manufacturing, \n11 years\nEducational Background: \nJackson High School, Diploma\nPCC, HR Management\nPSU Supervisory Development\nDale Carnegie, Public Speaking\nPrior Governmental Experience: King City, Current Council \nPresident\nKing City, Budget Committee\nPort of The Dalles Budget Committee\nColumbia Gorge Fire & Rescue Budget Committee\nThe Dalles Area Chamber of Commerce, Board Member\nPCC Volunteer Tutor/Mentor\nI’m Steve Gearhart and I’m asking for your vote to be re-\nelected. I have a strong commitment to listening. There is a \nreason we have two ears and one mouth. My job as a City \nCouncilor is to hear what residents are telling us and help turn \nthose concerns into action. I want to hear from you!\nMore than 600 people contacted City Council and said “Do \nnot let Fischer Road go through”. I heard them. I believe local \ngovernment works best when residents know their voices \nmatter.\nAs Council President, I have worked to bring people together. \nOur Council has faced important decisions, worked through \ndifferent points of view, and made real progress toward stronger \nCity operations and better long-term planning.\nA lot has happened since I have been on City Council, we hired \na great new City Manager, completed Strategic Planning, hired \na consultant to determine how we can better control Fischer \nRoad.\nWith the rapid new growth, it’s even more critical to listen to our \nresidents and proceed carefully and logically to plan our future \ntogether. I can deliver real results with my problem-solving, \ncollaboration and strong communication skills.\nThere will always be issues where people disagree. That is \nwhen listening matters most. I believe good leadership means \nasking questions, hearing people, finding common ground, and \nworking together to get things done.\nThat’s where I come in. I listen first, work through differences, \nand help people find a way forward.\nEndorsed by Rachel Kazmierski, Mayor of King City\nEndorsed by John Cook, former Mayor of Tigard\nThis Information Furnished By: Steve Gearhart\nSandy \nCunningham\nOccupation: Retired\nCurrent King City Councilor \n(appointed)\nOccupational Background: \nLegal Administration - managed \noperations, procedures, and \nfinancial oversight for legal \norganizations, with a focus \non efficient, accountable \nprocesses.\nEducational Background: Portland Community College - \nBusiness Administration\nPrior Governmental Experience: Current King City Councilor \n(appointed)\nI believe our community deserves open leadership, careful use \nof taxpayer dollars, and a Council that listens to residents.\nI have spent the past several years studying City budgets and \nissues, attending Council meetings, researching community \nconcerns, and listening to residents. That work has given me a \nstrong understanding of the challenges and opportunities facing \nKing City.\nMy background in legal administration taught me the value \nof good procedures, careful financial oversight, efficiency, \nand accountability. Together with my fellow councilors, I have \nbrought that focus to our work. We have worked to improve City \noperations, strengthen financial oversight, and plan for King \nCity’s future.\nDuring my time on the Council, we worked through several \nimportant outstanding issues. We hired a new city manager \nand city attorney, completed negotiations on an expired union \ncontract for city employees, and adopted a new budget.  \nWe also set clearer goals, improved financial reporting and \ncommunication, and began reviewing the Transportation \nSystem Plan for possible changes.\nI have also advocated for our senior community by learning \nabout programs, services, and financial or property tax help \navailable through regional, county, and state agencies.\nAs your councilor, I will work to:\n• Protect taxpayer dollars through responsible \nbudgeting.\n• Promote transparent and accountable local \ngovernment.\n• Support public safety and maintain high-quality city \nservices.\n• Plan for growth while protecting the character of our \nneighborhoods.\n• Listen to residents and encourage public participation.\nI am committed to accountable leadership, building on the \nprogress our community has made, and keeping residents at \nthe center of decisions about King City’s future.  It would be an \nhonor to continue serving King City and its residents.\nAccountability. Progress. Community.\nThis Information Furnished By: Sandy Cunningham\n\nWC-21\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of King CityCity of King City\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJames Minor Sr.\nOccupation: \nSenior Technical Writer. \nOccupational Background:\nI began my career as a \ntechnician, working in that field \nfor approximately 10 years, \nfollowed by about 10 years as \nan instructor. I then became a \nsenior technical writer, working \nwith both hardware and software. My career has centered on \neducating people, providing accurate information, documenting \ncomplex subjects, and working with others to solve problems.\nEducational Background: \nAssociate degree from Macomb County Community College, \nwith a major in Mathematics and Physics.\nPrior Governmental Experience: \nI served on the board of the King City Civic Association (KCCA), \nrepresenting a community of approximately 1,700 residents, \nbefore becoming its president. I also previously served as \npresident of an HOA in Lyons, Colorado. These experiences \ntaught me the importance of listening to residents, providing \ndirection, addressing difficult problems, and following projects \nthrough to completion.\nAdditional Information: \nI have lived in King City for eight years and want to serve on the \nCity Council because I care about the future of our community. \nKing City is growing, and I believe growth needs to be managed \nresponsibly while considering its impact on residents, taxes, \ninfrastructure, and the character of our community.\nAs a council member, I will work collaboratively with the other \ncouncil members, city staff, and residents. My approach is \nstraightforward: listen to the concerns, understand the problem, \nconsider the available options, and work together to find the \nbest practical solution. I also believe we should build on the \nwork of previous councils rather than continually starting over.\nI am not seeking office to promote an agenda. I want to use my \nexperience in leadership, problem-solving, communication, and \nworking with people to help make sound decisions and do what \nis best for King City and its residents.\nThis Information Furnished By: James Minor Sr.\nNo Photo \nSubmitted \nCraig Dirksen \nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nMechanical Engineering\nEducational Background: \nKlickitat High School, Klickitat \nWashington\nColumbia Basin College, Pasco, \nWashington Clark College,   \n     Vancouver, Washington\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tigard City Council\nTigard Mayor\nOregon Metro District 3 Representative\nChair, Metro Joint Policy Advisory Committee\nVote Craig Dirksen for King City Council!\n-Experience\n-Knowledge\n-Understanding\nCraig and his wife Jackie have lived in King City for four years \nafter living and raising a family for 46 years in Tigard, where \nCraig was involved in local politics.\n-12 years Tigard City Council\n-Nine years Tigard Mayor\n-Eight years Metro Council\nCraig understands the challenges a small city like King City \nfaces, and would be a rational, moderate voice on Council to \nkeep King City a great place to live.\nCraig seeks no endorsements and no donations to his campaign \nfrom anyone and is not beholden to anyone, either inside or \noutside King City.\nThis Information Furnished By: Craig Dirksen\n\nWC-22\nCouncilor Councilor\nCity of Lake OswegoCity of Lake Oswego\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nBryan Guiney\nOccupation: Oregon Field \nOffice Director, US Department \nof Housing and Urban \nDevelopment\nOccupational Background: \nInfantry Officer, US Marine \nCorps; Development Officer, \nBusiness Oregon; Committee \nAdministrator, Oregon \nLegislative Assembly\nEducational Background: University of Oregon, BA (2006); \nUniversity of Washington, MBA (2017)\nPrior Governmental Experience: Eagle Creek Wildfire \nRecovery Council; Oregon Federal Executive Board; Lake \nOswego Planning Commission\nCommunity Service: Oregon Veteran Community Foundation; \nUrban Nature Partners PDX\nPublic service is my life’s work. In the Marines, at the State, \nand in the Federal government, I’ve spent my career bringing \npeople together, managing public resources, solving problems, \nand delivering results. I will bring that experience to help Lake \nOswego navigate important decisions ahead.\nAs a father and husband raising my family here, I want \nLake Oswego to remain a community where families thrive, \nbusinesses succeed, and residents feel heard.\nI value service, accountability, and results. I will listen first, \ndo the homework, seek common ground, and make decisions \nbased on Lake Oswego’s long-term interests. \nMy priorities:\n• Grow smart and sustainably while protecting \nneighborhoods and our environment.\n• Keep Lake Oswego open for business and support \nvibrant commercial districts.\n• Practice fiscal discipline and responsibly steward \ntaxpayer dollars.\n• Invest in infrastructure and mobility to improve traffic, \nsafety, and connectivity.\n• Support our City workforce with clear expectations and \nthe tools to succeed.\n• Keep residents at the center of City Hall through \ntransparency and responsiveness.\nI would be honored to earn your vote. www.BryanGuiney.com\nEndorsements: Mayor Joe Buck; Councilors Rachel Verdick, \nMassene Mboup, Trudy Corrigan, John Wendland, Ali Afghan, \nJackie Manz; LO School Board Chair Kirsten Aird; Vice-Chair \nNeelam Gupta; Directors John Wallin, Kate Lupton; Clackamas \nCounty Chair Craig Roberts; Commissioner Martha Schrader; \nSheriff Angela Brandenburg (ret.); Metro Council President Juan \nCarlos González; Metro Councilor Christine Lewis; Professional \nFirefighters of Clackamas County IAFF Local 1159; Oregon \nLeague of Conservation Voters; Lake Oswego Chamber of \nCommerce; AFSCME Local 1546.\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Bryan Guiney PAC #24881\nSuzanne Miles\nOccupation: Attorney and Law \nProfessor\nOccupational Background: US \nDepartment of Justice, Assistant \nUS Attorney, District of Oregon; \nLaw Professor, University of \nOregon School of Law and UC \nBerkeley School of Law\nEducational Background: \nStanford Law School, J.D. \n(2005); The George Washington \nUniversity, BA (2000)\nPrior Governmental Experience: US Environmental Protection \nAgency, Regulatory Specialist; Forest Hills Elementary School \nParent Teacher Organization\nPersonal:\nLake Oswego is where I’m raising my daughters.  As a mom to \ntwo middle schoolers and a lifelong public servant, our schools, \nsafe neighborhoods and greenspace are my top priorities. \nPriorities: \nThat’s why I’m committed to: \n• SCHOOLS: Maintaining excellence in our schools by \nfostering city partnerships with our school communities \n• SAFETY: Supporting our local police and firefighters to \nkeep our community safe and welcoming\n• ENVIRONMENT: Nurturing our natural resources to keep \nLake Oswego green and thriving\n• SMALL BUSINESS: Partnering with businesses and \ncommunity groups as the heart of our healthy local \neconomy\n \n“Suzanne’s commitment to public service and public safety are \nunmatched. She is calm, direct, and skilled at getting results. \nYou will not find a better candidate for City Council.”  \n-BILLY WILLIAMS, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY, DISTRICT OF \nOREGON \n“As Chair of the Lake Oswego School Board, I have a deep \nrespect for Suzanne’s service to our nation, her intelligence, and \nher deep care for our community. Vote for Suzanne.”  \nNEELAM GUPTA, LAKE OSWEGO SCHOOL DISTRICT \nBOARD CHAIR\nENDORSEMENTS  \n \nLake Oswego Mayor Joe Buck \nLake Oswego School Board Chair Neelam Gupta\nRepresentative Daniel Nguyen \nLake Oswego City Councilor Ali Afghan\nLake Oswego City Councilor Trudy Corrigan\nLake Oswego City Councilor Jackie Manz \nLake Oswego City Councilor Massene Mboup\nLake Oswego City Councilor Rachel Verdick\nLake Oswego City Councilor John Wendland \nClackamas County Commissioner-Elect Sonya Fischer \nMetro Regional Government Councilor Christine Lewis  \nClackamas Education Service District Director Linda Brown\nKimvi To, Community Organizer \nThis Information Furnished By: Suzanne for Lake Oswego\n\nWC-23\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of North PlainsCity of North Plains\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nMatt Blancas\nOccupation: Federal \nAdministrator\nOccupational Background: \nPublic administration; human \nresources management and \nconsulting; policy development \nand implementation; regulatory \ncompliance; organizational \ndevelopment; executive \nadministration; higher education \nadministration and student \nservices; disability services; \nprofessional learning and training; program and project \ncoordination; public-sector operations\nEducational Background: University of Montana, B.S., \nBusiness Administration; Portland State University, Executive \nMaster of Public Administration; Pacific University, Education \nand Leadership; U.S. Army Adjutant General School, Human \nResources Administration\nPrior Governmental Experience: U.S. Army, Sergeant \nand Human Resources Specialist; City of Portland, Human \nResources Consultant; City of Hillsboro, Public Administration \nFellow; Portland Community College, Executive Administration \nand Human Resources; Portland State University, \nAdministration and Student Services; Oregon State Licensing \nBoard, Public Member\nA NEIGHBOR. A LEADER. A VOICE FOR NORTH PLAINS.\nNorth Plains deserves city government that is transparent, \nfinancially responsible, and accountable to the people it serves.\nI am running for City Council because I believe effective local \ngovernment starts with listening to residents, managing public \nresources responsibly, and making decisions with both today’s \nneeds and our community’s future in mind.\nTRANSPARENT & ACCOUNTABLE GOVERNMENT \nResidents deserve clear communication, accessible \ninformation, and meaningful opportunities to participate in \ndecisions affecting their community.\nFINANCIAL STABILITY \nI will support responsible budgeting, careful stewardship of \ntaxpayer dollars, and long-term financial planning that protects \nessential city services.\nSTRONG CITY ADMINISTRATION \nOur city needs clear policies, regulatory compliance, effective \noperations, and the organizational capacity to provide reliable \nservices to residents.\nTHOUGHTFUL LEADERSHIP \nEvery major decision should consider its long-term financial, \noperational, and community impacts. I will ask questions, \nevaluate the evidence, and approach decisions independently \nand respectfully.\nNorth Plains is our community. Regardless of where we may \nstand on individual issues, we share an interest in a city \ngovernment that works well, listens, and earns the public’s trust.\nI would be honored to earn your vote for North Plains City \nCouncil.\nThis Information Furnished By: Matt Blancas\nDon Weber\nOccupation: North Plains City \nCouncilor\nOccupational Background: \nRetired Director: a Big Brother \nand Big Sisters program\nYouth Minister, Finance Director \nand Parish Administrator: St. \nAloysius Gonzaga Parish and \nSchool in Spokane, Washington\nEducational Background:\nUniversity of Montana: Business Management degree\nGonzaga University: Masters in Pastoral Ministry\nPrior Governmental Experience:\nNorth Plains Re-Look Committee\nNorth Plains Parks & Recreation Board\nMy vision for North Plains comes from daily walks down almost \nevery street in town. We have a wonderful city, but one that \nmust improve how we serve our communities, businesses, and \nland within our boundaries. \nBefore 2025, council spent tremendous resources trying to \nmore than double the city’s size while underserving current \nresidents and businesses. I support improving city infrastructure \nfor safety and efficiency, and using our Urban Renewal Agency \nto better serve local businesses, especially on main street. We \nmust serve those within our current boundaries before adding \nhundreds of new acres. \nI support smart growth-- not expanding our city by over 500 \nacres with a majority of that land for industrial growth. We were \ntold for years that intensive industrial growth would produce \nnet financial benefits, but were never given accurate financial \nprojections. I will convene residents to hear from experts (not \ndevelopers and land speculators) on growth issues. \nMy background is in finance, so fiscal responsibility is important \nfor me. We are one of a few cities that are years behind on \naudits, making it impossible to have an accurate picture of \nour city’s financial health. I have advocated for North Plains to \ncomplete past due audits, and will complete this task if elected.\nI humbly ask you to support me and my fellow candidates \nMelody Axe and Anna Warrington for North Plains City Council.\nhttps://www.friendsofsmartgrowth.org/north-plains\nEndorsements:\nFriends of Smart Growth\nSave Helvetia\nLora Dexheimer\nOregon Data Center Defiance\nDean Moberg and Elaine Stewart: directors, Tualatin \nConservation District\nMark DeForge and Anna Warrington: North Plains City \nCouncilors\nMelody Axe, North Plains City Council candidate\nCheri Olson, Former North Plains Mayor\nThis Information Furnished By: Don Weber\n\nWC-24\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of North PlainsCity of North Plains\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nAnna Warrington\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nTeam Lead; Project \nManagement; Financial Analyst\nEducational Background: \nAssociates Degree in \nSupervision/Management\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: North Plains City \nCouncilor 2026\nAs a North Plains resident for two decades and a retired \nTeam Lead and Project Manager, I am proud to serve as your \nappointed City Councilor. Now, I am officially running for a \nCity Council seat to continue serving our wonderful small city. \nThroughout my 20-year career, I learned the value of asking \nthe right questions, researching facts, and solving issues \nsystematically. I bring this proven approach to our local \ngovernment to ensure our community thrives.\n*I REMAIN AGAINST HUGE EXPANSIONS IN NORTH PLAINS*\nMy focus is on constant, high-quality development over rapid \nexpansion that reflects what North Plains residents want. \nWhen 71% of voters rejected Measure 34-327, it was clear that \nfamilies want North Plains to grow in a small, thoughtful way.  \n*I SUPPORT NORTH PLAINS BUSINESSES*\nI’m focused on our Urban Renewal Agency fundings that can \nhelp our local businesses thrive. Growing & developing our \nbusiness is key to a successful city plan. \n*FINISHING CITY AUDITS IS A MUST*\nAddressing overdue audits is our top priority. We just received \nthe 2021/22 audit from our accountants. I will keep working on \nthe remaining reports until everything is current. Our community \ndeserves complete transparency on how city funds are handled, \nas well as full compliance with all state requirements.\nThere is much to do in coming years, and I am ready. I will work \nfor ALL residents, protect our rural environment, and deliver the \nbest results for the city we call home.\nI would be honored to have your vote.\nEndorsed by:\n  \nAaron Nichols – Friends of Smart Growth\nMel & Wendy Mortensen – Shepards Rest Tree Farm\nLinda Gales-Merten – Merten Vineyards LLC\nJohn Platt - Helvetia Winery, LLC\nFaun Hosey – Save Helvetia\nMandy Hagedorn – Previous NP City Councilor\nDon Weber – NP City Councilor\nMark DeForge – NP City Councilor\nLora Dexheimer \nThis Information Furnished By: Anna Warrington\nTera VanDyke\nOccupation: Director of Fiscal \nOperations, Northwest Regional \nEducation Service District\nOccupational Background: \nController; Assistant Finance \nDirector; Accounting Manager; \nAccountant; Accounts Payable \nClerk — Northwest Regional \nEducation Service District\nEducational Background: \nB.S., Accounting, Linfield \nCollege; MBA, Finance concentration, Washington State \nUniversity (in progress); School Finance Officer (SFO) \ncredential.\nPrior Governmental Experience: North Plains Budget \nCommittee; board and committee service with the Association of \nSchool Business Officials International and Oregon Association \nof School Business Officials.\nI was born and raised in North Plains, and my family has been \npart of this community for generations. I care deeply about \npreserving what makes North Plains special while preparing \nresponsibly for its future.\nI bring more than 18 years of public finance experience \nin budgeting, audits, compliance, financial planning, and \nstewardship of public funds. I am running because North Plains \nneeds strong financial oversight, transparent decision-making, \norganizational stability, dependable services, and leadership \ncommitted to rebuilding public trust.\nSound decisions require reliable, timely financial information. \nStaffing challenges have contributed to delays in completing \nrequired financial work and prior-year audits. Bringing \nthose audits current, addressing previous audit findings, \nand maintaining strong financial processes will help the city \nunderstand its financial position and plan responsibly for the \nfuture.\nGrowth should strengthen our community—not simply make \nit bigger. I support development that protects our quality of \nlife, considers impacts on infrastructure and city services, and \nprovides lasting value to residents. I do not believe data centers \nare the right fit for North Plains.\nGood local government listens. I will ask questions, gather \nthe facts, listen to residents and staff, and approach decisions \nthoughtfully and collaboratively.\nDoing nothing changes nothing. I decided it was time to step \nforward, bring my experience to the table, and help shape a \nstrong future for North Plains—one that preserves what makes \nour community special and makes it even better for generations \nto come.\nStay informed. Ask questions. Show up.\nThis Information Furnished By: Tera VanDyke\n\nWC-25\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of North PlainsCity of North Plains\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nMelody Axe\nOccupation: Direct Support \nSpecialist for people with \npersistent mental illness\nOccupational Background: \nCommunity Action: Family \nAdvocate throughout local and \nstatewide systems\nNational Alliance For Mental \nIllness Washington County: \nPrograms Manager\nDepartment of Human Services: \nHuman Services Specialist\nEducational Background: OSU: Human Development, Early \nChildhood Education                \nPCC: Early Childhood Education\nPrior Governmental Experience: Washington County \nPartnership for Student Success Leadership Council\nWashington County Children’s System of Care Advisory \nCommittee\nWashington County Mental Health Quality Assurance \nCommittee\nI love North Plains. It’s a gem. You feel it the moment you enter \ntown. The warmth of a small community, the beauty of our \nsurroundings, our pride in where we live and the connections \nthat make North Plains feel like home.\nPeople and families have always been at the heart of my \nprofessional and volunteer life. I’ve spent years listening to \npeople, bringing people together, navigating complicated \nchallenges and making our community stronger.\nI would bring that experience to City Council, along with calm \ncuriosity, the willingness to listen and ask questions before \ndeciding, and to seek understanding before acting.\nI believe reasonable, intentional growth should help North \nPlains thrive without sacrificing what makes it special. \nProtecting surrounding farmland is essential, as are our natural \nbeauty, local businesses, and sense of community. Thriving \nmeans adding to these assets to make them better, while \npreserving them thoughtfully as we prepare for the future.\nMy decision-making will be rooted in community engagement. \nA strong, healthy town requires its elected leaders to stay \nconnected. I will ask for your thoughts and decide on issues \nwith you. If you trust me with your vote, I pledge to work for you, \nnot for outside land speculators and developers.\nEndorsements:\n• Friends of Smart Growth\n• Save Helvetia\n• Lora Dexheimer\n• Oregon Data Center Defiance\n• Dean Moberg and Elaine Stewart: directors, Tualatin \nConservation District\n• Mark DeForge, Don Weber and Anna Warrington: North \nPlains City Councilors\n• Cheri Olson, Former North Plains Mayor\nPlease vote for me, Anna Warrington and Don Weber for \nNorth Plains City Council.\nhttps://www.friendsofsmartgrowth.org/north-plains\nThis Information Furnished By: Melody Axe\nLisa Ferrari-\nHarwood\nOccupation: Director of \nCommunity Outreach.\nOccupational Background: 18 \nyears of community outreach \nand food pantry as Director of \nTrue Life Panty in Beaverton, \nserving people experiencing \nfood insecurity and/or \nhomelessness.\nEducational Background: \nMaster of Theology, Christian Life School of Theology.\nPrior Governmental Experience: HOA Board President, City of \nBeaverton DVRT volunteer, City of Hillsboro DVRT volunteer.\nIt’s time to heal North Plains.\nNorth Plains is more than a place we live—it is a community \nwe share. I am running for City Council because I believe our \ncommunity deserves leadership that listens, communicates \nopenly, and brings people together.\nFor 18 years, I have served our community through True \nLife Pantry, working with volunteers, churches, businesses, \nand community partners to help neighbors facing difficult \ncircumstances. That experience has taught me that leadership \nbegins with listening. It means showing up, treating people with \ndignity, asking hard questions, and working together to find \nsolutions.\nAs your City Councilor, I will bring that same commitment to \nserving North Plains.\n• Strengthen transparency and communication between city \ngovernment and residents.\n• Encourage respectful dialogue and make sure residents feel \nheard, even when we disagree.\n• Support responsible growth while protecting the character, \nlivability, and future of North Plains.\n• Practice responsible stewardship of taxpayer dollars and \nadvocate for thoughtful, accountable decision-making.\n• Support families, seniors, local businesses, and community \norganizations that make North Plains a great place to call home.\nI am not running to create more division. I am running because I \nbelieve we can do better together.\nLeadership is not about having all the answers. It is about \nhaving the courage to listen, the integrity to seek the truth, \nand the willingness to put the community ahead of personal \ninterests.\nNorth Plains has a strong foundation and tremendous potential \nfor a thriving future.\nIt’s time to heal.\nIt’s time to listen.\nIt’s time to work together to ban data farms.\nIt’s time to restore the heart of North Plains.\nI would be honored to serve you!\nThis Information Furnished By: Lisa Ferrari-Harwood\n\nWC-26\nAuditor Council Member, District 4\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSimone Rede\nOccupation: Portland City \nAuditor \nOccupational Background: \nGovernment Performance \nAuditor\nEducational Background:  \nGrant High School; Vassar \nCollege, BA Urban Studies; \nCarnegie Mellon, MS Public \nPolicy & Management; Certified \nInternal Auditor\n \nPrior Governmental Experience: State & Metro performance \nauditor; Oregon Department of Corrections Audit Committee \nMember; U.S. Government Accountability Office Advisory \nCouncil Member \n \nCommunity Service: Multnomah Education Service District \ncounselor; SUN Community Schools program coordinator; \nAmeriCorps volunteer; Oregon Center for Public Policy board \nmember\n \nPortland deserves an auditor who sees the promise of our \npeople and our city and will use the office to get the best \npossible outcomes for all of us.\n \nAs your accountability champion I have worked to:\n• Sustain improvements to 911 call wait times for our \ncommunity.\n• Strengthen oversight of the city’s Arts Access Fund and \nensure the city follows through on climate commitments.\n• Call attention to Portland’s roads, parks, and asset \nmaintenance backlogs.\nThere is much more work to be done. You can count on me to \nuphold highest professional standards, remain independent, \nand apply my auditing authority and expertise to ensure fair \nopportunities and effective use of tax dollars.\n \nEXPERIENCED PROFESSIONAL \n \n“Portland’s city government needs an experienced and \nprofessional auditor. Simone has exactly the right auditing \nexperience for this position.” – Gary Blackmer, former State, \nCounty & City Auditor  \n \n \nWORKING FOR PORTLANDERS\n \n“Simone’s leadership has improved the quality of services to \nPortlanders and made city government more just.” \n – Danny Cage, MESD Board Member\nJoin Us in Supporting Simone Rede! \n \nMetro Auditor Brian Evans \nWashington County Auditor Kristine Adams-Wannberg \nFormer County & Metro Auditor Suzanne Flynn \nFormer City Commissioner Amanda Fritz \nEast County Rising PAC\nAPANO Action Fund\nBuilding Power for Communities of Color\nOregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\nPortland Gray Panthers\nOregon AFSCME \nAFSCME Local 189\n \nLearn more at www.simoneforauditor.com\n \nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Simone Rede\nJeremy   \nBeausoleil Smith\nOccupation: Capital Projects \n& Construction Manager at \nPortland State University \nOccupational Background: \nConstruction Management; \nProject Engineer - Kiewit\nEducational Background: \nVirginia Tech, BS Sustainable \nBuilding Construction\nPrior Governmental Experience: Multnomah County \nDemocratic Party Precinct Committee Person, West Multnomah \nSoil & Water Conservation District Budget Committee\nCommunity Service: Blazers Boys & Girls Club, Young \nDemocrats\nPORTLAND’S BEST DAYS ARE AHEAD OF US\nPortland is way too expensive. I’ll fight to lower costs and \nmake life easier.\nI’ve managed millions of dollars in taxpayer money at PSU on \nprojects like the Veterans Resource Center and the new $90 \nmillion Art and Design Building. \nI know what it takes to be a good steward of the taxpayer \ndollar.\nI’m running on a platform of Housing as a Human Right, a Green \nNew Deal for Portland, Fighting back against Trump’s Fascist \nAgenda, and Standing up for our Democracy! \nWe’re facing co-crises of affordability, climate, housing, \naddiction, mental health, and a hostile federal government. \nLIFE DOESN’T HAVE TO BE THIS HARD.\nPriorities\n• Housing for All: A Renters’ Bill of Rights will keep \nPortlanders in their homes as we further invest in housing \nwith mental health and addiction services while rapidly \nbuilding affordable Social Housing.\n• Infrastructure for All: A Green New Deal for Portland \nincludes addressing the threat of the Critical Energy \nInfrastructure Hub, fixing potholes, expanding pedestrian \n& cyclist infrastructure with union jobs, ending Ross \nIsland Toxic Algae Blooms, and protecting PCEF.\n• Community Safety For All: I’ll fight for safe school \nroutes, Expanding Portland Street Response, and faster \nemergency response times.\n• Standing Up for Human Rights: Protecting our Trans and \nImmigrant Neighbors and Holding ICE Accountable.\nI support a 4 Year AI Data Center Ban\nI oppose the Horrific Genocide in Gaza\nPortland’s worth fighting for, and I’ll fight for you every \nsingle day!\nPartial List of Endorsers: \nPSU-AAUP, Rep. Travis Nelson\nLatino Network Action Fund, Steph Routh - Portland Planning \nCommission, Chair\nSunrise Movement PDX, Katrina Doughty - Multnomah \nEducation Service District, Chair \nPro-Animal Oregon, Pacific Green Party\nFull List: jeremy4pdx.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Jeremy Beausoleil Smith for \nPortland City Council\n\nWC-27\nCouncil Member, District 4 Council Member, District 4\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nMitch Green\nOccupation: City Councilor\nOccupational Background: \nCivil Servant\nEducational Background: \nEconomics PhD, UMKC \nPrior Government \nExperience: Bonneville Power \nAdministration\nHi, Portland. I’m Mitch Green, \na PhD economist, a US Army \nveteran, a democratic socialist, and a Portland City Councilor \nrepresenting District 4.\nIn just two years in office I’ve delivered for working families \n– not corporations, and certainly not billionaires. I’ve \ncreated policies that advance workers’ rights, tenants’ rights, \naffordability, and environmental justice, while championing \ntransparency, good governance, and a holistic public safety \nsystem.\nWhat I’ve done so far:\n• Kickstarted Portland’s Social Housing program\n• Resisted ICE and federal overreach\n• Investigated Zenith Energy\n• Expanded Portland Street Response\n• Increased pedestrian safety with new sidewalks and stop \nsigns\n• Funded Rent Assistance and lowered affordable housing \nrents\n• Cut red tape to speed up housing production\n• Banned AI rent fixing\n• Banned masking for law enforcement\n• Pushed for cost controls on Bull Run to prevent water rates \ndoubling \n• Demanded transparency and oversight in city contracts\nWhat I plan to do next:\n• Make the biggest corporations pay their fair share\n• Create permanently-affordable housing\n• Protect taxpayers in the Moda deal\n• Guarantee collective bargaining rights for tenants\n• Use Portland Clean Energy Fund for urgent climate \nresiliency \n• Fund evidence-based solutions to homelessness\n• Invest in bottom-up economic development\n• Draw down fossil fuel storage and transfer at CEI Hub\n• Lower barrier to entry for street vendors and small \nbusinesses\nI’m proud to be endorsed by labor unions, community \norganizations, and leaders, including: \nPortland Democratic Socialists of America\nOregon Working Families Party\nSEIU Oregon\nOregon AFSCME \nPortland Association of Teachers\nTeamsters Joint Council No. 37\nPROTEC17\nNorth Coast States Carpenters Union\nBuilding Power for Communities of Color \nLatino Network Action Fund\nAPANO Action Fund\nOregon Sierra Club \nPro Animal Oregon\nNext Up Action Fund\nSen Khanh Pham\nRep Mark Gamba \nBeaverton City Councilor Nadia Hasan\nWashington County Commissioner Nafisa Fai\nSee complete endorsements list at mitch4portland.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Mitch Green PAC ID \n23365\nOlivia Clark\nOccupation: City Councilor, \nElected 2024\nOccupational Background: \nPolicy Director & Government \nLiaison, Governor John \nKitzhaber; TriMet Public \nAffairs Director; Oregon DEQ; \nCo-founder CASA farmworker \nhousing\nEducational Background: \nM.S., University of Oregon\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Governor \nKitzhaber’s Policy & Intergovernmental Director; Legislative \nDirector for three Oregon Mayors\nVOTE OLIVIA CLARK #1\nDear Neighbors:\nIt has been a privilege to serve as your City Councilor these last \ntwo years.  \nI would be honored to earn your #1 vote and continue to be \nthe voice of common sense on the Portland City Council.\nWe deserve an accountable, affordable and safe city!\nMy Priorities:\n• Improve 9-1-1 response times\n• End street camping and open-air drug use\n• Support small business and economic development\n• Clear obstacles to housing production\nMy Results: \n• Protected funding for police & fire\n• Passed Storefront Support Program for small business\n• Funded District 4 Neighborhood Associations with my office \nbudget\n• Initiated & unanimously passed the first maintenance plan for \nour parks, streets, fire stations & water sewer systems\n• Sponsored & passed Sidewalk Improvement and Pavement \nProgram \n• Delivered solutions to fix potholes & crumbling roads\n• Returned Sellwood Community House to the community\nJOIN US: VOTE OLIVIA #1\nwww.OliviaforPortland.com\nPortland Firefighters Association\nPortland Police Association\nNW Oregon Labor Council\nIBEW Local 48\nIronworkers Local 29\nColumbia Pacific Building & Construction Trades\nAFSCME Local 189\nOregon AFSCME \nSEIU Oregon\nNorth Coast States Carpenters Union\nUFCW 555\nUnited for Portland\nThe Street Trust Action Fund\nFuture Portland Action Fund\nMultiFamily Northwest\nGovernor John Kitzhaber\nMayor Keith Wilson\nSenators Kate Lieber, Rob Wagner & Lisa Reynolds\nRepresentatives Ben Bowman & Rob Nosse\nDistrict Attorney Nathan Vasquez\nMetro Councilors Duncan Hwang & Christine Lewis\nJennifer Daneluk, Ross Island Grocery & Cafe\nMike Roach, Paloma Clothing, Hillsdale\nJim Rice, Fields Sports Bar\nNeighborhood Leaders: \nBruce Studer, Pearl District\nLeslie Hammond, Marita Ingalsbe, Hayhurst \nMarianne Fitzgerald, Ashcreek-Crestwood\nArdys Dunn, Sellwood\nBert VanGorder, South Waterfront\nLisa Caballero Southwest Hills \nMichael Kaplan, South Portland\nDr. Marian Hodges, MD, Eastmoreland\nNancy Hiser, Linnton, Community Advocate\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Olivia (Committee ID 23199)\n\nWC-28\nCouncil Member, District 4 Council Member, District 4\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJayne Cronlund\nOccupation: Small business \nowner\nOccupational Background: \nNonprofit executive director, \nschool community organizer, \nfamily caregiver, AmeriCorps \nmember\nEducational Background: \nB.A.  Temple University; J.D. \nNortheastern University School \nof Law\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Southwest \nNeighborhoods Inc. 2nd Vice President, Oregon League of \nConservation Voters Clackamas County, Willamette Women \nDemocrats Board\nI am a listener and an experienced coach that helps people \nfind solutions to their toughest challenges.  I will use the power \nof collaboration and shared leadership to solve the complex \nproblems our city faces. \n Portland is unaffordable AND we are faced with a mental health \nand addiction crisis.   I have a plan to move toward a more \nflourishing future.\nI will stand strong for:\n• Economic recovery for our city by securing living wage \njobs that reflect the city’s creative, unique and sustainable \nreputation.\n• Sustained investment in safe, shared common spaces like \nparks, trails, natural areas and business districts.\n• Unified city government by developing shared goals for \nour limited resources, taking action to support those goals and \nconsistently reporting back to rebuild trust with my constituents.\nI support Jayne.  She knows how the city works and will \nwork collaboratively to get things done.\nAmanda Fritz, Former Portland City Commissioner\nJayne helped build the collaborative public private \npartnership foundation for the Backyard Habitat \nCertification Program while serving as Executive Director \nof the Three Rivers Land Conservancy.\nTerri Preeg Riggsby, West Multnomah Soil & Water \nConservation District Board Chair\nJayne is adept at working through complex problems to \nfind solutions and leads with her values. \nSue Marshall, Clark County Council Chair\nJayne’s steady inclusive presence during COVID sustained \na strong social fabric for our school community during a \nvery isolating time.\nAmy Beacom Ed.D, founder Center for Parental Leave \nLeadership\nJayne inspires people to find unseen resources to reach \ncommunity goals.\nAndrea Kappa, founder Chip Drop and Freya Apps\njayneforaflourishingfuture.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Jayne for Portland\nJohn McDonald\nOccupation:\nTriMet Hydro Technician \nOccupational Background:\nJournalist \nEducational Background:\nTroy University, B.S. Political \nScience. \nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Bay County \n(Fla.) Community Health Task \nForce, Broward County (Fla.) \nDemocratic Party Precinct Committeeman, PBOT Transportation \nAmbassador.  \nPortland Is Ready for a Comeback\nMy ask is simple: your vote.\nYour vote if you believe Portland can once again be a city that \nworks — a city with clean, safe streets, thriving neighborhoods, \nreliable transportation, affordable housing and an economy that \ncreates opportunity.\nYour vote if you believe we should recruit companies that will \nbring good paying jobs to Portland, expand our workforce and \ninvest in the people who already call this city home.\nYour vote if you believe affordable housing requires more than \npromises and homelessness demands both compassion and \naccountability.\nI believe Portland is poised for a renaissance. But a renaissance \ndoesn’t happen by itself. It takes leadership willing to bring \ngovernment, labor, business and the community to the same \ntable — and then insist on results.\nA vote for John McDonald is a vote to get Portland moving \nagain.\nJohn’s Commitments to Portland\nKeep the Trail Blazers in Portland — and modernize the \nModa Center.\nEnsure the Interstate Bridge Replacement Project stays on \ntrack.Repair our streets and clean up our transit system.\nExamine department budgets, inspect facilities and root out \nwaste, fraud and abuse. \nChange how Portland confronts homelessness. Limit \nnew contracts and demand accountability from current \nproviders. \nWhy John?\nJohn McDonald knows Portland from the street level up.\nHe is an award-winning journalist and published author. He has \noperated light-rail vehicles, managed national park bus fleets \nand is a proud member of ATU Local 757.\nHe understands the people who keep a city running because he \nis one of them.\nPortland can come back. Portland can work again. And \nDistrict 4 can help lead the way.\nThis Information Furnished By: John McDonald\n\nWC-29\nCouncil Member, District 4 Council Member, District 4\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nEric Zimmerman\nOccupation: City Councilor; \nLieutenant Colonel, Oregon \nNational Guard\nOccupational Background: \nChief of Staff, Commissioner \nJulia Brim-Edwards; Portland \nMayor’s Office; Deputy City \nManager; 19 years military \nservice\nEducational Background: \nUniversity of Portland, BS, MBA\nPrior Government \nExperience: US Embassy \nBangladesh, NATO Kosovo, Veterans Task Force, League of \nCities LGBTQ Caucus\nIt’s time to get Positive about Portland!\nPortland should be the City that says YES. We must rally as a \ncommunity and deliver on housing, affordability, public safety, \nand safer streets. As an out gay veteran in Portland, I’m fighting \nfor my hometown.\nYES to Public Safety\nI fought every attempt to defund public safety. I increased \nPortland Street Response. I protected Portland Police and \nPortland Fire Rescue.\nYES to Affordability\nMy record is clear, I passed tax cuts, and voted against tax \nincreases like the Arts Tax 42% hike and new street fee. I’m \nfocused on building and lowering the cost of housing, not \nraising taxes.\nYES to Camp Cleanups\nHomelessness impacts every neighborhood. Shelters, \nenforcement, camp cleanups, and mental health and addictions \ntreatment are all important. I’m leading on all three.\nYES to Parks, Sidewalks, Pothole Repair\nI voted to return maintenance workers to the front line in our \nparks and on our streets. Repairs aren’t an option, they’re a \ncommitment!\nLABOR, BUSINESS, & COMMUNITY LEADERS \nsay YES to Eric\nPortland Firefighters Local 43 \nPortland Police Association \nSEIU Oregon \nOregon AFSCME \nNW Oregon Labor Council \nUFCW 555 \nUnited For Portland \nColumbia Pacific Building & Construction Trades Council  \nNorth Coast States Carpenters Union \nGovernor John Kitzhaber \nCongresswoman Janelle Bynum \nSecretary of State Tobias Read \nMayor Keith Wilson \nSenate President Rob Wagner \nHouse Majority Leader Ben Bowman \nSenators Kate Lieber, Lisa Reynolds \nRepresentatives Dacia Grayber, Shannon Isadore, Travis \nNelson, Daniel Nguyen, Emerson Levy, Mari Watanabe, April \nDobson \nDistrict Attorney Nathan Vasquez \nCommissioner Julia Brim-Edwards \n \nIBEW 48 \nIronworkers Local 29  \nLIUNA Local 737 (Laborers) \nUA 290  \nSMART 16  \nPortland Metropolitan Association of Realtors  \nGo Portland Go \nLGBTQ+ Victory Fund\nPlease rank me #1\nwww.EZ4PDX.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Eric Zimmerman\nJamey Evenstar\nOccupation: Public Servant\nOccupational Background: \nPortland City Council Chief of \nStaff, Policy Director, Analyst\nEducational Background: \nBachelor’s, Psychology\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: See “Occupational \nBackground”\nBig Glasses, Clear Vision\nIt’s time we had a City Council that believes in Portland. \nWe need more working-class voices on Council to stand up \nto a fascist regime, have a backbone against special monied \ninterests, and refuse to accept the status quo.\nWe’re all frustrated by a lack of progress on important issues \nfacing our city. But Portland can do hard things. All it takes is \none new progressive leader to break the deadlock on Council \nand make real, lasting, positive change. \nI’ve worked for the City of Portland for almost a decade doing \nhard things and proving what’s possible: \n• Tenant protections to increase access to housing.\n• Buses out of traffic to make transit easier and faster.\n• Transition to a new form of government to improve \nrepresentation.\nAll while raising five kids (including one with developmental \ndisabilities) as a renter and a car-free transit rider. I have the \nreal-world experience to stay focused on what matters most: \nbuilding stable homes, stable families, and stable lives.\nEndorsements\nElecteds\nCouncil President Jamie Dunphy\nCouncilor Angelita Morillo\nSen. Khanh Pham\nRep. Mark Gamba\nMetro Councilor Duncan Hwang\nPPS Board Chair Eddie Wang\nPPS Director Christy Splitt\nPPS Director Rashelle Chase-Miller\nMESD Member Danny Cage\nLeaders\nJR Lilly, Native American community leader\nCassie Wilson, disability justice advocate\nJim Labbe, participatory budgeting advocate\nHelmi Hisserich, social housing expert\nSteph Routh, Planning Commissioner\nAshley Schofield, public education champion\nXavier Stickler, downtown community leader\nLisa Freeman, former District 4 candidate\nChad Lykins, former District 4 candidate\nOrganizations\nOregon AFSCME\nAFSCME Local 189\nPortland For All\nBuilding Power for Communities of Color\nAPANO Action Fund\nLatino Network Action Fund\nThe Ebony Collective Coalition\nNext Up Action Fund\nSunrise PDX\nSierra Club\nThe Street Trust Action Fund\nPro-Animal Oregon\nGray Panthers\nComplete list of endorsements and more information at \nevenstarforportland.com \n   \nThis Information Furnished By: Evenstar for Portland\n\nWC-30\nTrack Your Ballot!\nSign up to receive updates and \nnotifications about your ballot from \nWashington County Elections. \nReceive text, email, or voice alerts \nwhen we have mailed your ballot,\n when your ballot has been received \nat the Elections oﬀice, and when \nyour ballot has been accepted \nand counted.\n \nSign up at\nwashcovotes.ballottrax.net/voter\nor scan this QR code\nThe Washington County ballot \ntracking program is a partnership \nwith i3ballot. If you have any \nquestions about this service, \nplease call Washington County Elections \nat (503) 846-5800 or email us at \nelections@washingtoncountyor.gov\nSign up today to begin\nreceiving notifications \non your ballot!\nCouncil Member, District 4\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nEli Arnold\nOccupation: Bicycle Cop\nOccupational Background: \nPublic Safety and Military\nEducational Background: \nPortland State University, BS; \nMilitary Intelligence Center,\nOregon Dept. of Public Safety \nStandards and Training\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Multnomah County \nDemocrats PCP, U.S. Army Blackhawk Pilot & Intelligence \nAnalyst, 2016 Bernie Sanders Delegate\nI’ve dedicated my life to hard work and serving others: As a \ncommunity police officer, Army combat veteran, progressive \nDemocrat and father of four.  \nRiding around Portland as a bike cop, I hear what people want: \nsafe streets, affordable transportation and housing, police \nwho show up when needed, and better results from their \ntax dollars. City government shouldn’t ignore core services \nor price people out of Portland. I’m running to focus on what \nmatters most. \n \nI’m a results focused, practical progressive: Progress \nrequires working together, setting ideology aside and solving \nproblems. I’ll break the stalemate by building common ground—\nstarting with the 1,900 affordable units sitting empty. Let’s cut \nthe bureaucracy, get people into homes, and move people from \ntents to shelter and services.\nAffordable: We can make Portland more affordable \nby cutting everyday household costs and expanding \ntransportation access. I support using existing clean \nenergy resources to move toward fareless transit, making \nit easier to get around without added cost.\nSafe: Basic services come first—public safety, clean \nstreets, and maintained parks. Portland has fewer sworn \nofficers than peer cities, and it shows in retail theft, gun \nviolence, reckless driving, and open-air drug use. I’ll \nprioritize staffing and coordination across police, fire, \ndispatch, and behavioral health response so help shows \nup when needed.\nThriving: Make Portland a place where families and \nbusinesses thrive. I will not support raising taxes and fees. \nInstead, I’ll push for accountability—measuring outcomes, \nreporting results, and ensuring every dollar delivers real \nvalue. \nRank Eli Arnold #1 \nPortland Fire Fighters’ Association, IAFF Local 43\nLisa Naito, former State Representative\nAmanda Fritz, former City Commissioner\nGo Portland Go\nLisa Caballero, Transportation Advocate\nBruce Studer, Community Organizer\nMike Lindberg, former City Commissioner\nBob Weinstein, Government Watchdog\nEliforPortland.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Eli Arnold\n\nWC-31\nCouncilor Councilor\nCity of RivergroveCity of Rivergrove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nLeah Silber\nOccupation: Local Software \nCompany CEO\nOccupational Background: \nManagement at Silicon Valley \nsoftware companies, focused \non Operations, Marketing and \nHR\nEducational Background: \nBrooklyn College, Accounting \nand Political Science\nPrior Governmental Experience: Rivergrove City Councilor \nsince 2024. VP, League of Oregon Cities Women’s Caucus. \nServed on regional committees, including ODOT’s Small Cities \nAllotment Advisory Committee.\nI believe in a better Rivergrove.\nWith roughly 220 households, we’re small enough that \neveryone who wants a voice should have one. Rivergrove can \nand should feel more like a community. We need a council that \nprioritizes harmony, competence and common ground.\nI’m raising a young family here, and I’m the only councilor or \ncandidate doing so. That shapes what I notice: playgrounds, \nsidewalks and safe routes to school, the traffic on Childs Road, \nand how walkable we are. We need safe and easy river access \nfor all, and to get our sewer, flood and other infrastructure in \norder. These details are what a city our size is all about, and \nwhat I believe we must prioritize.\nGood ideas need funding and partners. I’ve spent my term \nbuilding relationships with the Counties, Metro, the League \nof Oregon Cities, and anyone else willing to pick up the \nphone for a city our size. Those relationships are how small \ncities get money, projects, and seats at the table when larger \ngovernments make decisions for us.\nI also believe in spending carefully. I’m the only councilor \nwho voted against pitching a new Rivergrove tax, and in May, \nRivergrove rejected it decisively. Thank you! We should spend \non what residents need, get competitive bids before hiring, start \nfollowing budget law, and act on everything the recent audit \nfound.\nMost of all, I believe residents deserve to be heard. I’ve helped \nneighbors navigate issues big and small, and I’ll help anyone \nwho asks.\nI’m running alongside Joan Joyce and David Dull for a \nbetter Rivergrove.\nQuestions? Ideas? Call me at 971.396.8320. Days before the \nelection or years after, I’m here to help.\nwww.leahsilber.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Leah Silber\nDavid M. Dull\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nForty-five-year career in Sales \nand Operations management \nat Xerox Corporation.  I held \nnumerous management \npositions during my career \nand lead the Pacific Northwest \nSales Organization as Director \nof Production Sales for the last \neight years.\nEducational Background: UOP BA Business Management\nPrior Governmental Experience: Former City Councilor for the \nCity of Rivergrove for 2 terms.\nOptional Information:\n• President of the Wisteria Homeowners Association for 8 \nyears.\n• Board member for South Pasadena Booster Club, where \nwe raised over $300K a year for the local schools.\nIn both my professional and civic responsibilities, my success \ncame from finding the common ground we shared and then \nensuring the outcome reflected those principles.\nMy family has lived in Rivergrove for almost 25 years.  It’s \nimportant to me that we have streets and parks that are safe for \nour children and grandchildren.  I want to see the city work to \nencourage citizen involvement.  I want to see our city leaders \nout in the city and meeting face-to-face with our neighbors, \nincluding at the monthly City Council meetings.  \nAs a concerned citizen, I will ensure the Rivergrove City Council \nmakes fiscally responsible decisions and uses a common-sense \napproach to running the city.  If you share those goals for our \ncity then I hope you will vote for Leah Silber, Joan Joyce and \nDavid Dull.\nThis Information Furnished By: David M. Dull\n\nWC-32\nCouncilor Councilor\nCity of RivergroveCity of Rivergrove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJoan Joyce\nOccupation: Retired Associate \nPrincipal; CASA ( Court \nAppointed Special Advocate) \nGuardian ad Litem; Project \nPOOCH Board President \n(5013c with $400k annual \nbudget)\nOccupational Background: \nTeacher and Associate \nPrincipal, Tigard-Tualatin \nSchool District; Small Business \nOwner; Board Member, Columbia-Willamette YMCA; Board \nMember, Spinal Cord Injury Association of Oregon; Instructor/\nLecturer, Portland State University School of Education\nEducational Background: Portland State University, M.S., \nSpecial Education; School Administration Certificate; Oregon \nState University, B.A., Psychology; Elementary Education\nPrior Governmental Experience: Appointed by the Mayor to \nthe Rivergrove Park Advisory Committee (2024)\nOPTIONAL INFORMATION\nFor more than two decades, Rivergrove has been my home. \nI care deeply about our community and am running for City \nCouncil because I believe local government works best when \nit listens, communicates openly, and brings people together to \nsolve problems.\nI spent my 30 year career doing just that-bringing people \ntogether, listening, and tackling challenges. That collaborative \napproach guided me in my career as an educator, and in my \nvolunteer service to our community. It is the same approach I \nwill bring to City Council.\nAs President of the Project POOCH Board of Directors working \nwith a $400k annual budget and through my service as a Court \nAppointed Special Advocate (CASA) for children in foster care, \nI’ve worked with people from many different backgrounds to find \npractical solutions and build consensus.\nRivergrove deserves thoughtful leadership that values \ntransparency, respectful communication, and careful \nstewardship of public resources. I support decisions that are \nwell researched, fiscally responsible, and made with meaningful \npublic involvement. I also believe our City Council is strongest \nwhen members meet in person, while continuing to provide a \nvirtual option for residents.\nI’ve recently gotten to know David Dull and Leah Silber and \nhave come to respect their commitment to Rivergrove. We \nshare a common-sense, community-focused approach to \ncity government and a commitment to fiscally responsible \nleadership.\nPlease vote for Joan Joyce, David Dull, and Leah Silber for City \nCouncil. Together, we’ll listen, communicate openly, and make \nthoughtful decisions for our community.\nThis Information Furnished By: Joan Joyce\nBrett Egusa\nOccupation: Senior Legal \nEditor, Thomson Reuters\nOccupational Background: \nAssociate Chief Counsel, \nProcurement and Fiscal Law \nDivision, FEMA\nAssistant Dean (Reserves), US \nArmy JAG School\nEducational Background: Lewis & Clark Law School, JD\nEisenhower School, Master’s in NatSec\nUCSD, BA, PoliSci\nPrior Governmental Experience: Board Member (elected), \nTaylor Run Citizens Association, Alexandria, VA (2013-2020)\nI am running for city council because our city faces important \ndecisions in the next few years. Issues include updating \noutdated 1970’s ordinances, completing critical capital \nimprovement projects (boat ramp and Canal Road), improving \nsafety on Childs Road, and ensuring that grant funds are \nmanaged responsibly and effectively. For everything, we need \npractical solutions, thoughtful leadership, reasonable minds, and \na commitment to moving Rivergrove forward.\nMy professional background is uniquely suited to help the city \nnavigate these challenges. Throughout my career, both civilian \nand military, I have specialized in government procurement, \ngrants management, fiscal law, and public policy. I also bring \nprior experience in local government and community leadership \nas an elected Board Member of the Taylor Run Citizens \nAssociation in Alexandria, Virginia (2013-2020).\nThis experience will help Rivergrove make sound decisions \nregarding public projects, financing, and stewardship of public \nfunds. In addition, my background in drafting policies and \nregulations can help modernize city ordinances, making them \nsimpler, clearer, and more reasonable for residents while \npreserving our community’s character.\nI grew up in the Lake Oswego area and attended Lakeridge \nHigh School. After my years of military and federal service, I \nreturned to Lake Oswego with my family so that my children \ncould have the same benefit of attending our wonderful schools \nand experiencing our great community. We have a personal \nstake in Rivergrove’s future. I will work collaboratively with fellow \ncouncilors to ensure our city government operates effectively \nand responsibly, in the best interests of all residents.\nThis Information Furnished By: Brett Egusa\n\nWC-33\nFor more information \nabout voting in Oregon\nCALL\n1-866-ORE-VOTE/673-8683\nVISIT\nwww.oregonvotes.gov\nTTY\n1-800-735-2900\nfor the hearing impaired\n        ONLINE\n        VOTERS’\n        GUIDE \nThe information contained \nin this voters’ pamphlet is \nalso available in the \nOnline Voters’ Guide.\nEspañol\nUna versión en español de \nalgunas partes de la Guía del \nElector está a su disposición \nen el portal del Internet cuya \ndirección aparece abajo. \nConscientes de que este \nmaterial en línea podría no \nllegar adecuadamente a todos \nlos electores que necesitan \neste servicio, se invita a toda \npersona a imprimir la versión \nen línea y circularla a \naquellose electores que no \ntengan acceso a una \ncomputadora.  \nCouncilor\nCity of RivergroveCity of Rivergrove\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nCurt Taylor\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nFinancial Advisor / Registered \nRepresentative\nEducational Background: \nAssociates Degree, Diablo \nValley College\nUS Navy - Nuclear Power \nSchool, Submarine Service\nPrior Governmental Experience: City of Rivergrove - \nCouncillor (current)\nCity of Rivergrove - Planning Commission\nI am seeking re-election to the Rivergrove City Council to \ncontinue the work on the many positive opportunities the city \nhas before it. These opportunities include renovation of the \nStark Boat Ramp, upgrade access on Canal Road, and a \ncomprehensive review and update of the Rivergrove Land \nDevelopment Ordnance. Rivergrove is a very small city with \na volunteer unpaid council. Continuity of the current council is \ncritical to move these and other city improvements forward. \nThis Information Furnished By: Curt Taylor\nNo Photo \nSubmitted\n\nWC-34\nMayor Council Member\nCity of SherwoodCity of Sherwood\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nTim Rosener\nOccupation: Self-Employed: \nManagement Consultant\nOccupational Background: \nThroughout my thirty-six-year \ncareer, I have focused on \ndelivering innovative technology \nand consulting services to local \ngovernments across the United \nStates. During this time in the \nGovernment Technology Sector, \nI dedicated myself to helping local governments provide quality \nservices with efficiency and transparency.\nEducational Background: Community College of the Air \nForce.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Served Honorably, USAF \n37th Aerospace Rescue and Recovery Squadron (1984 - 1987) \nand USAF Reserves 939th CAMS (1987 - 1992). Mayor 2023 \n– Present, City Council President 2019-2022, Sherwood City \nCouncilor 2017 – 2018, Oregon Broadband Advisory Council \nChair, League of Oregon Cities Board, FCC Intergovernmental \nAdvisory Committee. Vice President League of Oregon Cities. \nRegional Solutions Advisory Committee member.\nOptional \nAs a long-time resident of Sherwood and your Mayor, I am \ndedicated to preserving the unique character that makes \nour city a special place to live. While growth and change are \ninevitable, we must retain local control over our future. I am \ncommitted to ensuring that decisions about our community are \nmade by those who live here, not by outside special interests, \nMetro, or Salem.\nI have worked hard to bring new amenities to Sherwood, \nincluding the Pedestrian Bridge, the New Robin Hood Festival \nPlaza, and the Skate Park, with more to come!\nServing our community in other ways, such as volunteering \non the boards of Oregon Space Camp Adventures and the \nSherwood Education Foundation, continues to be a passion \nof mine. I am also a proud founding member of the Pirates of \nPinehurst’s annual Halloween show and Gold Hunt.\nSherwood is more than just a place on the map; it’s a \ncommunity where all are welcome. Together, we can keep \nSherwood the place we all love to call home.\nThis Information Furnished By: Tim Rosener\nTaylor Giles\nOccupation: Small Business \nOwner, www.DigaPix.com;\nOccupational Background: \nOwner & Operator, Digapix.com \n(media digitization services); \nHealthcare & Software Product \nExecutive\nEducational Background: \nUtah Valley University, B.S. in \nComputer Science\nPrior Governmental Experience: Sherwood City Councilor \n(2022–present); Chair, Sherwood School District Bond Oversight \nCommittee (3 years); Sherwood Planning Commissioner (2 \nyears)\n• Small Biz Roots: I own and run Digapix.com, a local \nsmall business helping families and organizations preserve \ntheir memories by digitizing video, film, and photos right \nhere in town, so I know exactly what it takes to balance a \nbudget and keep things running smoothly.\n• Local Dad & Neighbor: My family and I have been \nSherwood locals since ‘03. We’ve raised five kids here and \nlove giving back through sports and the arts.\n• Housing for Everyone: We need homes that work for \nyoung families just starting out and for our seniors who \nwant to stay in the community they love.\n• Smart Spending: I’m big on being transparent with your \ntax dollars and focusing on the essentials—like great \ninternet and safe trails.\n• Safety First: Keeping our police, libraries, and parks top-\nnotch so Sherwood stays the best place to live.\n• I’m All Ears: I actually share my cell number with all my \nconstituents because I want to hear your ideas directly. \nI’d love to keep working for you!\nThis Information Furnished By: Taylor Giles\n\nWC-35\nMayor Mayor\nCity of TigardCity of Tigard\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nYi-Kang Hu\nOccupation: Mayor of Tigard; \nAttorney; Small Business \nOwner\nOccupational Background: \nResearch Scientist\nEducational Background: \nJD, Lewis & Clark Law \nSchool; Ph.D., Biochemistry & \nMolecular Biology, OHSU; BA, \nReed College\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tigard City Council \nPresident; Tigard Planning Commission President; Tigard \nTransportation Advisory Committee\nCommunity Service: Immigration Counseling Service; Tigard \nChamber of Commerce Diversity and Community Engagement \nCommittee \n \nYi-Kang Hu is a tireless advocate for Tigard. He puts \ncommunity voices first, brings people together, and solves \nproblems. \n \nSince becoming Mayor, Yi restored stability to the City Council, \nrebuilt city leadership, led efforts to close a $6.5 million budget \nshortfall while protecting essential services, and helped \nadvance Tigard’s first Economic Development Strategic Plan. \n \nYi will lead Tigard into its next chapter, with three clear \npriorities:\n• Affordability: Control costs for residents through \nresponsible budgeting. Expand housing options for every \nbudget and stage of life.\n• Opportunity: Make it easier to do business with the \ncity. Help local businesses and entrepreneurs grow. \nCreate good-paying jobs. Revitalize downtown and key \ncommercial districts.\n• Livability: Ensure reliable public safety services continue. \nImprove transportation safety for pedestrians, cyclists, and \nmotorists. Strengthen parks, public spaces, and library \nservices.\nTigard needs leadership that works for the whole city, has \nstrong relationships across our region, and is ready to lead on \nday one. Vote for Yi to keep Tigard moving forward.\nENDORSEMENTS\n• Tualatin Valley Firefighters Union\n• United Food and Commercial Workers Local 555\n• Oregon League of Conservation Voters\n• Maureen Wolf, Tigard City Council President\n• Jake Schlack and Jeanette Shaw, Tigard City Councilors\n• Jason Snider, Washington County Commissioner; Former \nTigard Mayor\n• John Cook, Former Tigard Mayor\n• Courtney Neron Misslin, State Senator\n• Ben Bowman, House Majority Leader\n• Sue Rieke Smith, State Representative\n• Nafisa Fai and Pam Treece, Washington County \nCommissioners\n• Juan Carlos González, Metro Council President\n• Mayors Lisa Batey (Milwaukie), Frank Bubenik (Tualatin), \nJoe Buck (Lake Oswego), Shawn O’Neil (Wilsonville), \nand Malynda Wenzl (Forest Grove)\n• Kevin Barton, Washington County District Attorney\n• Caprice Massey Washington County Sheriff\nFor a complete list of endorsements: www.yikanghu.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Yi-Kang Hu\nBill Monahan\nOccupation: Consultant\nOccupational Background: \n30 years in city government; \nattorney; consultant\nEducational Background: \nJD – Lewis and Clark College \nLaw School\nMCP – University of Rhode \nIsland\nBA – Stonehill College\n                     \nPrior Governmental Experience: Tigard – City Manager, \nCommunity Development Director\nMilwaukie – City Manager\nBrockton, MA – City Planner\nAbout Bill Monahan\nI have lived in Tigard since 1982, serving as Planning Director \nuntil 1988 and City Manager from 1994 to 2005. I also served \nas Milwaukie City Manager, as an attorney for several Oregon \ncities, and own a Tigard business. My wife Linda and I have two \nchildren, Tigard High graduates.\nWhy I’m running for Tigard Mayor\nTigard has lost its way. Council turmoil, a mayor’s resignation, \nstaff departures, investigations, and repeated departure of \ncity managers motivated me to act. When the current mayor \nled Council to place a $150 million Police and Public Works \nmeasure on the ballot, I knew Tigard needed change.\nVoters overwhelmingly rejected the overpriced, poorly timed \nmeasure after the city spent millions on land and design before \nseeking voter approval.\nThe police need a better facility, but city leaders must be more \ntransparent and honest. Tigard needs an experienced leader \nwho ties sound analysis and reliable numbers to real needs. \nHow my experience prepared me to serve\nAs Tigard city manager, I helped develop sound financial \npolicies and managed an efficient. We met facility needs within \nbudget, secured land, completed preliminary design, and won \nvoters’ approval for the Tigard Library. I later helped develop two \nsuccessful Milwaukie bond measures. My practical, results-\noriented municipal experience sets me apart - with insight into \nproven, affordable solutions for Tigard.\nMy priorities when elected\n• Lead with integrity, transparency, and accountability\n• Develop reasonable police facility solutions\n• Deliver affordable core services\n• Build strong relationships with Council, staff, and the \ncommunity\nTigard’s current leadership is headed in the wrong direction.  \nVote for Bill Monahan.  Let’s Bring Back a Better Tigard!\nLearn more www.citizenstoelectbillmonahan.com. \nThis Information Furnished By: Bill Monahan\n\nWC-36\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of TigardCity of Tigard\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJohn           \nGoodhouse\nOccupation: Independent \nInsurance Broker, Owner\nOccupational Background: \nSmall Business Owner, \nDowntown Tigard\nEducational Background: \nBachelor’s in Management/\nCommunications/Leadership; \nConcordia University\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tigard City Council, Past \nCouncil President; Tigard Planning Commission; Tigard \nChamber of Commerce \nGovernment & Public Policy; Past President, Men’s Auxiliary \nVFW Post 3452\nCommittee Appointments: National League of Cities \nTransportation Committee; Board Chair-Willamette River \nWater Coalition; League of Oregon Cities Finance Committee, \nNational League of Cities Board of Directors, Family Promise of \nTualatin Valley Board of Directors\nFamily: Married to Rebecca; Father to Meeka, Austin, and \nJasmine.\nKeeping Community First: “As a lifelong Tigard resident, my \ngreatest honor has been serving as a former city councilor in \nthe community where I grew up and where I’ve raised my kids. \nKeeping our community safe and livable continues to be my top \npriority.” John Goodhouse for Tigard City Council\nPriorities\n-Support New and Existing businesses in Tigard\n-Support the process for more private events in the City of \nTigard to drive economic growth, support local small business \nand foster a deep sense of belonging for all residents\n-Supporting our Police Department to make our community a \nsafer place to live, get around and raise our families\n-Support Downtown Tigard for the Tigard Community to shop, \neat, and enjoy local activities as well as entertainment\n-Work with builders and city planning to make the cost of \nhousing more affordable to streamline the process of building \nhomes\n-Improve transportation throughout Tigard, from sidewalks, bike \nlanes, trails connecting neighborhoods and traffic congestion\nProudly endorsed by Tigard Citizens whether you are a \nDemocrat, Republican, Independent or Non-Affiliated. John \nGoodhouse will represent you and work hard for you.\nRecognized nationally for his prior work on diversity issues \nby the National League of Cities for his efforts to see everyone \ninvolved and engaged in their communities.\nEndorsements See endorsements at John Goodhouse for \nTigard City Councilor on Facebook\n \nVote for John Goodhouse for Tigard City Councilor\nThis Information Furnished By: John Goodhouse\nSue Garino\nOccupation: Retired Public \nServant\nOccupational Background: \nExecutive Board Member, Tigard \nPolice Officers’ Association; \nTigard Police Department\nEducational Background: \nChico State University; Notre \nDame de Namur University\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Tigard Police \nDepartment: Property & Evidence Specialist; Community \nService Officer; Records Specialist\nCommunity Background: Summerfield resident and volunteer\nELECT SUE GARINO\nExperience. Accountability. Integrity.\nSue knows Tigard. She’s served our community, supported \npublic safety, and represented working people. Now, she’s \nrunning for City Council to keep Tigard safe, affordable, and \nresponsive to those who call it home.\n“Tigard is my forever home. Too many families, seniors, and \nsmall businesses are feeling squeezed and unheard. I’m running \nbecause they deserve a strong voice at City Hall.” –Sue Garino\n“Sue will be a leader for ALL of Tigard! She knows what our \ncommunity needs for a vibrant future: good paying jobs, thriving \nlocal businesses, and workforce housing.” –Jeanette Shaw, \nTigard City Councilor\nSue’s Priorities:\n• Limit the growth of taxes, fees, and spending. Focus \ntaxpayer dollars on essential services. Stop using one-time \nmoney for ongoing programs. Require measurable results \nfrom new spending.\n• Strengthen essential city services. Prioritize emergency \nservices and responders. Maintain streets, parks, and \ninfrastructure. Ensure reliable access to clean water.\n• Make City Hall work for residents. Streamline city \nprocesses. Improve customer service. Make sure when \nresidents call the City, someone answers the phone or \nfollows up promptly.\n• Advocate for Tigard’s aging residents. Promote \naccessible housing and transportation options. Protect our \nsenior center.\n“Sue found solutions to improve workplace conditions for her \nunion members and keep Tigard safe. She knows how to listen, \nfind common ground, and deliver results.” –Jamey McDonald, \nRet. Tigard Police Chief\nEndorsements:\n• Tualatin Valley Firefighters Union IAFF Local 1660\n• Tigard Police Officers’ Association\n• Maureen Wolf, Tigard City Council President\n• Jeanette Shaw, Tigard City Councilor\n• Jake Schlack, Tigard City Councilor\n• Jason Snider, Washington County Commissioner, Former \nTigard Mayor\n• John Cook, Former Tigard Mayor\n• Valerie Pratt, Tualatin City Council President\n• Jamey McDonald, Ret. Tigard Police Chief\n• Kathy McAlpine, Ret. Tigard Police Chief\nwww.suefortigard.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Sue Garino\n\nWC-37\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of TigardCity of Tigard\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nGabriel Elijio           \nVelasquez\nOccupation: Clinic Services \nSpecialist, Legacy Medical\nOccupational Background: \nHealthcare; State and Local \nPolitics; Fundraiser\nEducational Background: \nBachelor of Science in Political \nScience, Portland State \nUniversity; Tigard High School\nPrior Governmental Experience: Chair, Tigard Town Center  \nAdvisory Commission (TCAC); TIF Grant Review Board\nExperienced | Dedicated | Lifelong Neighbor\nI am a healthcare worker and a local civic leader, currently \nserving as Chair of Tigard’s Town Center Advisory Commission \nand as a member of the City Center Grant Approval Committee.\nMy Priorities for Tigard\nAffordable Housing\n• Extend the ban on corporate single-family home buyers, as \nTigard’s Homes are for families, not for investor portfolios.\n• Bridge the gap between renters and owners so working \nfamilies can thrive in their own communities.\nSafe Streets\n• Collaborate with municipal, state, and federal leaders on a \nsingle strategy to ensure everyone has shelter and no one \nsleeps on Tigard’s streets.\n• Create a comprehensive, city-wide network of protected \nbike lanes and continuous sidewalks.\nA Thriving Economy\n• Streamline zoning to turn underutilized parking lots into \nvibrant hubs for small businesses and housing. \n• Partner with local businesses to establish community \nspaces that expand fresh food access citywide.\n“I know Gabe will do everything he can for working families. As \nChair of the TCAC, he has worked tirelessly to make Tigard a \nmore affordable, safe, and thriving city and will continue to do \nso as a City Councilor for Tigard.” \n- Karen Emerson, Tigard Chamber First Citizen & Former \nTTSD Board Member\nEndorsements:\n• Oregon League of Conservation Voters\n• Washington County Democrats\n• Oregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\n• Washington County Commissioner, Nafisa Fai\n• State Representatives: Nelson and Chaichi\n• Metro Councilor, Miles Palacios\n• Former TTSD Board Member, Karen Emerson\n• Former Public Safety Advisory Board Member, Jimmy \nBrown\n• Vice Chair of the TCAC, Bob Tomasovic\n• Public Safety Levy Campaign Leader, Patty Lofgren\n• THS Marching Programs Director, Katie McKee\n• Business Owner & Teacher, Sascha Newell\n• Community Advocate, Rick Love\nFor more info, visit: gabevelasquez.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Gabriel Velasquez\nJeff Darland\nOccupation: Stay-at-home \nParent\nOccupational Background:\nInternational Engineering \nLeadership\nAdvanced Manufacturing\nResearch and Development\nEducational Background:\nThe Johns Hopkins University, B.S.\nMontana State University, M.S.\nPrior Governmental Experience:Tigard Budget Committee\nFollow the Money. Plan for the Future. Build a Tigard That \nLasts.\nMy path to public service began with a water bill.\nRepeated rate increases prompted me to follow the numbers \nthrough Tigard’s water infrastructure, debt, and capital plans. \nThat work led directly to the Budget Committee, where I \nidentified gaps in how growth pays for itself.\nResidents should not need to be financial experts to understand \nwhat their city charges them or how their money is spent.\nMy manufacturing career took me across Asia and Europe. \nI learned to break down complicated systems, question \nassumptions, trace costs, and focus on results.\nI stepped away from my career to care for my young children. \nThey are growing up in Tigard and I want it to be a place where \nthey can stay, build a life, and put down roots. Today’s decisions \nshape their future.\nOn the City Council, I will focus on three commitments:\nClearer Government. I will push for plain-language \nexplanations of utility rates, debt, and major investments. You \ndeserve to know what you pay for and receive.\nLasting Affordability. Affordability requires more than simply \nbuilding housing. I support thoughtful growth, and will pursue \npartnerships using public land and community land trusts to \ncreate homes with lasting affordability.\nCommunity-Focused Growth. New growth should pay its \nfair share, and deliver lasting value: walkable neighborhoods, \nprotected natural areas, safe streets, and public spaces that \nbring people together.\nI will approach every proposal with an open mind, follow the \nevidence, listen to those affected, and explain my decisions. \nI will support good ideas and question those that fail local \nresidents and businesses.\nIf I earn your vote, you will have a Councilor who follows the \nmoney, asks useful questions, and plans beyond the next \nbudget cycle.\nThis Information Furnished By: Jeff Darland\n\nWC-38\nCouncil Member Council Member\nCity of TigardCity of Tigard\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nTom Anderson\nOccupation: Principal Real \nEstate Broker\nOccupational Background: \nSmall Business Owner\nEducational Background: BA, \nWashington State University\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Tigard City \nCouncil (2016–2020, 2026–\nPresent); Tigard Planning \nCommission (2007–2013), \nPresident and Vice President; Tigard Homelessness Task Force\nCommunity Leadership: Tigard Chamber of Commerce \nGovernment & Public Policy Committee; Tigard Downtown \nAlliance; Rise Church, Session President; Tigard Rotary Club, \nFormer President; Tigard Basketball Association, Former Board \nMember; Tigard Little League and Junior Baseball Organization \nCoach; Tualatin Valley Community Band; Overnight Homeless \nShelter Volunteer\nELECT TOM ANDERSON\nPROVEN LEADERSHIP. LOCAL PRIORITIES.\nTom wants Tigard to remain a community where people can \nafford to live, businesses can succeed, and families can \nput down roots. To keep Tigard moving forward, he will:\n• Expand Housing Options: Encourage entry-level and \nmiddle housing to improve affordability.\n• Grow Tigard’s Economy: Partner workforce development \nprograms with industrial spaces to fill vacant facilities and \ncreate good jobs.\n• Improve Local Roads: Advocate for state transfer of Hall \nBlvd. with corridor upgrades to city.\n• Revitalize Downtown: Address parking needs to support \nbusinesses and residents.\n• Enhance Public Safety: Improve facility conditions for \nemergency services and responders.\n• Protect Green Spaces: Maintain service levels and \nsecure sustainable funding for parks, trails, and ballfields.\n“Tom consistently steps up for our community. Since his return \nto City Council, he has provided the stability, thoughtfulness, \nand experience needed as we move Tigard forward. He’s \nearned my vote.” Maureen Wolf, Tigard City Council \nPresident\n“When Tigard’s kids needed better baseball and softball fields \nat Cook Family Park, Tom listened, secured grant funds, and \nnavigated an intricate permitting process to make it happen.” \nKevin Robinson, Former Tigard Little League Board \nMember\nEndorsements\n• Maureen Wolf, Tigard City Council President\n• Jeanette Shaw, Tigard City Councilor\n• Jake Schlack, Tigard City Councilor\n• Jason Snider, Washington County Commissioner, Former \nTigard Mayor\n• John Cook, Former Tigard Mayor\n• Craig Dirksen, Former Metro Councilor, Former Tigard \nMayor\n• Liz Newton, Former Tigard City Councilor\n• Frank Bubenik, Tualatin Mayor\n• Roy Rogers, TVF&R Board Director, Former Washington \nCounty Commissioner\nwww.tomandersontigard.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Elect Tom Anderson\nYousef Allouzi\nOccupation: Data Analyst \nOccupational Background: \nOregon Judicial Department\nEducational Background: BS \nEconomics, Magna Cum Laude, \nOregon State University\nMaster of Public Policy, Oregon \nState University\nPrior Governmental Experience: City of Tigard Budget \nCommittee\nCommunity Volunteer Experience: Small Claims Court \nMediator\nFamily Promise of Tualatin Valley\nMuslim Educational Trust\nBuilding a Tigard That Delivers for Everyone \n \nAs the federal administration attacks local communities, Tigard \nneeds bold leaders who will protect neighbors and ensure \nresidents have the resources they need to thrive. \n \nYousef is running for City Council to bring fresh ideas and \npractical experience as Tigard confronts growth, affordability, \nand safety. He has lived here for more than 25 years, served \non Tigard’s Budget Committee, and built his career around data \nand public policy. He believes elected officials should be directly \naccountable to the people they serve. \n \nOur environment, public spaces, and neighborhoods should not \nbe treated as commodities for billionaires. Growth should make \nTigard more livable for everyone. \n \nSafe Streets for All: Families should be able to reach schools, \nlibraries, parks, and neighborhoods safely. Yousef supports \nstreets and crossings designed around people, not just traffic. \n \nHousing You Can Afford: Tigard needs policies that create \nmultigenerational affordable housing and help people remain in \nthe community they call home. \n \nPolicies That Center People: Government should be a fair \nsystem of cooperation. Yousef will bring a thoughtful, evidence-\nbased approach to city decisions that puts residents first. \n \nEndorsements: \nWashington County Democrats \nOregon League of Conservation Voters \nAPANO Action Fund \nOregon Futures Lab & Color PAC\nThe Ebony Collective Coalition \nMetro Council President Juan Carlos Gonzalez \nMetro Councilor Duncan Hwang \nState Senator Kayse Jama \nState Senate Democratic Nominee Myrna Muñoz \nState Representative Mari Watanabe \nState House Democratic Nominee Tammy Carpenter \nMount Hood Community College Board Member Mohamed \nAlyajouri \nFormer Tigard City Councilor Jairaj Singh \nBeaverton City Councilors Edward Kimmi & Nadia Hasan \nTigard–Tualatin School Board Members Crystal Weston & \nDavid Jaimes \n \nwww.yousefallouzi.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Yousef Allouzi\n\nWC-39\nRegistering to vote\nYou are qualified to register to vote in \nOregon if you can answer yes to the \nfollowing questions:\n Are you a resident of Oregon?\n Are you a US citizen?\n Are you at least 16 years of age?\nIf you are 16 years of age, you will not \nreceive a ballot until an election occurs \non or after your 18th birthday. \n \n/gid00042/gid00047/gid00032/gid00045\n/gid00019/gid00032/gid00034/gid00036/gid00046/gid00047/gid00045/gid00028/gid00047/gid00036/gid00042/gid00041\n/gid00010/gid00041/gid00033/gid00042/gid00045/gid00040/gid00028/gid00047/gid00036/gid00042/gid00041\nHow to register\nRegister to vote online at oregonvotes.gov \nor pick up a voter registration card at \nany of the following places:\n Any County Elections Oﬀice\n The Secretary of State’s Oﬀice\n Some state agencies such as \n the Division of Motor Vehicles\n A voter registration drive\n Some locations of the US Postal Service\n Libraries\nAfter completing your registration card, \nmail or bring it to our oﬀice in person.\nThe deadline for new registrations and party \nchanges is 21 days prior to Election Day.\nWhat information is \nrequired to register?\nTo complete your registration, you \nmust provide the following:\n Full legal name\n Home address\n Mailing address (if applicable)\n Date of birth\n Signature\n Oregon Driver’s License number, \n Identification Card number, or a \n Social Security number\nCouncil Member\nCity of TigardCity of Tigard\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nKate Ristau\nOccupation: Executive Director\nOccupational Background: \nNonprofit Board President; \nOrganizational Management \nand Business Development; \nHigher Education; Author\nEducational Background: \nMA, University of Oregon; BA, \nIllinois State University\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tigard Library Board (2017–\nPresent), Chair (2022–Present)\nA BETTER FUTURE TOGETHER\nKate is a nonprofit executive, community leader, 20+ year \nTigard resident, and a mom. A fierce advocate for library \nand community programs, Kate brings a record of strategic \nleadership, fiscal stewardship, practical solutions, and \ncommunity service. Her approach is straightforward: Listen first. \nPlan ahead. Work together.\nPriorities:\n• PLAN RESPONSIBLY by making smart budget decisions \nthat reflect our community’s values, protecting taxpayer \ndollars, and investing in strong city services. \n• PROTECT COMMUNITY SERVICES by investing in the \nTigard Public Library, funding recreation programs, and \nworking to ensure every child in Tigard has enough to eat.\n• SUPPORT HOUSING WE CAN AFFORD by encouraging \nstarter homes, workforce housing, and smart land use \ndecisions from the Tigard Triangle to River Terrace 2.0.\n• MAKE TIGARD SAFER AND MORE CONNECTED by \nimproving transportation and sidewalks on Hall Boulevard, \nHighway 99W, and SW 72nd Avenue, and investing in \nparks, trails, and public spaces across Tigard.\n• SUPPORT A THRIVING LOCAL ECONOMY by investing \nin downtown Tigard, supporting local businesses, and \nstreamlining permitting to make it easier to do business in \nTigard.\n“Kate brings the experience, thoughtful leadership, \nand commitment to community that Tigard needs. She \nunderstands the challenges facing our city and knows that \ngood local government means listening to residents, working \ncollaboratively, and making responsible decisions for the future.” \nJason Snider, Washington County Commissioner\nEndorsements\n• Jake Schlack, Tigard City Councilor\n• Jason Snider, Washington County Commissioner, Former \nTigard Mayor\n• John Cook, Former Tigard Mayor\n• Pam Treece, Washington County Commissioner\n• John Dugger, Beaverton City Council President\n• Ashley Hartmeier-Prigg, Beaverton City Councilor\n• Kevin Teater, Beaverton City Councilor\n• Jenny Stoffel, Tigard Library Board Vice Chair\n• Pam Michael, Tigard Library Board Member\n• Mary Bogert, Former Tigard Library Board Chair\n• Oregon League of Conservation Voters\nFor questions and complete endorsements, visit KateforTigard.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Kate Ristau\n\nWC-40\nMayor Mayor\nCity of TualatinCity of Tualatin\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nOctavio         \nGonzalez\nOccupation: Landscape \nBusiness Professional.\nOccupational Background: \nHOA landscape account \nmanagement; banking and \nfinance; small business owner.\nEducational Background: \nClackamas Community College; \nbusiness and landscape \nstudies.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tualatin City Councilor, \nelected 2022; Metropolitan Area Communications Commission \nmember; alternate: Washington County Consolidated \nCommunications Agency 911, Washington County Coordinating \nCommittee, Willamette River Water Coalition; Tigard-Tualatin \nSchool District Budget Committee, nine years, ending as vice \nchair.\nOCTAVIO GONZALEZ\nTUALATIN MAYOR\n \nFOCUS ON WHAT MATTERS\nAt City Hall, taxes and fees may look small on a spreadsheet. \nAt your kitchen table, they mean less for groceries, medicine, \nhome repairs, or keeping a small business open. I will protect \nhousehold budgets and oppose unnecessary costs.\nOn City Council, I am the only councilor to consistently oppose \nincreases in utility, permit, service, and business licensing \nfees; property taxes; and trash and recycling rates.\nBefore I support any tax, fee, position, or program, City Hall \nmust demonstrate need, full cost, why resources fall short, and \nhow results will be measured.\nAS MAYOR, I WILL:\nEssential services first: Support well-equipped police, safer \nneighborhoods and crossings, reliable roads and utilities, \nmaintained parks and facilities, and long-range planning that \nprevents costly surprises.\nLocal businesses: Help businesses succeed by simplifying \npermits, setting clear timelines, reviewing costly rules, and \nexpanding youth apprenticeships and workforce partnerships.\nResponsible growth: Match growth with infrastructure and \nservices that protect Tualatin’s quality of life.\nBorn in Mexico to immigrant parents, I have lived in Oregon \nover 30 years, including 15 in Tualatin. I became an American \ncitizen in 2021 and am a father of two. I served as Bridgeport \nElementary’s “crosswalk dad.”\nCity Hall should remain neutral, accountable, and focused on \ncore services. I will not seek or accept endorsements.\nTualatin’s best days are ahead when we listen, spend \ncarefully, and focus on what matters. Together, we can keep \nTualatin safe, affordable, and future-ready.\nYOUR VOICE. YOUR MONEY. YOUR CITY.\nOctavio4Tualatin.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Octavio Gonzalez\nValerie Pratt\nOccupation: \nChief Financial Officer\nOccupational Background: \nCertified Public Accountant, \nBusiness Owner\nEducational Background: \nPortland State University: \nBachelor of Science Business \nAdministration-Accounting.\nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Tualatin City \nCouncil President; City Council; \nClimate Action Plan Steering \nCommittee; Transportation \nSystem Plan Community Advisory Committee; Community \nPolice Foundation-President; Council Committee for Advisory \nAppointments; Budget Committee; Stafford Hamlet-Council \nLiaison; Core Area Parking District Board; Parks Advisory \nCommittee-Chair; Parks and Recreation Master Plan Advisory \nCommittee-Chair\nI am running for Mayor to continue my three decades of \ncommunity service in the wonderful city of Tualatin.\nI offer total commitment, continuous advocacy, and practical \nsolutions. I will work hard to listen to community needs, ensure \nefficient use of city funds, and build a safer, more vibrant \nTualatin that welcomes everyone.\n“Valerie listens to residents and works diligently to find solutions. \nShe will be a fantastic mayor”\n Catherine Holland, Tualatin Resident\nI am proud to have taken a leadership role in the following \nefforts:\n• Speaking out against tolling on I-205 \n• Adopting a parks master plan \n• Adopting  a Climate Action Plan \n• Thoroughly questioning city budgets and utility fee \nincreases\n• Supporting a new advisory committee focused on diverse \nperspectives\nMy goals as Mayor include:\n• Revitalizing Tualatin’s downtown core  making it a \ndestination of choice\n• Improved public transit and transportation system\n• Development of our parks system\n• Reducing home insecurity\n• Supporting local businesses and workforce development\nEndorsements:\nTualatin \nBridget Brooks, Councilor\nChristen Sacco, Councilor\nRobert Kellogg, Former Councilor\nBeth Dittman, Council Candidate\nMayors\nTim Rosener, Sherwood \nYi-Kang Hu, Tigard\nRory Bialostosky, West Linn\nShawn O’Neal, Wilsonville\nAdam Marl, Oregon City Mayoral Candidate\nRoy Rogers, Former Tualatin Mayor\nClackamas County\nPaul Savas, Commissioner\nSonya Fischer, Commissioner-Elect\nWashington County \nJason Snider, Commissioner\nPam Treece, Commissioner\nMetro\nJuan Carlos Gonzalez, President\nChristine Lewis, Councilor\nGerritt Rosenthal, Councilor\nOregon State\nCourtney Neron Misslin, Senator\nBen Bowman, Representative\nMark Gamba, Representative\nSue Reike Smith, Representative\nJules Walters, Representative\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Val Pratt\n\nWC-41\nCouncil Member, Position 1 Council Member, Position 5\nCity of TualatinCity of Tualatin\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nMaria Antonieta \nReyes\nOccupation: Director of \nPhilanthropy\nOccupational Background: \nI have spent more than \n25 years in nonprofit \nexecutive leadership, serving \norganizations including The \nSalvation Army, American Red \nCross, Oregon Zoo Foundation, \nand World Forestry Center.\nEducational Background: Bachelor of Science in Human \nServices, with a focus in Business Management.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tualatin City Councilor, \nserving two terms since 2019. I have also served on numerous \ncommunity committees focused on education, economic \ndevelopment, housing, transportation, parks, and public safety.\nIt is an honor to seek a third term on the Tualatin City Council. \nFor the past eight years, I have listened to residents, built \nstrong partnerships, and delivered meaningful progress on \nthe issues that matter most to our community. During my \nservice, we opened the beautiful Las Casitas neighborhood \npark and made strategic investments that protect Tualatin’s \nexceptional quality of life. Affordable and attainable housing \nhas remained a central priority. By supporting developments \nsuch as Autumn Sunrise and Plambeck, we have expanded \nopportunities for individuals and families to call Tualatin home. \nI will continue pursuing practical housing solutions that serve \nresidents at every stage of life while preserving the character of \nour neighborhoods. My work is not finished. Over the next four \nyears, I will focus on revitalizing our City Center, completing \nParks Bond projects, implementing our Transportation Plan, \nstrengthening public safety, and supporting local businesses \nand economic development. These priorities will help create \na more connected, resilient, and prosperous community. My \nleadership experience has taught me how to bring people \ntogether, manage resources responsibly, and turn vision into \nreality. I am proud of what we have accomplished together, and \nI am ready to continue the work. Tualatin is my home, the city I \nlove, and the city I want to live in forever. I ask for your vote so \nwe can build on our progress, meet the challenges ahead, and \ncreate an even stronger and more vibrant Tualatin for everyone. \nThis Information Furnished By: Maria Antonieta Reyes\nBeth Dittman\nOccupation: Small business \nowner\nOccupational Background: \nHigher education administration, \nLife coaching\nEducational Background: \nWillamette University, B.A. \n(2002); Oregon State University, \nEd.M. (2008)\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tualatin Parks & Recreation \nAdvisory Committee – Chair, Vice Chair; Bridgeport Elementary \nPTO – President, Vice President; Hazelbrook Middle School \nPSO – Sponsorship Chair; Tigard-Tualatin School District \nBudget Committee – Member; Tualatin Community Advisory \n(Urban Renewal) Committee – Member.\nShowing Up for Tualatin\nLocal government makes a difference in daily life. It’s where we \nsupport local businesses, strengthen neighborhoods, invest in \nparks and infrastructure, and build community where everyone \nfeels they belong. Progress happens when people show up and \nwork together.\nDedicated to Tualatin\nMy family chose Tualatin 16 years ago because it felt like a \nplace where neighbors care about one another. That sense of \ncommunity has deepened through every opportunity I have had \nto serve.\nWhether advocating for parks and green spaces, building \nstronger school communities, helping reimagine downtown, or \nmaking difficult budget decisions for schools, I’ve learned that \nthe best solutions come from listening, building relationships, \nand finding common ground. That’s the leadership I’ll bring to \nCity Council.\nInvesting in Our Future\nMy priorities are to keep Tualatin welcoming, fiscally \nresponsible, and forward-looking; a place where every resident \nknows their voice matters and thoughtful investments strengthen \nour community for generations. \nWhether supporting downtown business improvement programs, \nexpanding affordable housing so more workers can afford to \nlive here, connecting neighborhoods to retail, transit, parks and \ntrails, or investing in Riverfront Park and more lighted turf fields, \nI’ll focus on practical solutions that move Tualatin forward.\nAs a small business owner, parent of children in our public \nschools, and caregiver for an aging parent, I’ll represent our \nentire community and serve as a responsible steward of public \nresources.\nEndorsements: Adam Peterson, Bridget Brooks, Christen \nSacco, Christina George, Crystal Weston, David Jaimes, \nDemocratic Party of Clackamas County, Emma Gray, Jill \nZurschmeide, Kyli McLaughlin, Patricia Parsons, Ross Baker, \nSusan Noack, Tristan Irvin, Valerie Pratt, Washington County \nDemocrats.\nwww.voteforbethdittman.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Beth Dittman\n\nWC-42\nCouncilor Councilor\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nSam Scull\nOccupation: Business Owner; \nWilsonville City Councilor \nOccupational Background: \nGlobal Manufacturing Executive \nEducational Background: \nSouthern Illinois University, \nBS Industrial Technology; \nPepperdine University, MBA \nPrior Governmental \nExperience: Wilsonville \nPlanning Commission; Budget Committee (2 terms); \nDevelopment Review Board\nWhy I Serve — Protecting What We Love; Preparing for \nWhat’s Next.\nI care deeply about Wilsonville and the people who live and \nwork here. Years of community service have given me a strong \nunderstanding of how our city works and how to bring people \ntogether to solve problems. I believe local control succeeds \nwhen local leaders listen, make thoughtful, fiscally responsible \ndecisions, and treat every resident with respect — regardless \nof their viewpoint. As Wilsonville grows, I will work to keep \nresidents informed, involved, and confident that their voices \nare being heard while preserving the qualities that make our \ncommunity a great place to call home.\nKey Priorities\nFiscal Responsibility & Transparent Government\n• Prioritize fiscal management\n• Promote open, accountable government\nTransportation & Infrastructure\n• Improve roads, bridges, and mobility\n• Invest before infrastructure falls behind\nLocal Business & Economic Vitality\n• Support local small businesses and jobs\n• Reduce blight with responsible economic growth\nPublic Safety & Emergency Preparedness\n• Support police, fire, and emergency services\n• Strengthen community preparedness\nThoughtful, Well-Planned Community Growth\n• Balance growth with community needs\n• Oppose stand-alone Data Centers \nI believe in practical leadership grounded in decency, respect, \nand results. I listen to every voice while focusing on public \nsafety, affordability, transportation, and a vibrant Town Center.\nEndorsed by:\n \nCourtney Neron Misslin, our State Senator\nSue Rieke Smith, our State Representative\nSonya Fischer, Clackamas County Commissioner-elect\nShawn O’Neil, Wilsonville Mayor \nCaroline Berry, Wilsonville Council President \nAnne Shevlin, Wilsonville Councilor \nJulie Fitzgerald, former Wilsonville Mayor \nTim Knapp, former Wilsonville Mayor\nKristin Akervall, former Wilsonville Council President\nJoann Linville, former Wilsonville Councilor \nDave Mauk, Charbonneau HOA VP-Director \nJoe Harris, Legend Villebois HOA Director \nDick Spence, 2011 Wilsonville First Citizen\nFor a full list of endorsements see: Sam4Wilsonville.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Sam Scull\nAdrienne \nScritsmier\nOccupation: Educator, \nClackamas Community College\nOccupational Background: \nHigher Education, Food Security \nAdvocate\nEducational Background: \nEastern Oregon University, \nEducation, MS; University of \nOregon, Sociology, Political \nScience, BA\nPrior Governmental Experience: Wilsonville Budget \nCommittee; Wilsonville Sister City Advisory Board, Chair\nI love Wilsonville\nIt is where I grew up, graduated high school, found a great \njob, chose to raise my family, and where I want to retire. I am \nrunning for City Council to ensure that working families see \nthemselves reflected in local leadership. Wilsonville is a safe, \nsupportive community. I am dedicated to ensuring it remains that \nway for generations.\nTransparency and Trust\nEvery resident deserves to know how decisions are made. We \nneed leaders committed to safe schools, secure jobs, and \naffordability. Wilsonville must avoid passing unnecessary \nfinancial burdens onto residents.\nReliable Transportation\nI’ll advocate for state and federal funds to replace the Boone \nBridge before disaster strikes.  I support continued funding of \nSMART, safe routes to school, and addressing any I-5 spillover \ntraffic congestion.\nProtecting People and Resources\nI oppose new data centers and airport expansion. They \nwould damage our farmland, increase utility costs, and pollute \nthe air we breathe and water we drink.\nTown Center: Vision Alone Is Not Enough\nRevitalizing Town Center requires private owners willing to sell \nand developers ready to build.  We can make these private \ninvestments viable by putting strong infrastructure in place to \nsupport redevelopment that welcomes businesses, creates jobs, \nbuilds homes, and spurs economic growth. Wilsonville needs \nto get the work started.   \nEndorsements:\nIAFF Local 1660 Tualatin Valley Fire Fighters\nUnited Food and Commercial Workers Local 555\nDemocratic Party of Clackamas County\nCourtney Neron Misslin, State Senator\nSue Rieke Smith, State Representative\nSonya Fischer, County Commissioner-elect\nShawn O’Neil, Mayor\nCaroline Berry, City Council President\nAnne Shevlin, City Councilor\nMaegan Vidal, WLWV School Board Director\nKristin Akervall, former City Council President\nJoann Linville, former City Councilor\nBetty Reynolds, former Oregon School Board President\nRick Wallace, Wilsonville Pride founder\nhttps://www.adrienneforwilsonville.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Friends of Adrienne Scritsmier\n\nWC-43\nIt’s against the law to:\n \n sign another person’s ballot \n return envelope for them\n vote more than once in an \n election or cast a fraudulent ballot\n vote a ballot if you are not \n legally qualified to do so\n coerce, pressure or otherwise \n unduly influence another voter\n sell, oﬀer to sell, purchase or \n oﬀer to purchase another \n voter’s ballot\n obstruct an entrance of a building \n in which a voting booth or oﬀicial \n ballot dropsite is located\n deface, remove, alter or destroy \n another voter’s ballot, a posted \n election notice or election \n equipment or supplies\n attempt to collect voted ballots \n within 100 feet of an oﬀicial \n ballot dropsite\n establish a dropsite without \n displaying a sign stating \n “Not An Oﬀicial Ballot Dropsite”\nFor more information about voting in \nOregon or if you think your rights \nas a voter have been violated: \noregonvotes.gov\n1 866 673 VOTE / 1 866 673 8683\nse habla español\n1 800 735 2900\nfor the hearing impaired\nAny violations of the identified election \nlaws are subject to penalties ranging \nfrom Civil Penalties (Up to $250 per \nviolation), Class A Misdemeanors or \nClass C Felonies.\nCouncilor\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nJennie Hill\nOccupation: Real Estate \nAgent, Local Business Owner\nOccupational Background: \nGraphic Designer, Marketing \nDirector\nEducational Background: \nNewberg High School, HS \nDiploma \nArt Institute of Portland, BS in \nGraphic Design\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nCLARITY, COMMUNITY, AND VISION FOR WILSONVILLE\nI’m running for Wilsonville City Council because I care \nabout the community we call home. I envision a city where \nlocal businesses thrive, people and families feel connected, \nneighborhoods are safe and vibrant, and every resident has a \nstake in shaping our quality of life.\nI’ve built a business, led a team, managed budgets, navigated \ncomplex regulations, and helped hundreds of families make \nfinancial decisions. Every day, I listen, solve problems, and find \npractical solutions that move people forward.\nMy business experience has taught me that success requires \nfinancial stewardship, accountability, and a willingness to adapt. \nThese are principles I would bring to City Council.\nMY PRIORITIES FOR WILSONVILLE\nWhat should Wilsonville be known for?\nGreat cities are more than roads and budgets. They are places \nwhere people feel connected and proud to belong. Wilsonville \nneeds a cohesive vision for who we are and who we want to \nbecome. Residents should have more influence on how our \ncity develops than outside entities, which is why I endorsed \nMeasure 3-632.\nHealthy Businesses = Healthy Communities\nWilsonville succeeds when entrepreneurs feel confident \nopening businesses, employers invest here, and local \ncompanies grow without unnecessary obstacles. City \ngovernment should remove barriers, streamline processes, \ninvest wisely in infrastructure, and be a trusted partner to \nbusiness.\nTransparent and Conscientious Spending\nEvery dollar City Hall spends comes from someone who worked \nto earn it. As a small business owner, I expect spending to \nproduce results. Our tax dollars deserve the care and discipline. \nBefore asking residents for more money or taking on debt, we \nmust demonstrate responsible stewardship of what we already \nhave.\nMy family is invested in this community, and I believe \nWilsonville’s future can be better than its past. I would be \nhonored to help build it with you.\nThis Information Furnished By: Jennie Hill\n\nWC-44\nTualatin Soil & Water Conservation DistrictTualatin Soil & Water Conservation District\nDirector, Zone 4\nOlivia Duren\nOccupation: Restoration \nProgram Manager, The \nFreshwater Trust\nOccupational Background: \nBiologist\nEducational Background: \nMaster of Science, Oregon \nState University; Bachelor of \nScience, Cornell University\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tualatin Soil and Water \nConservation District (SWCD) Board of Directors (current); \nOregon Invasive Species Council\nThe Tualatin watershed is the core of our local economy, \nenvironment, and community well-being. As a local resident of \nGaston, I seek to continue serving on the Tualatin SWCD Board \nof Directors to:\n• Promote smart planning and land use to preserve rural \nland, viability of agricultural and forestry, and community \naccess to thriving natural areas in Washington County.\n• Ensure farmers, foresters and community members have \naccess to technical resources that support healthy land \nand water and enable our lifeways to thrive for generations \nto come.\n• Uphold organizational health so SWCD staff can effectively \nhelp all of Washington County - rural and urban alike - \nkeep waterways, soils, habitats, forests and farms resilient.\n \nIn my professional work as a Restoration Program Manager I \npartner with Oregon landowners, municipalities and community \nstakeholders to build common-sense conservation solutions \nthat work for both people and watersheds. I build teams and \nprograms that improve watershed health while cost-effectively \nachieving state water quality goals. \nAs a local landowner I have collaborated with the Tualatin \nSWCD to restore oak habitat on our land - I have experienced \nfirsthand the professionalism, expertise and dedication that \nSWCD staff bring to their work. I seek to continue serving on \nthe Tualatin SWCD Board of Directors to invest my 20+ years \nof professional conservation experience to support SWCD staff \nin advancing their important mission. Together we will make \na positive and lasting impact on our local watersheds and the \ncommunities they support.\nThis Information Furnished By: Olivia Duren\nDirector At-Large, Position 2\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nDean Moberg\nOccupation: Natural resources \nconsultant\nOccupational Background: \nOwner, Gash Gold Vermilion \nConsulting; Conservationist, \nUSDA; High school agriculture \nteacher; Farm worker.\nEducational Background: \nPhD: Environmental Science \n& Engineering, OHSU; MA: \nTeaching, Cornell University; \nBS: Plant Science, UC Davis.\nPrior Governmental Experience: Tualatin Soil and Water \nConservation District (SWCD) Director (current); USDA \nConservationist (retired).\nI believe: Everyone can help conserve natural resources if \nprovided with accurate information and practical options in \na collaborative, respectful manner. I will listen to residents, \npractice open accountability and fiscal transparency, and \nmaintain equitable programs available to all. \nWhy I am running:  I’ve worked with conservation districts \nin five states for over 40 years. We need to adapt to climate \nchange and sequester carbon, protect the Tualatin River and its \ntributaries from pollution, establish richly diverse wildlife habitat, \nand ensure that land use laws with community input protect \nfamily farms and forests. I pledge to develop practical local \napproaches to conservation. \nAwards, publications, certifications:\n• 2024 Honorable Mention Climate Smart Award, Oregon \nDepartment Forestry.\n• Coauthor The Soils of Oregon (2022).\n• Coauthor Climate-informed Forestry Strategies for \nWashington County (2026).\n• USDA Technical Service Provider, soil health and transition \nto organic plans.\nENDORSEMENTS\nBarbie Minor, THPRD Board Director\nBrian Daellenbach, President Tualatin Riverkeepers\nDan M. Sullivan, Professor Emeritus Soil Science, OSU\nDavid Vanasche, Vanasche Farm LLC\nDeborah D. Garman, beekeeper, 350.org Wash. Co. Team Lead\nDeborah E. Politi, Cedar Mill Farmers Market Board Chair\nFarrah Chaichi, State Representative\nGreg Mecklem, Eagles Nest Reserve Vineyard\nKipperlyn Sinclair, Hillsboro City Council\nLakshmi Tata, Edible Stories Market Garden, farmer\nLeonora Ko, USDA Soil Conservationist (retired)\nLyle Spiesschaert, farmer\nLyn Jacobs MD, La Finquita del Buho\nMartin Streck, PSU Geology Professor\nMegan Klemens, public school teacher\nPeter Hayes, family forester\nSave Helvetia\nwww.deanmoberg.com\nMemberships:\n• Cedar Mill Farmers Market (board member)\n• Friends of Family Farmers\n• Washington County Small Woodlands Association\n• Soil and Water Conservation Society\n• Bird Alliance\n• Tualatin Riverkeepers\n• Tualatin Valley Beekeepers Association \nThis Information Furnished By: Dean P. Moberg\n\nWC-45\nDirector, Zone 5\nTualatin Soil & Water Conservation DistrictTualatin Soil & Water Conservation District\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nLaura Graham\nOccupation: Retired\nOccupational Background: \nBusiness Systems Analyst (25+ \nyears)\nEducational Background: \nB.S. Business Management \nand Administration - Concordia \nUniversity (Portland), Tigard \nHigh School\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nFor the past nine years, I have managed 40 acres of timber \nforest on family-owned land in Washington County. Earlier in my \nlife, I lived on an acre and a half of old-growth forest, where I \nworked to restore a year-round creek by planting native species \nand removing invasive plants. Through these experiences, \nconservation has become more than an interest to me. It is \nsomething I practice every day.\nI have experienced firsthand the value of the Tualatin Soil \nand Water Conservation District. As a participant in TSWCD’s \nForest Health Conservation Program, I have benefited from \nits expertise and financial assistance in improving the health \nand resilience of our forest—for humans and critters alike. I am \nrunning for the Board to give back to our community and to an \norganization that has helped me.\nI bring both hands-on forest management experience and \nmore than 25 years of business experience. My professional \nbackground as a business systems analyst taught me the \nimportance of thoughtful decision-making, fiscal responsibility, \naccountability, and planning for the future.\nI am passionate about conservation and believe our approach \nmust evolve as conditions change. A changing climate, \nincreasing wildfire risk, invasive species, water availability, and \nthe health of our forests and watersheds require us to think not \nonly about the land we have today, but about what we leave for \nfuture generations.\nAs a private landowner, I believe successful conservation \ndepends on cooperation. Programs need to be practical \nand accessible, giving landowners the knowledge, technical \nassistance, and resources to be good stewards of their land.\nI would be honored to bring my conservation experience, \nbusiness perspective, and commitment to responsible \nstewardship to the TSWCD Board.\nThis Information Furnished By: Laura Graham\nDirector, Zone 5\nEthan       \nVanderZanden\nOccupation: Farmer\nOccupational Background: \nFarm Equipment Operator; \nFarm Laborer\nEducational Background: \nOregon State University - \nBachelor of Science in Agricultural \nBusiness Management \n     Glencoe High School - Diploma\nPrior Governmental Experience: None\nI am running for the Tualatin Soil and Water Conservation \nDistrict Board because I believe healthy soil, clean water, and \nthriving communities depend on thoughtful, practical care of our \nnatural resources.\nMy experience working with the land has given me a firsthand \nlook at the importance of soil health and clean water. \nI would bring a practical, collaborative approach to the board. I \nvalue listening to people with different perspectives and believe \nthe best conservation solutions come from working together.\nI am committed to responsible use of public resources and to \nmaking sure district programs are accessible, effective, and \nresponsive to the community. \nI would be honored to serve on the board and contribute my \ntime, perspective, and willingness to work toward a healthier, \nmore resilient Tualatin Basin for generations to come.\nThis Information Furnished By: Ethan VanderZanden\n\nWC-46\nAM I REGISTERED\nTO VOTE? \nNow you can check \nyour registration online:\nwww.oregonvotes.gov\nFor more information \nabout voting in Oregon\nCALL\n1-866-ORE-VOTE/673-8683\nVISIT\nwww.oregonvotes.gov\nTTY\n1-800-735-2900\nfor the hearing impaired\nWest Multnomah Soil & Water West Multnomah Soil & Water \nConservation DistrictConservation District\nThe above information has not been verified for accuracy by Washington County.\nDirector, Zone 5\nTerri Preeg \nRiggsby\nOccupation: Executive Director \nwith Network of Oregon \nWatershed Councils; Former \nSenior Performance Auditor \nwith Oregon Secretary of State\nOccupational Background: \nEnvironmental Management & \nPolicy; Government Oversight\nEducational Background: Masters in Public Administration \nand Natural Resource Policy, Portland State University; Urban \nSustainability Graduate Certificate, Harvard University; \nPrior Governmental Experience: Current Board Chair, West \nMultnomah Soil and Water Conservation District; President, \nOregon Association of Conservation Districts; Chair, Multnomah \nCounty Audit Committee; Metro Policy Advisory Committee \nI am an experienced and dedicated leader who has \ndelivered results for decades on the West Multnomah Soil \n& Water Conservation District Board.  I pride myself in being \nthe voice of reason, implementing practical solutions to complex \nenvironmental issues while building our local green economy. \nThis brings new local jobs that support our community and \nboosts our economy.  I ask for your vote – every single voice \nmatters, and together we can build a better tomorrow! \nConservation Leader for 25 years - 1,000 Friends of Oregon \nInnovator Award Winner                \nI will continue to prioritize the protection and improvement of \nlocal watersheds and air quality, especially as we face hotter \nsummers and devastating wildfires, as well as extreme winters \nwith ice and storm events.  \nGovernment Oversight Expert \nI will use my auditing skills to make sure our public dollars are \nspent wisely and transparently, and that the District’s programs \nare accessible to everyone.  \nEnvironmental Protection for People, Fish & Wildlife\nI know that by working with the community, local farmers, and \npartner organizations we can restore our important urban \nstreams for wild salmon, lamprey, and other fish; we can \nsupport pollinator and bird habitat with native gardens; and we \ncan sustain and build vibrant, local food production on Sauvie \nIsland and throughout our District.\n“Terri has a proven record finding practical, sound \nenvironmental solutions. She works with everyone to \nget things done. She’s smart, sensible, trustworthy, and \nwill continue to honor your vote.” Former Portland City \nCommissioner Amanda Fritz\nThis Information Furnished By: Terri Preeg Riggsby\n\nWC-47\nMeasure No. 3-641\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nVote to approve the Wilsonville Town Center \nDevelopment District.\nQuestion: Should the City adopt proposed Ordinance No. \n905 establishing the Wilsonville Town Center Development \nDistrict, an urban renewal district?\nSummary: This measure would approve adoption of \nOrdinance No. 905 establishing the Wilsonville Town \nCenter Development District, an urban renewal district \nunder Oregon law, to address blight and help fund \nimprovements within the district boundary. To read the \nOrdinance, go to https://www.wilsonvilleoregon.gov/\nord-905.\nThe measure authorizes the use of tax increment financing \n(TIF), a funding tool available under Oregon’s Urban \nRenewal laws. TIF is not a new tax or tax rate increase. \nInstead, future growth in assessed property values within \nthe district generates revenue used to help fund eligible \nprojects, while taxing districts continue receiving property \ntax revenues based on current assessed values. \nThe Town Center Development District proposes to help \nfund: \nGathering Spaces, Parking:\n• Streetscapes, plazas for outdoor dining and events\n• Parking facilities, traffic mitigation improvements \nSmall Business, Economic Development:\n• Incentives for new restaurants, food carts, and small \nlocal businesses\nInfrastructure:\n• Landscaped streets, sidewalks, and other \nimprovements supporting new development in Town \nCenter \nPlan Details:\n• Maximum indebtedness (spending cap) of $152 \nmillion ($85.5 M in today’s dollars)\n• Structured as smaller borrowings over time\n• 28-year duration\nExplanatory Statement\nThis measure asks whether the City of Wilsonville should adopt \nOrdinance No. 905 creating the Town Center Development \nDistrict (the “District”), an urban renewal district established \nunder Oregon law. If approved, the Ordinance would adopt \nthe Town Center Development District Plan (the “Plan”) and \nauthorize the use of tax increment financing to address blight \nand help fund eligible projects identified in the Plan.\nFull Text & Documents\nAdditional information, including Ordinance No. 905, the Plan, \nand the accompanying Report, is available at https://www.\nwilsonvilleoregon.gov/ord-905.\nWhat would the District do?\nThe Town Center Plan, adopted in 2019 following a multi-year \npublic engagement process, establishes a long-term vision for \nthe area. The proposed Plan would provide one financing\ntool —tax increment financing (urban renewal)— to help \nfund programs and public improvements supporting that vision.\nBallot Title The proposed District includes property within and adjacent to Town Center Loop and the Village at Main commercial area \nsouth of Wilsonville Road.\nPrivate development projects (new buildings) would remain \nsubject to applicable land use review, building permits, fees, \nand other City approvals.\nWhat projects could be funded?\nThe Plan identifies eligible public projects and programs, \nincluding:\n• Parks and public gathering spaces\n• Parking facilities\n• Economic development programs, incentives for a food \ncart pod and small businesses\n• Transportation, sidewalk, bicycle, and pedestrian \nimprovements\n• Utility infrastructure\nTogether, these projects are intended to address blight and \nfacilitate redevelopment.\nHow does tax increment financing work?\nTax increment financing uses growth in assessed property \nvalues within the District, occurring after its creation, to repay \ndebt issued for eligible projects. The portion of tax revenues\nattributable to growth within the District is called ‘tax increment.’\nThe measure would not establish a new property tax or \nincrease existing property tax rates.\nThe Plan authorizes up to $152 million in principal \nindebtedness, borrowed in portions over time, with tax \nincrement collection anticipated for 28 years, through fiscal \nyear 2056.\nWhat is the development forecast?\nThe technical Report accompanying the Plan assumes gradual \nredevelopment, including 2,381 housing units and 1.1 million \nsquare feet of new commercial development.\nThe Report also assumes the District’s taxable assessed value \ncould increase from approximately $206 million in 2027 to \napproximately $1.7 billion if forecasted redevelopment occurs.\nThese assumptions are used for financial planning and \nanalysis. They do not approve, require, or guarantee that \nredevelopment or future assessed values will occur as forecast.\nAfter the District closes, tax increment collection stops and the \nfull assessed value of the area would be available to all taxing \ndistricts. This new, larger tax base becomes a part of the fiscal\nfoundation for future public services.\nWhat happens next?\nIf this measure passes, the City will undertake the required \nstatutory steps to consider adoption of Ordinance No. 905 - \nnamely, conferring with overlapping taxing districts, a public \nhearing before the Planning Commission, public notice, and a \npublic hearing before the City Council.\nIf the measure fails, the District will not be created at this time.\nSubmitted By: Kimberly Veliz\n\nWC-48\nMeasure No. 3-641\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nNOW’s the time to move forward with Town Center:  \ntransform it into a vibrant, mixed-use district with \nretail, dining, housing, and gathering spaces. More at: \nWilsonvilleTownCenter.com\nIf voters don’t approve measure 3-641, project costs will \nescalate causing uncertainty about what improvements can be \nmade, when, and if ever\nTOWN CENTER IMPROVEMENTS CREATE A VIBRANT \nCOMMUNITY\n• New infrastructure for future food cart pods and more \nactivity for our existing businesses\n• Car, bike, and pedestrian friendly MAIN STREET\n• Improved traffic flow on Wilsonville Road\n• New convenient parking to replace sprawling, underused \nlots\n• Safer connected streets and modernized underground \nutilities\nFISCAL RESPONSIBILITY, ZERO NEW PROPERTY TAXES \n• Urban Renewal ranked the #1 way to fund the Town \nCenter public infrastructure by the 2023 Urban Renewal \nTask Force\n• Villebois neighborhood used Urban Renewal to pay \nfor building trails and parks, streets, residential fire \nsprinklers, and undergrounding of electrical, water and \nsewer services. Urban Renewal revenue helped fund the \nBoeckman Dip Bridge serving the Frog Pond Primary \nSchool and Frog Pond neighborhood \n• Responsible use of urban renewal in Wilsonville since \n1990 means consulting and conferring with schools, \nthe fire district and other overlapping taxing district \npartners about the use of Urban Renewal to enhance \nlivability, support development, and student population \ngrowth. In collaboration with these partners, Wilsonville \nused Urban Renewal to help fund land for a new \nschool, gym and sports fields, major streets, parks, \nand undergrounding of electrical, water and sewer \nservices\n• Urban Renewal uses taxes collected from future \nproperty value growth to repay the cost of the new \npublic infrastructure, rather than raising current local tax \nrates, creating a new revenue stream for overlapping \ntaxing districts like schools and the fire district, in \nperpetuity\n• Urban Renewal financing for Town Center Development \nDistrict reflects realistic economic scenario and public \ninput\n• Let’s move forward with Town Center making it the \nvibrant heart of Wilsonville. Vote YES on 3-641\nCaroline Berry, Wilsonville City Council President\nThis Information Furnished By: Caroline Berry\nArgument in Favor\nMake Town Center the Heart of Wilsonville\nFor years, cool grocery stores, distinctive shops, \nappealing restaurants, and vibrant gathering places have \nbypassed Wilsonville.\nWhy? Because our sprawling underused parking lots and \naged, outdated strip malls are unappealing to developers \nseeking an active downtown core.\nWilsonville deserves better. Existing businesses in Town \nCenter can benefit from the increased foot traffic that an \nactive Town Center can bring.\nTo address the blight, a multi-year, city-wide planning process \nproduced the mixed-use Town Center Plan. The Plan envisions \na dynamic, thriving community hub with walkable and engaging \npublic spaces and parks, spaces that connect people to one \nanother and the environment, and year-around activities. \nAdopted in 2019, the Plan achieves the community’s vision \nby addressing parking, economic feasibility, building heights, \ninfrastructure, and residential, commercial and retail potential. \nSee WilsonvilleTownCenter.com for details.\nHow does the Town Center Plan become reality? Private-\nsector investors need to choose Wilsonville. Development \nwill happen over time, property by property. Success requires \npublic infrastructure, retail, dining, and entertainment, plus a \nvariety of housing for retirees, students, workers, and families. \nAll of these are essential to a thriving Town Center.\nHow can the public infrastructure be funded? A 2023 \nCitizen Task Force ranked Urban Renewal as the best way to \nfund the Town Center public infrastructure. Urban Renewal is \nnot a new tax or a tax increase. Urban renewal helped fund \nmost of Wilsonville’s major streets, acres of parks, employment \nareas, and our water and sewer system. Urban renewal made \nVillebois possible. It has helped our schools acquire land for a \nnew school, build a gymnasium, and add sports fields.\nNow’s the time to get started. Let’s create a Town Center that \nbecomes the heart of Wilsonville. Vote YES on 3-641.\nVote YES for Wilsonville. \nDick Spence\nJulie Fitzgerald \nSusie Stevens \nHilly Alexander \nJason Jones\nThis Information Furnished By: Jason Jones\n\nWC-49\nMeasure No. 3-641\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nLET’S PUT TOWN CENTER TO WORK FOR WILSONVILLE\nMeasure 3-641 gives Wilsonville a practical way to invest in the \nheart of our community.\nMost of us can picture things we would like to see in Town \nCenter: places to gather, improved public spaces, new \nrestaurants and shops, better streets and sidewalks, easy \nparking, and opportunities for local businesses to grow. \nBut those things do not happen without the infrastructure to \nsupport them.\nUrban renewal can help pay for streets, utilities, public spaces, \nparking, and other improvements that make redevelopment \nand private investment possible.\nHere’s the important part: Measure 3-641 does not create a \nnew tax on Wilsonville residents. \nInstead, as property values within the Town Center district \ngrow over time, a portion of the additional property tax revenue \ngenerated by that growth is reinvested back into the district. \nThose funds can be used to repay the cost of eligible Town \nCenter projects.\nIn other words, future growth pays for the improvements \nthat support future growth. \nThat is a practical way to revitalize an aging commercial area, \nsupport existing and new businesses, strengthen Wilsonville’s \nlong-term tax base, and create a Town Center where people \nwant to spend time.\nWe have talked about improving Town Center for years. \nMeasure 3-641 gives us a tool to start making those plans real.\nVote YES on Measure 3-641.\nThis Information Furnished By: Christie Mayer\nArgument in Favor\nYes on 3-641: Town Center Renewal for Housing \nAffordability \n \nUrban Renewal in Town Center provides public infrastructure \nimprovements that will support new investment in our city, \nincluding potential housing. This addresses housing cost issues \nthat Wilsonville residents face today. \n \nWilsonville housing supply is strained, driving a housing \naffordability crunch. Housing shortages disproportionately \naffect renting households, many led by low to middle income \nparents. Existing mortgages aren’t affected, but rents often go \nup annually by 10% without any negotiation. \n \nHousing in Town Center is a chance to ease that pressure \nwhile building value with walkable housing options, eliminating \nblight, and bringing more patrons to our businesses. \n \nFor decades Wilsonville has had roughly equal numbers \nof renting and homeowning households. That is part of \nits character, not a defect. Renting in Wilsonville offers \nopportunities. Young folks can save for a house. Retirees can \nminimize surprise maintenance expenses. Wilsonville beats \nmany communities for good schools and jobs that pay well. \nBusinesses benefit from a large local workforce. \n \nWe’re talking about neighbors who work, shop, walk, bike, \nplay, study, and contribute to our community. But because \nWilsonville is so desirable, many of these neighbors have faced \n40+% increases in housing costs over 4 to 5 years, squeezing \nsome of Wilsonville’s households much harder than others. \n \nWilsonville’s job market keeps growing, both in Town Center \nand in Coffee and Basalt Creek Industrial areas. As that \nhappens, the demand for existing homes will pinch harder. \n \nHousing in Town Center is good for our community, and \nit’s an economic necessity. The alternative is even more of \nWilsonville’s labor force living elsewhere, putting more strain on \nour commuter arteries. \n \nSay yes to Town Center renewal — not despite housing, but \nbecause of it. \nThis Information Furnished By:: Andrew Engel\n\nWC-50\nMeasure No. 3-641\nCity of WilsonvilleCity of Wilsonville\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Opposition\nDEVELOPMENT DISTRICT OR HIGH-DENSITY APARTMENT \nDISTRICT?\nAs a Wilsonville City Councilor, I urge you to vote No on 3-641.\nWhen City staff prepared this measure, they hired a polling \ncompany to test names for what had always been called the \nTown Center Urban Renewal Plan. I objected when “urban \nrenewal” was replaced with the more palatable “development \ndistrict.”\nNeither label tells voters what’s actually being proposed.\nHere’s one that does: High-Density Apartment District.\nFollow the City’s own numbers.\nThe City’s Explanatory Statement projects 2,381 new housing \nunits against just 1.1 million square feet of new commercial \nspace.\nBy the City’s own projections, only 3.6% of that housing will \nbe condos or townhomes, with 96.4% apartments. That’s \nabout 2,300 new apartments—more than one for every five \nhomes that exist in Wilsonville today.\nThe City already has a powerful incentive to build that housing. \nSince 2022, Town Center has sat inside a Vertical Housing \nDevelopment Zone, offering developers a 10-year property-\ntax abatement that increases with every residential floor they \nbuild—up to 80%.\nZoning may require commercial storefronts on the ground \nfloor of these buildings, but it cannot create the customers or \nbusinesses to fill them.\nWilsonville already has visibly vacant commercial space. We \ndon’t lack leasable commercial space. We lack businesses that \nhave found Wilsonville an attractive place to invest.\nMeasure 3-641 asks voters to authorize up to $152 million in \nurban renewal debt, repaid through 2056.\nCalling it a “Development District” is a marketing ploy.\nThe City’s own numbers point to something more specific: a \nHigh-Density Apartment District.\nThat is what voters are actually being asked to finance.\nDon’t be fooled.\nVote No on 3-641 and let’s create a plan that is right for \nWilsonville.\nAdam Cunningham \nMember, Wilsonville City Council\nThis Information Furnished By: Eric Winters\nArgument in Opposition\nWILSONVILLE AT A CROSSROADS—CHOOSE THE RIGHT \nPATH\nWilsonville is at a crossroads.\nMeasure 3-641 asks voters to choose between two very \ndifferent futures for Town Center.\nOne path uses more than $150 million in public financing, tax \nincentives and high-density zoning to steer Town Center toward \nthousands of new apartments.\nThe other begins by asking a simpler question:\nWhat do the people who already live here want Town \nCenter to be?\nFor decades, Town Center primarily served a commercial \nfunction. Some properties clearly need updating, but that \ndoes not require redesigning the center of our city around the \nbusiness interests of corporate landlords and thousands of their \nfuture tenants.\nGive commercial property owners enough flexibility to \nredevelop their own properties in response to what Wilsonville \nresidents actually want. Last year, owners of the boarded-up \ntheater complex pursued a redevelopment proposal with an \nanchor store, a taproom and an 18-space food-cart pod with \na dining pavilion. City staff raised obstacles tied to the Town \nCenter plan, and the owners ultimately walked away.\nLet private investment work.\nDo not let housing become the purpose of Town Center while \nrestaurants, shops, entertainment and services become the \nafterthought.\nThat is the tail wagging the dog.\nAnd there is more at stake in this election than Measure 3-641.\nIf voters reject the City’s high-density apartment district, the \nnext City Council will immediately face an important choice: \nrespect that direction and pursue a different approach, or \npursue the same vision through other means.\nOur next Council needs to hit the ground running. City Council \ncandidates Jennie Hill and Katie Dunwell understand what \nis at stake, they are the only candidates who fought for your \nright to vote on this issue. \nTown Center does not need another long wait for one \npredetermined vision. It needs flexibility, private investment and \nleadership willing to listen when Wilsonville voters choose a \ndifferent path.\nVote NO on 3-641\nEric Winters\nThis Information Furnished By: Eric Winters\n\nWC-51\nMeasure No. 26-267\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nNo Explanatory Statement was filed. \nAmends Charter: Portland residents decide 2% of \ncity’s annual discretionary budget.\nQuestion: Should Portland create participatory budgeting \nprogram for residents to make binding decisions to spend \n2% of city’s discretionary budget?\nSummary: Currently, Mayor works with City Administrator, \nbureaus, and community to develop proposed budget; \nCouncil then considers, amends as needed and adopts \nfollowing public hearings. If measure is not approved, this \nprocess would not change. \nIf measure approved, amended Charter would require \nCouncil to allocate an amount equivalent to at least 2% \nof the city’s adopted General Fund discretionary ongoing \nexpenses (Council may identify funding from entire city \nbudget to meet funding obligation) towards a participatory \nbudgeting program that is:\n• Developed with input from oversight committee, \nselected by a process determined by Council, of \nresidents from all Council districts\n• Open to any resident’s funding proposals for citywide \nor district projects:\n• Proposals assessed and refined into limited \nnumber of feasible projects by city staff \nand budget delegates from all districts in a \ntransparent process\n• Feasible projects advance to binding selection \nprocess open, accessible to all residents\n• Operational July 2027\n• Under recent forecast, preliminary estimate:\n• 16.4 million in FY 2027-28\n• 17.0 million in FY 2028-29\n• Compliant with Oregon Local Budget Law\nOther provisions.\nSubmitted By: Louise Hansen\nBallot Title\nWASHINGTON COUNTY \nELECTION RESULTS\nare available starting\nat 8 p.m.\non Election Day\nwww.washcovotes.org\n \nRESULTADOS DE LAS \nELECCIONES DEL \nCONDADO DE \nWASHINGTON\nestán disponibles \na partir de las 8 p.m. \nel día de las elecciones\nwww.washcovotes.org\nReferred to the People by the City Council\n\nWC-52\nMeasure No. 26-267\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nVote YES for Your 2 Cents PDX! Measure 26-267.\nParticipatory budgeting lets people like you help decide how \nyour City spends a small sum of money. It is used successfully \nin more than 7,000 cities around the world, and in over 100 US \ncities. \nThis measure does not raise taxes. A Yes vote for Measure \n26-267 lets Portlanders help choose what our neighborhoods \nneed. \nPortlanders can share their priorities and projects, and then \nvote on which ones you want to get a share of ~$16 million. \nThen the winning items get funded.\nPortlanders have lost trust in City Hall. Too much money has \nbeen misplaced or poorly managed while our schools struggle, \nparks go dark, shelters close, and city workers lose their jobs. \nThat’s why we need Your 2 Cents now. Studies show \nthat participatory budgeting is one of the best ways for \ncities to bring people together, make government more \naccountable, and get good things done for the community.\nOregon Metro is already doing this in Clackamas and \nWashington counties. For example, the Community Choice \nGrants program funded parks projects selected by community \nmembers, like a Willamette riverwalk and playground. Now it’s \ntime to bring it to Portland.\nNo city anywhere has laid off workers because of \nparticipatory budgeting. In fact, studies show that cities \nwith PB programs often deliver more results for residents, \nwith more cost efficiency. \nPowerful interests want to keep you out of the conversation. \nYour 2 Cents gives you a voice.\nMeasure 26-267 is supported by community organizations \nyou trust, including:\n- Multnomah County Democrats  \n- Democratic Socialists of America \n- Sierra Club\n- Next Up Action Fund\n- East County Rising Action Fund\n- Working Families Party\n- American Federation of Teachers - Oregon\n- PSU-AAUP\n- Coalition of Communities of Color\n- Urban League of Portland\n- Participatory Budgeting Oregon\n- Imagine Black\n- Mother PAC\n- 350PDX\nLearn more and see the full supporter list at Your2CentsPDX.com.\nThis Information Furnished By: Edward Kurtz\nArgument in Favor\nFrequently Asked Questions \n \n1. How does participatory budgeting work? \n \nStep 1: Residents like you propose budget priorities and/or \nprojects, and city staff make sure they are doable.\nStep 2: Portlanders vote on the priorities/projects. (In NYC, this \nhappens online and at public libraries, see participate.nyc.gov.)\nStep 3: The winning items get funded, and the cycle repeats.\n \n2. What is Your Two Cents (Measure 26-267)?\n \nA record-breaking 78,743 Portlanders signed a petition \nto create a participatory budgeting program, giving all \nPortlanders a direct say in how to spend $16 million each \nyear – equivalent to 2% of the city’s $800 million discretionary \nbudget. The measure allows these funds to come from across \nthe city’s entire $8.5 billion budget. \n \n3. Will it raise my taxes?\n \nNo. Measure 26-267 does not raise taxes. It simply gives \nPortlanders a greater say in how existing city funds are spent.\n \n4.   Will it cut city jobs?\n \nNo. Measure 26-267 would protect jobs. No participatory \nbudgeting process in the world has ever cut jobs because \nit does not change the size of the budget, it just gives \nresidents more say.\n \n5. How much will it cost? \n \nApproximately $1-1.5 million annually. (The city’s current \nbudget for community outreach is $5.1M).\n \n6. Where is it successful? \n \nMore than 7,000 cities worldwide have used participatory \nbudgeting. New York City and Boston have operated programs \nfor more than a decade as part of their city charters. In the \nU.S., more than 100 cities have successfully used participatory \nbudgeting programs. \n \nIn Oregon, Metro Government has already completed \ntwo rounds of participatory budgeting in Washington and \nClackamas Counties. It was so successful they plan to expand.\nThis Information Furnished By: Edward Kurtz\n\nWC-53\nMeasure No. 26-267\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nPortland Small Businesses For Your 2 Cents PDX \nAs small business owners we understand the importance \nof sound budgeting for operational excellence and making \neffective use of scarce resources. Public budgeting in the \nCity of Portland currently lacks adequate transparency and \naccountability. Recent City spending decisions, grants and \neven business loans have proceeded with little oversight and \naccountability which has further undermined the public trust. \nWe need new solutions to increase transparency and \naccountability, provide opportunities for more meaningful public \nparticipation, and rebuild public trust. We need to tap the \nideas and knowledge of all Portlanders in developing, vetting, \nand funding feasible solutions to our City’s most pressing \nchallenges and opportunities. We need residents and City \nstaff working together, on more equal terms, to shape strategic \npublic investments in our neighborhoods and communities. We \nneed measure 26-267 to finally bring participatory budgeting to \nPortland. \nParticipatory budgeting is a demonstrated mechanism for \nincreasing transparency, accountability and participation. In \nNew York City public projects selected by residents through \nparticipatory budgeting are implemented quicker and with fewer \ncomplaints. Existing programs implemented in cities across the \nUS and around the world for decades provide proven models \nand best practices Portland can build on and refine to adapt PB \nfor our City.\nFor all these reasons, we, the undersigned small business \nowners in Portland, urge voters vote yes on Measure 26-267 \nfor Your 2 Cents PDX. \nBarb Grover, Splendid Cycles, SE Portland\nEli Spevak, Orange Splot LLC, NE Portland\nLily Burnett, Parkrose Coffee, East Portland\nMark Lakeman, Communitecture, SE Portland\nJeffrey Mandel, Exit Shoes, Downtown\nPeter Platt, Andina Restaurant, NW Portland\nLaura Streib, St. Johns Mercantile LLC, North Portland\nThis Information Furnished By: Edward Kurtz\nArgument in Favor\nEast Portland Residents for Your 2 Cents PDX\n \nMeasure 26-267 (Your 2 Cents) would give East Portlanders \na direct say in delivering improvements by and for East \nPortlanders!\n \nEast Portland has long struggled to get its fair share in the \nbudget process. East Portland has unique needs and presents \nchallenges and opportunities long ignored by City Hall.\n \nWith Your 2 Cents, East Portlanders – not lobbyists or City \ninsiders – will determine outcomes for East Portland.  \n \nYour 2 Cents is an opportunity to advance long-sought City \nprojects in District 1 serving East Portlanders including:\n \n• Fire Station 29 Improvements (Powellhurst) \n• Leach Botanical Garden\n• Sumner Neighborhood Park \n• 150th & Division Community Garden\n• Rental Assistance & Anti-Displacement Programs\n• East Portland Grocery Stores\n• Neighborhood Greenways & Lighting\n• Mobile/Decentralized Community Services\n• International District & Music Venues \n• Soccer Fields\n• Sidewalk improvements\n• Youth Programs \n• Gateway Green Access Improvements & Recreation \nPrograms\n• Rocky Butte Loop\n• Cultural & Business Districts Branding\n• Placemaking for crime prevention\n• East Portland Urban Shade (parks & street trees)\n \nAlthea Ender, David Douglas School Board Member\nErica Fuller, Centennial \nGayle Palmer, Centennial \nHelena Guerrero Sullivan, East County Rising\nJR Lilly, Native American Community Leader\nLily Burnett, Parkrose \nLinda Robinson, Hazelwood Park advocate\nLeanna Petrone, East County Rising\nNick Sauvie, Housing Advocate\nStephanie Routh, Lents \nTimur Ender, Mt. Hood Community College Board Member\nTom Lewis, Rockwood Water PUD Director\nTy Wilkins, Powellhurst-Gilbert \nDavid Linn, Centennial School Board Director\nJose Gamero-Gerogeson, David Douglas School Board \nDirector\nShattoyia Bentley, Mt. Hood Community College Board \nMember\nBrian Ettling, Wilkes\nThis Information Furnished By: Edward Kurtz\n\nWC-54\nMeasure No. 26-267\nCity of PortlandCity of Portland\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nWe Shared Real Power with Our Constituents through  \nParticipatory Budgeting and Would Do it Again!\nWe are your Oregon Metro Councilors. In 2019 Portland voters \napproved a bond measure to protect clean water, healthy \nhabitat, and access to nature. The Metro Council included \nmeasure provisions allowing for participatory budgeting so \nresidents could directly decide how to spend a portion of bond \nfunds.\nThis led to Metro’s Nature in Neighborhoods Community \nChoice Grants Program that used participatory budgeting, the \nsame process proposed by Your Two Cents campaign for the \ncity of Portland through Measure 26-267 \nIn 2023-24 Metro launched the first cycle in District 4 \n(Hillsboro-area) to allocate $2 million. With staff support, \nresidents developed and selected 22 projects including \n$75,562 in natural feature enhancements at Hillsboro’s \nHamby Park, $198,250 for a new “pocket forest” and walking \npath at Cornelius’s Tarrybrooke Park, and $201,500 to \nrestore wetlands at Forest Grove’s Kyle Park. In 2025-26 we \ncompleted our second cycle.\nParticipatory budgeting democratizes decision-making allowing \nall residents the opportunity to directly determine real-world \noutcomes in their neighborhoods and communities.\nMeasure 26-267, Your 2 Cents PDX, would bring this process \nto the City of Portland and help rebuild trust and accountability \nover the city budget. Take it from us, elected officials who have \nimplemented participatory budgeting programs in our region. \nParticipatory budgeting works! We urge a Yes vote on Measure \n26-267.\nJuan Carlos Gonzalez, Metro Council President\nChristine Lewis, Metro Councilor District 2\nDuncan Hwang, Metro Councilor District 6\nAshton Simpson, Metro Councilor District 1\nMary Nolan, Metro Councilor District 5\nThis Information Furnished By: Edward Kurtz\nArgument in Opposition\nFewer firefighters.\nFewer engines.\nSlower response.\nThat’s the price of Measure 26-267.\nWhen you dial 911, Portland’s firefighters answer. Some calls \nare fast-moving fires. Most are medical emergencies: a heart \nattack, a stroke, an overdose, a crash. The men and women of\nPortland Fire & Rescue are firefighters, paramedics, and EMTs, \ntrained and ready for every call.\nAnd whether it’s a house on fire or someone who has stopped \nbreathing, seconds make all the difference. Every engine, every \nstation, every firefighter on duty means a faster response, and \na faster response saves lives.\nThat’s why Portland’s Fire Fighters are asking you to vote \nNO on Measure 26-267.\nEarlier this year, North Portland came dangerously close to \nlosing a fire engine at Station 22. It wasn’t because the need \nfor the engine went away, but the city simply did not have \nenough money to maintain existing services. Firefighters and \ncommunity members fought to keep that engine in service, and \nthe City Council made tough choices and cut elsewhere to save \nit. That’s how close to the edge Portland’s public safety budget \nalready is.\nMeasure 26-267 would permanently remove at least $16 \nmillion every year from the same General Fund that pays \nfor fire and emergency response. This mandatory set-aside \nwould be locked into the city charter, with no dedicated funding \nsource — an experiment no other American city has tried.\nFor perspective: $16 million is roughly what it costs to operate \nsix fire engine companies for a year. That’s roughly one in \nfive of Portland’s fire engines.\nWith resources for our public safety bureaus already stretched \ntoo thin, we can’t afford more cuts.\nMeasure 26-267 risks cuts to basic city services, including \nessential fire and emergency services.\nPortland Fire Fighters’ Association, IAFF Local 43, urges \nyou to vote NO.\nLearn more: https://www.protectourcityservices.org/\nThis Information Furnished By: Amy Ruiz\n\nWC-55\nMeasure No. 34-352\nCity of BanksCity of Banks\nNo Arguments in Favor or Opposition of this measure were filed.\nLevy to fund dedicated Washington County Law \nEnforcement Services\nQuestion: Shall City levy $2.50 per assessed $1,000 for \nfive years beginning FY 2027-2028 for law enforcement \nservices? This measure renews current local option taxes. \nSummary: The Banks city council recommended this \nmeasure for the November 3, 2026, ballot to renew the \ncurrent local option tax of $2.50 per $1,000 of assessed \nvalue. If approved, the city will levy $2.50 per $1,000 of \nassessed value for each of five years beginning 2027 \nto continue the contract with the Washington County \nSheriff for full-time local police services. If this levy fails, \nthe city will be without dedicated police services, which \ninclude prompt 911 response, crime prevention, law and \ntraffic enforcement, youth and school programs, crime \ninvestigation, enforcement of city ordinances, court bailiff \nservices and other city-specific policing. The city has \ncontracted with Washington County since 2006, and this \nlevy continues funding for those services at full-time (24/7) \nlevel (two deputies, two shifts per week). For homes \nassessed at $100,000, the annual tax is estimated at $250. \nThis is an ESTIMATE ONLY based on best information \navailable from the county assessor at the time of estimate \nand may not reflect early payment discounts, compression, \nor collection costs.\nExplanatory Statement\nWhy is this levy proposed?\nThe City needs funding to continue the contract for law \nenforcement services provided by the Washington County \nSheriff’s department as the current contract funding is due to \nexpire. The current contract expires on June 30, 2027; after \nwhich, the City will have no dedicated police services without \na new contract and a levy to fund it. These levy funds will be \nused to provide full-time (24/7) coverage for the City of Banks \nby the Washington County Sheriff’s Department for local police \nservices and the normal required administrative expenses such \nas utility, audit, liability insurance, 911 dispatch and materials \nexpenses. If this measure is approved, the city will continue to \nlevy $2.50 per $1,000 of assessed value for each of five years \nbeginning in tax year 2027. This will be a renewal of the current \nlaw enforcement levy of $2.50 per $1,000 of assessed value \nthat has been in place since 2022 and will continue a contract \nwith the Washington County Sheriff’s Department for 2 full-time \ndedicated deputies who work a combined total of 80 hours \nper week in Banks. The services provided by the Washington \nCounty Sheriff’s Department include police patrols specifically \nin the City of Banks as well as crime scene investigation, any \npublic awareness programs offered by Washington County, \naccess to national crime databases, traffic enforcement \nsweeps, municipal court bailiff services, crime prevention, etc.  \nShould this levy fail, Banks will only have law enforcement \nresponses for emergency calls, i.e., high priority 911 calls, \netc. with an officer that is shared with the entire west side of \nWashington County.  There would be no services available for \nother types of police work in the City. This levy is necessary to \nmaintain full-time police coverage in the City of Banks and to \nserve the City’s residents, visitors, businesses and students of \nthe Banks School System.\nBallot Title Who is served by the City’s law enforcement service?\nIf you received this ballot, you would be served by the law \nenforcement services supported by this levy.  \nWhat impact would the levy have on a homeowner’s \nproperty tax?\nThe replacement levy is estimated to average $2.50 per $1,000 \nassessed value.  If approved, owners of a home would see the \nfollowing estimated annual impact:\n$100,000 assessed home $2.50 x $100,000 = $250\n$150,000 assessed home $2.50 x $150,000 = $375\nWhen would this levy first appear on property tax bills?\nThe property tax bill due in November 2027 would include this \nlevy.  \n    \nSubmitted By: Marsha Kirk, Mayor, City of Banks\n\nWC-56\nMeasure No. 34-353\nCity of Forest GroveCity of Forest Grove\nNo Arguments in Favor or Opposition of this measure were filed.\nGeneral Obligation Bonds to replace the current police \nfacility\nQuestion: Shall Forest Grove issue general obligation \nbonds not to exceed $29.5 million in principal for a new \npolice facility? If the bonds are approved, they will be \npayable from taxes on property or property ownership that \nare not subject to the limits of sections 11 and 11b, Article \nXI of the Oregon Constitution. \nSummary: Approval of this measure would authorize the \nCity of Forest Grove to issue up to $29.5 million in general \nobligation bonds to provide a new police facility on City \nproperty.\nThe current facility was constructed in 1977 and has \nremained the same size while the department has grown \nfrom 12 to 42 personnel. Renovating or expanding the \ncurrent facility is more expensive than building a new \nfacility. The bonds would finance capital costs, including:\n• Adding space for evidence\n• Creating a safe place to interview victims\n• Ensuring separation of adults and juvenile offenders\n• Improving accessibility\n• Meeting seismic and building codes\n• Providing secure parking for police vehicles and \nequipment\nThe levy rate is estimated at $0.69 per $1,000 assessed \nvalue. The bonds are estimated to cost approximately \n$193/year for the average assessed home. Actual levy \nrates may differ due to the interest rates at the time of \nissuance and changes in assessed value. Bonds may be \nissued in multiple series, maturing 21 years or less from \nissuance.\nExplanatory Statement\nFORM OF EXPLANATORY STATEMENT  \nCITY OF FOREST GROVE \nWASHINGTON COUNTY, OREGON\nProceeds from these bonds would provide a new police \nfacility for the Forest Grove Police Department. The current \npolice facility was constructed in 1977. Since then, the Police \nDepartment has grown from 12 to 42 personnel while the \n12,120-square-foot facility has remained the same size. \nPolice officers use the current facility to investigate cases, \nsecure and process evidence, temporarily detain and process \noffenders, meet with victims, interview suspects, train on \ncurrent practices and procedures, write reports, and secure \npatrol vehicles and equipment.\nA 2016 Police Facility Needs Assessment identified 34 \nsignificant deficiencies in the current facility, including:\n• Inadequate space for processing and storing evidence, \nwhich can jeopardize cases.\n• Lacks a safe and private place to interview victims, which \ncan result in sensitive information being overheard.\n• Lacks adequate separation between adult and juvenile \noffenders as required by state law.\n• Limited accessibility for persons with disabilities.\n• Noncompliant with ADA requirements, building and \nseismic codes.\n• Insufficient secure parking for police vehicles and \nequipment.\nBallot Title In 2018, an Alternatives Analysis evaluated whether to remodel, expand, or replace the current facility. Its findings included: \n• Remodeling or expanding the current facility is impractical \nand more expensive than constructing a new facility. \n• The current facility cannot support a second story.\n• The current facility does not meet current building codes \nand safety standards.\n• The current lot size is insufficient to expand the current \nfacility and secure parking.\nBased on these findings, Mackenzie Architecture designed a \nnew facility, which was value-engineered from approximately \n25,000 to 21,500 square feet to reduce costs and is about 45% \nlarger than the current facility. The design provides:\n• Expanded storage for evidence and records in a climate-\ncontrolled environment to support evidence integrity.\n• Dedicated, private, and safe interview spaces for victims \nand families.\n• Separate holding areas for adult and juvenile offenders to \ncomply with state law and address safety concerns.\n• Compliance with ADA requirements, building and seismic \ncodes.\n• Secure parking to protect vehicles and equipment.\n• Adequate space for officers to work on cases and \neffectively train.\nThe new design incorporates elements of Forest Grove’s \nhistory and proximity to nature, using materials such as brick \nand wood. It is expected to meet the community’s needs for 40 \nyears or more.\nThe new facility will be located on the corner of 19th and Birch \non City-owned property, adjacent to other City facilities. The \nsite is centrally located to facilitate convenient public access \nand maintain response times. \nFunding the new facility is estimated to cost $29.5 million. \nFor the average assessed household, this is approximately \n$193 per year or $17 per month. Actual levy rates may differ \ndue to the interest rates at the time of issuance and changes \nin assessed value. Bonds may be issued in multiple series, \nmaturing 21 years or less from issuance.\nA new facility is the next step in the City’s long-term building \nplan and an investment to ensure the safety, accessibility, and \noperational efficiency of the Forest Grove Police Department.\nSubmitted By: The Forest Grove City Council.\n\nWC-57\n \nMeasure No. 34-354\nCity of King CityCity of King City\nNo Arguments in Favor of this measure were filed.\nCity of King City Charter Modernization Amendment\nQuestion: Shall the City of King City amend and restate \nthe existing Charter to modernize, update, and clarify \nCharter provisions?\nSummary: The King City Charter establishes the \ngovernmental structure for the City. This measure amends \nthe City of King City Charter of 2006.\nAmendments to the Charter would be as follows:\n• Definitions added for clarity.\n• Modernize text to closely mirror the current League of \nOregon Cities Model Charter.\n• Preamble was clarified and shortened.\n• The word “shall” was replaced with “will” where \napplicable.\n• Removed sections that are not legally enforceable or \nare covered by state statute.\n• Renumbered charter from 1.00 to 1.1.\n• Added 2.4 Charter Review.\n• Clarified vacancies in public offices.\n• Clarified all duties for Councilors, Mayor, and \nPresident.\n• Clarified Councilor prohibitions.\n• Removed the prohibition on compensation for \nCouncilors.\n• Removed officer (City Manager, Judge, City Attorney) \nduties from the Charter and wil be adopted by \nresolution or ordinance.\nThis measure was referred to by the voters of the City of \nKing City and City Council based on the recommendation \nof the Charter Review Committee and City Council \nworkshops and special session meetings.\nExplanatory Statement\nKing City’s Charter is the City’s governing document. It \nestablishes the City’s organization, powers, responsibilities of \nelected officials and officers, and procedures for Council action. \nThe Charter was last revised in 2006.\nThe City Council created a Charter Review Committee to \nreview the Charter and recommend updates. The Committee \nmet publicly from April 2025 through January 2026. The \nCity Council considered the Committee’s recommendations \nat public meetings and work sessions before referring this \nmeasure to voters.\nThis measure would amend and restate the Charter. It would \nretain King City’s council-manager form of government, current \nnumber of Councilors, and current method for selecting the \nMayor. It would reorganize the Charter and add definitions to \nclarify terms used throughout the document.\nThe measure would update provisions addressing City powers; \nCity Council membership, vacancies, officers, meetings, and \nprocedures; City officers; departments and agencies; financial \nmanagement; and miscellaneous matters. It would separately \nidentify legislative, administrative, and quasi-judicial authority \nand add a Charter review provision.\nThe measure would revise requirements concerning vacancies, \nCouncilor duties, prohibitions, and Councilor compensation. It \nwould remove detailed duties of the City Manager, Municipal\nBallot Title Judge, and City Attorney from the Charter. Those duties would instead be established by City ordinance or resolution, as \napplicable.\nThe complete proposed Charter is available at King City -- City \nHall and on the City’s website. If the measure does not pass, \nthe current Charter will remain in effect.\nSubmitted By: Ronnie Smith\n\nWC-58\nCandidate \nstatements \nand measure \narguments \nare printed \nas submitted\nWashington County \ndoes not fact check the \ninformation submitted by \ncandidates and measure \nargument filers. Inaccurate \nor wrong information is \nnot corrected.\nMeasure No. 34-354 Arguments\nCity of King CityCity of King City\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an \nendorsement by Washington County, nor does the county \nwarrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Opposition\nMeasure 34–354\nCity of King City Charter Modernization Amendments\nVOTE NO \nThis amendment revises the present King City charter \nwith one GLARING omission!\n \nAs is, this amendment does NOT change the present system.\n4 out of 7 council members still control \ntheir choice for your mayor as selected by city council.\n \nThat is not right!\nA NO VOTE sends this revised charter amendment \nback to the next city council to be sworn in\nJanuary 2027\nThen it can be revised \nSo that you the citizens can \n \nVOTE for your Mayor!\nFrom the League of Oregon Cities – \nThere are 243 towns/cities in Oregon \nOnly 11 still appoint the mayor from city council.\nKing City population ranks 105 out of 243 \n(Approximately 5600 residents and growing!)\n Towns and cities much smaller than King City \nallow all their residents \nto choose by vote whom they trust to lead them.\nThis Information Furnished By: Vincent Arditi\n\nWC-59\nMeasure No. 34-355\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nPROJECTED TO RESULT IN LOWER BOND TAX RATE \nThis measure is expected to result in a bond levy rate of \n$1.84 per $1,000 of assessed value, which is lower than the \naverage rate District taxpayers have paid since the last \nbond was approved. \nSECURES MATCHING FUNDS \nAdditionally, the District would receive $6.1 million in \nstate matching funds through the Oregon School Capital \nImprovement Match Program, but only if the bond measure \npasses. \nHILLSBORO SCHOOLS FACE CHALLENGES\n• Aging School Buildings: Roofs, siding, HVAC systems, \nplumbing, flooring, and lighting across the District are \nreaching the end of their usable life. These systems need \nrepair or replacement to keep schools functional, dry, \nand safe, and to prevent further deterioration and costlier \nrepairs later.\n• Healthy Schools: Some building systems are causing \nleaks and mold, conditions that have led to classroom \nclosures in parts of the District.\n• Safety and Security: District facilities need updated \ndoors, locks, fencing, cameras, and phone systems, along \nwith stronger cybersecurity and network protections to \nkeep students and staff safe.\n• Career and Technical Education: Vocational and career-\ntraining spaces have not kept pace with growing demand \nfor computing, fire science, and STEM programs.\n2026 BOND REQUEST \nHillsboro School District has developed a bond proposal to \naddress these facility needs. By law, bond proceeds may only \nbe used for capital costs. If passed, the bond is expected to \nfund:\n• Fix Aging Buildings:\nRepair or replace failing roofs, siding, HVAC, plumbing, \nflooring, and lighting\n• Support Healthier Schools:\nReplace systems causing leaks, mold, and closed \nclassrooms\n• Increase Safety & Security:\nInstall new doors, locks, fencing, cameras, and phone \nsystems; enhance building automation, cybersecurity, and \nnetwork infrastructure\n• Expand Career & Technical Education:\nUpdate computing labs, create a fire science classroom, \nexpand STEM and vocational spaces\n• Update District Facilities and Equipment including:\nUpgrade fields, tracks, and Title IX facilities; replace \nbuses, kitchen equipment, facility fleet vehicles, and aging \ntechnology\nOVERSIGHT AND ACCOUNTABILITY \nA community oversight committee would monitor bond \nspending to ensure funds are used for voter-approved projects.\nFINANCIALS\n• Bonds may be issued in multiple series, each maturing \nwithin 21 years from issuance.\n• This measure is estimated to result in a bond levy rate of \n$1.84 per $1,000 of assessed property value.\n• Actual levy rates may differ due to changes in interest \nrates and property values.\nSubmitted By: Beth Graser\nBonds Fixing Failing & Aging Infrastructure; Improving \nSafety, Vocational Facilities.\nQuestion: Shall District issue bonds to increase safety, \nsecurity, repair aging schools and failing infrastructure, \nexpand vocational classrooms, secure matching funds?\nIf the bonds are approved, they will be payable from taxes \non property or property ownership that are not subject to \nthe limits of sections 11 and 11b, Article XI of the Oregon \nConstitution.\nSummary: \nLower Tax Rate: \nThis measure is expected to result in a bond levy rate \nof $1.84 per $1,000 assessed value – lower than the \naverage rate paid since last bond approval.\nIf passed, District is eligible for $6.1 million in state \nmatching funds.\nHillsboro School District seeks approval for a principal \namount of $430 million in general obligation bonds to \nfinance capital costs, including:\nFix Aging Buildings:\n• Replace failing roofs, siding, HVAC, plumbing, \nflooring, and lighting.\nHealthy Schools: \n• Replace systems causing leaks, mold, closed \nclassrooms\nSafety & Security:\n• New doors, locks, fencing, cameras, phone systems, \nbuilding automation.\n• Enhance cybersecurity, network infrastructure\nCareer & Technical Education:\n• Update computing labs, create fire science \nclassroom, expand STEM and vocational spaces.\nAdditional Projects:\n• Field, track, and Title IX upgrades; bus, kitchen, fleet \nand aging technology replacements.\nRequires community oversight committee, regular \naudits.\nFinancials:\n• Bonds may be issued in multiple series, each \nmaturing within 21 years from issuance.\n• Estimated levy rate is $1.84 per $1,000 of assessed \nvalue.\n• Actual levy rates may differ due to interest rates, \nchanges in property value.\nBallot Title\nExplanatory Statement\nHillsboro School District is the fourth-largest school district \nin Oregon, serving approximately 18,000 students across 37 \nschools in Hillsboro, North Plains, Cornelius, and surrounding \nareas of Washington County. \nCONTINUES THE COMMUNITY’S INVESTMENT IN \nSCHOOLS \nThis bond measure is part of the District’s ongoing commitment \nto safe, secure, and well maintained schools for Hillsboro-area \nstudents and staff.\n\nWC-60\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nYES ON MEASURE 34-355: SCHOOLS THAT ARE SECURE, \nREPAIRED, PREPARED - WITHOUT RAISING TAXES\nThe Hillsboro School Board unanimously voted to place \nMeasure 34-355 on the ballot because students deserve \nschools that are safe, well-maintained, and ready for the future.\nVoters have a proud history of approving bond measures to \nensure the District has local dollars to support the urgent needs \nof its facilities, equipment, and programs.\nA YES vote on 34-355 will repair aging buildings, secure \nevery campus, and prepare students for real careers, without \nraising taxes.\nREPAIR\nRoofs, HVAC, plumbing, and other major building components \nacross the District are reaching the end of their usable life. \nAn aging boiler in one building already caused mold issues, \nclosing classrooms for nearly two months. This bond fixes \nthese problems before they get worse and more expensive.\nSECURE\nSafe campuses for students and staff is a District priority. \nThis bond funds new doors, locks, fencing, cameras, and \nphone systems, plus upgraded cybersecurity and network \ninfrastructure, so every school campus is protected.\nPREPARE\nA new Fire Science classroom, advanced computing labs, and \nexpanded STEM and vocational spaces give Hillsboro School \nDistrict students hands-on training for their future careers.\nNO TAX INCREASE\nThis bond will keep the tax rate at or below what District \ntaxpayers have paid on average since the last bond. Passing \nthe bond also makes the District eligible for an additional $6.1 \nmillion in state matching funds. \nACCOUNTABILITY\nA community oversight committee and regular independent \naudits will ensure every dollar is spent as promised.\nMeasure 34-355 has significant support from parents, teachers, \ncivic leaders, and the business community, which shows a firm \ncommitment to investing in our students’ future. \nVote YES on 34-355 to keep schools secure, repaired, and \nready!\nHillsboro School Board\nKatie Rhyne, Chair \nMark Watson, Vice Chair \nSee Eun Kim \nPatrick Maguire \nYessica Hardin Mercado \nIvette Pantoja \nNancy Thomas\nLearn more: YesForHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Katie Rhyne, Hillsboro School \nBoard\nArgument in Favor\nSÍ A LA MEDIDA 34-355!\nLa Mesa Directiva del Distrito Escolar de Hillsboro votó por \nunanimidad para presentar la Medida 34-355 porque nuestros \nestudiantes merecen escuelas seguras, en buenas condiciones \ny preparadas para el futuro. \nVotar Sí a la Medida 34-355 permitirá reparar y renovar \nedificios, mejorar la seguridad y ampliar oportunidades para \nnuestros estudiantes, sin aumentar los impuestos. \nREPARAR Y RENOVAR\nLos techos, sistemas de calefacción y ventilación, tuberías y \notros componentes están llegando al final de su vida útil. En \nuna escuela, una caldera antigua ya provocó problemas de \nmoho que obligaron a cerrar salones de clase casi dos meses. \nEste bono permitirá atender estos problemas antes de que \nsean más graves y costosos. \nPROTEGER\nQueremos escuelas seguras para nuestros estudiantes y \npersonal. Este bono mejorará la seguridad con puertas, \ncerraduras, cercas, cámaras y sistemas de comunicación. \nTambién ayudará a proteger los sistemas y redes tecnológicas \ndel Distrito. \nPREPARAR\nUn nuevo salón para Ciencias de Bomberos, laboratorios \navanzados de computación y más espacios para STEM \ny educación técnica y vocacional brindarán experiencias \nprácticas y habilidades para futuras carreras. \nSIN AUMENTAR LOS IMPUESTOS\nEste bono mantendrá la tasa de impuestos igual o por debajo \ndel promedio registrado desde la aprobación del último bono. \nAdemás, el Distrito podrá recibir $6.1 millones adicionales en \nfondos de contrapartida del estado. \nTRANSPARENCIA Y RESPONSABILIDAD\nUn comité de supervisión comunitaria y auditorías \nindependientes ayudarán a asegurar que los fondos se utilicen \nsegún lo prometido.\nLa Medida 34-355 cuenta con el apoyo de familias, \neducadores, líderes comunitarios y negocios comprometidos \ncon nuestros estudiantes y el futuro de Hillsboro. \nVota SÍ a la medida 34-355 para reparar y renovar nuestras \nescuelas, proteger a nuestros estudiantes y prepararlos \npara el futuro. \nMesa Directiva del Distrito Escolar de Hillsboro\nLatino PAC HSD\nMaria Caballero Rubio, Centro Cultural\nOlga Acuña, Exconcejal de la Ciudad de Hillsboro\nCecilia Giron, Presidenta, LULAC of Washington County\nVisita: YesForHillsboroSchools.com\nInformación Proporcionada Por: Yessica Hardin Mercado, \nHillsboro School Board\n\nWC-61\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nFIREFIGHTERS, POLICE, AND LABOR UNIONS URGE YOU \nTO VOTE YES FOR SAFE AND SECURE SCHOOLS\nSafety and physical wellbeing for students and staff should \nalways be the number one priority at schools. Right now, \nsome Hillsboro School District buildings don’t have the modern \nsecurity systems that our kids and educators need. Measure \n34-355 will change that.\nSecuring Our Schools\nStaff should always know who is on campus, which is why this \nbond measure will ensure that every school has modern locks, \nfencing, and camera coverage that are critical for complete \nsecurity. \nIn an emergency, seconds matter, and schools must respond \nquickly. This bond will replace outdated devices and unsafe \nclassroom dividers with new emergency alerts and upgraded \nphone systems, cybersecurity protections, and classrooms \nthat can be properly secured and locked. \nHealthy Buildings Matter, Too\nSafety means physical health, too, and declining HVAC \nsystems or deteriorating building components can create \nhazards for students and staff alike. An aging boiler from the \n1950s already caused mold issues that shut down one school’s \nclassrooms for nearly two months. This bond addresses \nthese problems directly, before they can cause harm.\nAccountable & Responsible \nMeasure 34-355 will keep tax rates at or below what District \ntaxpayers have been paying, and includes a community \noversight committee and independent audits to monitor \nresponsible, transparent spending, every step of the way. \nOur students and the staff who care for them deserve schools \nthat are genuinely secure and healthy. \nPlease join educators and public safety professionals in \nvoting YES on Measure 34-355!\nHillsboro Education Association\nMary Kay Babcock, President\nHillsboro Classified United 4671\nDavid Eggleston, President\nHillsboro Professional Firefighters \nHillsboro Police Officers’ Association\nCaprice Massey, Washington County Sheriff  \nPat Garrett, Former Washington County Sheriff\nEducators\nMike Scott, Former Superintendent\nNadine Zimmerlund, Retired Administrator\nSteve Callaway, Former Principal\nCynthia Honma, Retired Educator\nJon Pede, Former Principal\nCrystal Schmidt DiPaola, Former Principal\nLearn more at YesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Nancy Thomas, Hillsboro School \nBoard\nArgument in Favor\nHILLSBORO SCHOOL DISTRICT EDUCATORS: YES ON \nMEASURE 34-355!\nEducators see every day how the condition of our schools \naffects teaching and learning. If school buildings don’t work the \nway they should, student and teacher performance suffers.\nAcross the District, many declining building components need \nrepair or replacement. They are the basics every school \ndepends on: roofs, heating and cooling systems, plumbing, \nkitchens, lighting, flooring, and technology. \nIf aging systems break down, consequences are real. A boiler \nthat was original to its 1950s building recently leaked, causing \nmold and closing several classrooms for nearly two months. \nThis bond addresses these problems before the next failure \ndisrupts students and becomes a more expensive emergency.\nMeasure 34-355 takes care of the public investment our \ncommunity made in its schools.\nBond dollars are restricted to capital projects, not staff salaries \nor regular daily operating expenses. Addressing major repairs \nthrough the bond keeps education dollars from being diverted \nto costly emergency repairs.\nFor students and educators, that means safer, more reliable \nschools with fewer disruptions. For taxpayers, it means \nmaintaining current buildings and addressing aging systems \nbefore they become expensive problems.\nWell-maintained, safe public schools are an important part of a \nstrong community.\nJoin Hillsboro School District educators in voting YES on \nMeasure 34-355!\nHillsboro Education Association\nHillsboro Classified United 4671\nPRINCIPALS\nAmanda Bethune, Butternut Creek ES\nJose Barraza, Eastwood ES\nShannon Lawson, Hillsboro Online Academy\nGraciela Cerda, Imlay Elementary\nAurora Lopez, Mooberry ES\nDevin Dye, North Plains ES\nAllison Combs, Orenco ES\nKellie Petrick, Patterson ES\nJamie Lentz, Quatama ES\nAndrew Bekken, Brown MS\nChristy Walters, Tamarack ES\nJuliet Hong, West Union ES\nTEACHERS\nAndy Bunting, Eastwood ES\nDarren Battaglia, Poynter MS\nDavid Juster, Hillsboro HS\nJeff Johnston, Glencoe HS\nKristina Schmalzer, City View Charter School\nLeAndre Butler, Imlay ES\nLisa Tanedo, Evergreen MS\nNicole Genduso, Ladd Acres ES\nÚna Maher, Hillsboro Online Academy\nLearn more: YesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: See Eun Kim, Hillsboro School \nBoard of Directors\n\nWC-62\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nLOCAL ELECTED LEADERS SUPPORT HILLSBORO \nSCHOOL BOND\nMeasure 34-355 is a chance to stand up for public \neducation. \nLocal elected officials know that what families want for their \nkids is a high-quality education in secure, modern schools. \nMeasure 34-355 will help ensure every student has access \nto a safe, healthy, updated learning environment—because \ninvesting in our schools is investing in our collective future. \nBetter schools help build a stronger community and give \nour kids more opportunities to succeed.\nA YES vote will: \n• Renew our investment in public education—without \nraising tax rates \n• Fund critical maintenance and repairs, so schools are safe \nand healthy spaces\n• Upgrade and expand Career & Technical Education \nequipment and computing labs\n• Improve ADA accessibility to be equitable for students, \nstaff, and visitors\n• Build a new classroom for the Fire Science program\nThis bond is not just about critical maintenance and repairs \nto school facilities—it’s about giving all kids a better future. \nStudents deserve to learn in buildings that are safe, where \nthey feel secure and comfortable, and where learning is not \ninterrupted by unexpected system failures. This investment is \nneeded now to ensure they can thrive. \nJoin us in standing up for our local schools! \nPlease vote YES on Measure 34-355!\nOregon State Senators \nSenator WLinsvey Campos\nSenator Janeen Sollman\nSenator Lisa Reynolds, M.D.\nOregon State Representatives\nRepresentative Nathan Sosa\nRepresentative Susan McLain\nRepresentative Hai Pham\nRepresentative Farrah Chaichi\nOregon Labor Commissioner Christina Stephenson\nMetro Council\nJuan Carlos González, President\nMiles Palacios\nWashington County \nCommissioners\nKathryn Harrington, Chair\nJerry Willey, Vice Chair\nJason Snider\nCaprice Massey, Washington County Sheriff\nKristine Adams-Wannberg, Washington County Auditor\nHillsboro City Council\nBeach Pace, Mayor\nRob Harris, President\nOlivia Alcaire\nSaba Anvery\nElizabeth Case\nCristian Salgado\nKipperlyn Sinclair\nCornelius City Council\nEdgar Baker\nEdén Lopez\nLearn more at: YesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Mark Watson, Hillsboro School \nBoard\nArgument in Favor\nBUSINESS & WORKFORCE LEADERS: MEASURE 34-355 \nPREPARES STUDENTS FOR REAL CAREERS\nStrong schools build a strong local economy. \nBusiness owners, employers, and local leaders know a \nstrong workforce starts with strong local schools. Please join \nHillsboro’s business community in voting YES to prepare our \nstudents, and our workforce, for the future.\nUpgrade Hands-On Learning  Spaces\nHigh Schools need to meet the growing demand for hands-on \ncareer training. Measure 34-355 funds a new Fire Science \nclassroom, updated computing and STEM labs, and \nexpanded STEM and vocational spaces, giving Hillsboro \nstudents firsthand preparation for high-demand jobs in \nconstruction, technology, health care, and public safety. \nGraduate Prepared and Ready\nStudents with Career and Technical Education experience \nfinish high school ready to step directly into skilled, well-paying \nwork in career fields that our whole community depends on. \nEmployers across our region are asking for more graduates \nwith exactly this kind of training. Investing now means Hillsboro \nstudents won’t have to leave our community to find opportunity. \nLocal businesses have access to a homegrown, well-trained \nworkforce for years to come.\nSmart Investment for Taxpayers\nThis bond keeps the tax rate at or below what District taxpayers \nhave paid on average since the last bond, and it unlocks $6.1 \nmillion in state matching funds that Hillsboro cannot receive \nunless this measure passes. A community oversight committee \nand independent audits will ensure funds are spent exactly as \npromised.\nStrong schools build a strong local economy. \nBusiness owners, educators, civic leaders, and \nentrepreneurs across our community ask you to please \nvote YES on Measure 34-355!\nWashington County Chamber of Commerce \nDeanna Palm, Former CEO/President, Chamber of Commerce\nMike & Laura McMurray, Hillsboro Hops \nNina Carlson, NW Natural\nYadira Martinez, Dental Therapy Programs Manager\nTitonian Wallace Sr., Nesace Media\nAbraham Diaz, Business/Marketing Teacher, Glencoe HS\nMaureen Wolf, Chair, NWRESD Board\nGina Roletto, PCC Board\nYesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Ivette Pantoja, Hillsboro School \nBoard\n\nWC-63\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nHILLSBORO PARENTS: A VOTE FOR MEASURE 34-355 IS \nA VOTE FOR OUR KIDS’ FUTURE!\nEvery parent deserves to send their child to school knowing \nthat they will be safe, healthy, and given real opportunities to \nsucceed. Bond Measure 34-355 does exactly that. Vote YES to \nensure that our public investment is in schools that are secure, \nwell-maintained, and built for the future. \nSafety Should Come First\nThe District’s last bond funded secure entry systems at each \nschool. This bond would enhance these installations further, \nwith upgraded fire doors, fencing, door locks, security cameras, \nand a panic alert system for staff.\nTimely Maintenance Keeps Learning Focused\nWhen one school’s boiler failed, resulting mold issues \ndisplaced classrooms for nearly two months. Replacing roofs, \nHVAC, and other building systems allows our students to focus \non their learning, and not be interrupted by leaks or unhealthy \nmold issues. \nInvestment in the Future\nMeasure 34-355 will fund opportunities not just for our kids, but \nfor the future of our community: a new Fire Science classroom, \nupdated computing and STEM labs, and expanded vocational \nspaces will let Hillsboro School District students explore real \ncareers before they ever graduate.  \nThe Bottom Line Matters, Too\nThe bond will not raise the tax rate above what we as \ntaxpayers have already been paying, and a community \noversight committee will make sure every dollar goes exactly \nwhere it’s promised.\nJoin Hillsboro School District parents: vote YES on \nMeasure 34-355!\nParents, Organizations\nLatino PAC HSD\nBlack Village Family Advisory Committee\nCentury HS Boosters\nEastwood Elementary PTA\nImlay Elementary PTA\nIndian Hills Elementary PTO\nMcKinney Elementary PTO\nOrenco Elementary Boosters\nTamarack Community Group\nAlicia Infante\nKyle & Lisa Allen\nRobyn & Jake Bruun\nBriana Ellenberger\nAlexandra Rosas Castro\nGraduates\nAbraham Staffa, Hillsboro Online Academy ‘26\nKal Sibbel, LHS ‘25\nHazel Cleveland, Oak Street Campus ‘26\nNicole Ayala Congachi, GHS ‘26\nDanny Adzima, HHS ‘21\nLearn more: YesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Briana Ellenberger, Orenco \nBoosters\nArgument in Favor\nEXTRACURRICULARS MEAN BETTER STUDENT \nEDUCATIONAL OUTCOMES: YES ON 34-355\nExtracurricular activities aren’t just about having fun, they also \nhelp students succeed: students involved in at least one \nextracurricular activity attend school more regularly and get \nbetter grades than their peers. Measure 34-355 will make sure \nthere are safe, adequate options and facilities for students to \nengage in activities that lead to better academic outcomes. \nExpanding and Enhancing Existing Facilities\nBond project improvements include: \n• Lighted turf fields to expand practice time year-round for \nschool and community sports teams, as well as band, \ncheer, and colorguard\n• Enhancements to auditoriums for plays and performances\n• Additional on-field bathrooms for participants and \nspectators\n• Added hitting facilities and dugouts to make playing \nspaces equitable for boys and girls teams \nSchool Facilities are Community Facilities\nHillsboro School District schools and fields are resources that \nare used year-round by community youth and adult groups, \nproviding a benefit that extends beyond current students. \nCapital Bonds Allow Broad and Equitable Development\nDonations from community groups and booster clubs help with \nindividual projects at specific schools, but more improvements \nare needed at facilities all over the District. Measure 34-355 will \nensure that these additions, expansions, and enhancements of \nstudent spaces are made in an equitable way. \nNo Tax Rate Increase\nThis bond keeps the tax rate at or below what District \ntaxpayers have paid on average since the last bond. Passing \nthe bond also makes the District eligible for an additional $6.1 \nmillion in state matching funds. \nJoin us in voting YES for Measure 34-355 for better sports \nand activity spaces across the District! \nRian Petrick, Director of Extracurricular Services and Athletics\nJonathan Lopez Arana, Hillsboro HS Athletic Director\nKathryn Pawelko, Evergreen MS\nAlicia Glasscock, Imlay ES\nKL Wombacher, Hillsboro Hops\nJen Anderson, Hillsboro Hops\nDorian Russell, NWRESD Board\nBecky Tymchuk, NWRESD Board\nKristi Wilson, Former PCC Board Chair & Parent\nYesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Patrick Maguire, Hillsboro \nSchool Board\n\nWC-64\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Favor\nCOMMUNITY LEADERS SAY: THE BOND IS A \nRESPONSIBLE INVESTMENT FOR TAXPAYERS \nAs taxpayers, we look hard at every measure coming before \nus. We know the difference between wasteful spending and a \ngenuinely responsible investment.\nThis measure will not raise the tax rate above what we’ve \npaid for the past decade.\nIt replaces expiring debt with new investment, rather than \nputting an additional burden on top of what families already \npay. It also makes Hillsboro School District eligible for $6.1 \nmillion in state matching funds, money that disappears if this \nbond doesn’t pass.\nMeasure 34-355 funds the basic repairs, security, and \neducation upgrades our students need. It invests in the \nbuildings we already own.\nFor nearly three decades, Hillsboro School District has been \nrecognized for its fiscal responsibility, earning that distinction \nyear after year through careful budgeting and honest public \nreporting. That track record matters. It’s a big reason we’re \nconfident asking our neighbors to trust the District with this \nbond.\nOn top of the District’s own record, this bond includes a \ncommunity oversight committee and independent audits, so \nresidents will see exactly how every dollar is spent.\nWe don’t support blank checks. We support proven, \naccountable management of our tax dollars, and that’s \nexactly what Hillsboro School District delivers. \nYou can read about the bond and explore every project with the \ninteractive dashboard at Hillsboro-Bond.org.\nJoin us in voting YES on Measure 34-355, a responsible \ninvestment from a district that has earned our trust.\nHSD Budget Committee Community Representatives\nKristine Adams-Wannberg\nRobert Knoke\nMaria Mondragon-Almy\nDustin Rose\nCommunity Leaders\nKyle & Lisa Allen\nLaura Bekken\nHarvey Berkey\nMaria Caballero Rubio\nSteve Callaway\nDebbie Clarke\nDebby Garman\nMarilynn Helzerman\nTom Hughes\nErika Lopez\nCarol Loughner\nFred & Gayle Nachtigal\nDeanna Palm\nChuck & Katie Riley\nJaci Spross\nKim Strelchun\nAnnie Tronco\nHilary Uhlig\nNik Whitcomb\nJerry & Judy Willey\nMike & Nadine Zimmerlund\nYesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Kim Strelchun\nArgument in Favor\nFrom the Fire Science Classroom to the Fire Department: \nHillsboro Graduates Say Vote Yes!\nWe are graduates of Hillsboro School District’s Career and \nTechnical Education programs, and this bond directly funds the \nkind of hands-on training that changed the direction of our \nlives.\nBefore we ever earned a diploma, the Fire Science CTE \nprogram gave us real experience for our future careers as \nfirefighters and paramedics. That training didn’t just prepare \nus for a job. It gave us a head start most students never \nget, and it’s the reason several of us walked straight from \ngraduation into positions we love with local fire departments \nand paramedic teams.\n“These Career and Technical Education programs are real-\nworld opportunities that ultimately lead to a career in our \ncommunity.” - Isaac Lacatus, Paramedic Student\nThe problem is that demand for these programs has outgrown \nthe District’s available space. This bond funds a new Fire \nScience classroom and expanded computing and STEM \nlabs, so that students after us won’t be turned away because \na program is full or outdated, and will graduate with high-\ndemand job prospects like we did.   \n \nWe know firsthand what this investment is worth. It’s not a \nhypothetical benefit. It’s the difference between graduating with \noptions, or without them.\nMeasure 34-355 does all of this while keeping the tax rate \nat or below what Hillsboro families already pay, and it puts a \ncommunity oversight committee in place to track exactly how \nthe money is spent.\nWe’re living proof of what these programs can do. Please join \nus in voting YES so the next generation of Hillsboro School \nDistrict students gets the same life-changing opportunities that \nwe did.\nVote YES on Measure 34-355. \nHillsboro Professional Firefighters\nRhea Duncan, Fire Science Instructor\nFire Science Graduates \nIsaac Lacatus, HHS ‘24 \nCameron Bell, CHS ‘26 \nKiyah Parker, LHS ‘26 \nNathaniel Pratt, GHS ‘26 \nMadox Rekow, LHS ‘26\nYesforHillsboroSchools.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Katie Rhyne, Hillsboro School \nBoard\n\nWC-65\nMeasure No. 34-355 Arguments\nHillsboro School District 1JHillsboro School District 1J\nThe printing of these arguments does not constitute an endorsement by Washington County, \nnor does the county warrant the accuracy or truth of any statements.\nArgument in Opposition\nTaxpayers Association of Oregon  \nsays Vote no on 34-355\nIn just over 10 years,  \nOregon schools received $9.8 billion  \nfrom brand “new” tax sources!\n• In 2012, politicians helped a pass measure stealing the \nbusiness Kicker Tax Refund and diverting it to education.  \nResult = $2 billion more for education since.\n• In 2014, school advocates backed legalizing marijuana \nbecause the pot tax would fund schools. Result = $220 \nmillion more for education since.  \n• In 2016, voters approved Ballot Measure 99 to divert \nlottery funds towards funding school outdoor programs. \nResult = nearly $50 million more for education since.\n• In 2019, lawmakers approved one of the largest taxes in \nOregon history (Corporate Activities Tax) to fund schools.  \nResult = $6 billion more for education since.\n• In 2022, Congress had fully awarded Oregon $1.6 billion \nin Covid school assistance. Result = $1.6 billion more for \nschools.\nThese are all new sources of tax money for schools.\nWhat good is raising $9 billion more for schools across the \nstate if local property taxes keep going up?\nOur school districts need to do better with the extra funds we \nare sending them.\nVote No on 34-355\n25 years of daily tax and political news at OregonWatchdog.com \nJoin the movement to keep taxes low & stop government waste at  \nOregonWatchdog.com\nThis Information Furnished By: Jason Williams\nArgument in Opposition\nTaxpayers Association of Oregon  \nsays Vote no on 34-355\nHillsboro has seen a staggering 10% decline in enrollment \nover the years.\n \nWhy go on a $430 million bond spendingspree that includes \n“expanding spaces” when you are losing students?\n \nIf you have less students to serve, you ask for less.\n \nSeniors on limited income can’t afford these non-stop property \ntaxes.\n \nJoin the movement to keep taxes low & stop government waste at \nOregonWatchdog.com \n(since 1999)\nThis Information Furnished By: Jason Williams\n\nWC-66\nThe Washington County ballot tracking program is a partnership with i3ballot. \nIf you have any questions about this service, please call \nWashington County Elections at (503) 846-5800 or email us at \nelections@washingtoncountyor.gov\nSign up at\nwashcovotes.ballottrax.net/voter\nor scan this QR code\nTrack Your Ballot!\nSign up to receive updates and notifications about your ballot from \nWashington County Elections. Receive text, email, or voice alerts when \nwe have mailed your ballot, when your ballot has been received at the \nElections oﬀice, and when your ballot has been accepted and counted. \nSign up today to begin receiving\nnotifications on your ballot!\n\nWC-67\nScan this QR code \nto see these drop site \nlocations on a map:\nYou may drop oﬀ your ballot at any oﬀicial ballot drop box or elections oﬀice in the State of Oregon.\nALOHA\nAloha Community Library\n17455 SW Farmington Rd, Ste. 26A\nAloha, OR 97007\nBANKS\nBanks City Hall/Library\n42461 NW Market Street\nBanks, OR 97106\nBEAVERTON\nBeaverton City (Near Library)\nCurbside on Hall Boulevard, \nbetween 3rd and 5th Streets\nBeaverton, OR 97005\nBeaverton Library-Murray Scholls\n11200 SW Murray Scholls Pl, Ste. 102\nBeaverton, OR 97007\nBethany Library\n15325 NW Central Dr, Ste J-8\nPortland, OR 97229\nCedar Mill Community Library\n1080 NW Saltzman Rd\nPortland, OR 97229\nWashington County Service Center East\n3700 SW Murray Blvd\nBeaverton, OR 97005\nWest Slope Community Library\n3678 SW 78th Ave\nPortland, OR 97225\nCORNELIUS\nCornelius City Hall\n1355 N Barlow St\nCornelius, OR 97113\nFOREST GROVE\nForest Grove City Library\nPacific Ave & Birch St\nForest Grove, OR 97116\nGARDEN HOME\nGarden Home Community Library\n7475 SW Oleson Rd\nPortland, OR 97223\nHILLSBORO\nHillsboro Main Library\n2850 NE Brookwood Pkwy\nHillsboro, OR 97124\nHillsboro Public Library-Shute Park\n775 SE 10th Ave\nHillsboro, OR 97123\nPublic Services Building Parking Lot\n155 N 1st Ave\nHillsboro, OR 97124\nWashington County Elections\n2925 NE Aloclek Dr, Ste 170\nHillsboro, OR 97124\nWestside Commons\n801 NE 34th Ave\nHillsboro, OR 97124\nKING CITY\nKing City City Hall\n15300 SW 116th Ave\nKing City, OR 97224\nNORTH PLAINS\nNorth Plains Library Annex\n31360 NW Commercial St\nNorth Plains, OR 97133\nSHERWOOD\nSherwood City Hall\n22560 SW Pine St\nSherwood, OR 97140\nTIGARD\nTigard City Hall\n13125 SW Hall Blvd\nTigard, OR 97223\nTUALATIN\nTualatin City Hall\n18880 SW Martinazzi Ave\nTualatin, OR 97062\nWASHINGTON COUNTY\nOﬀicial Ballot Drop Sites\nReturn your voted ballot to an oﬀicial ballot drop box by 8 p.m. \non Election Day. No postage is required.\nAll of the oﬀicial drop boxes listed below are open 24 hours a day \nuntil the 8 p.m. deadline on Election Day.\n\nWC-PB\nWashington County Elections\n3700 SW Murray Blvd  Suite 101\nBeaverton  OR 97005 \nNonprofit Organization\nCAR-RT SORT\nUS Postage \nPaid \nPortland, OR\nPermit No. 2467\nVoters’ \nPamphlet\nwww.co.washington.or.us\nDated Election Material\nResidential Customer\nWashington County Elections\n3700 SW Murray Blvd  Suite 101\nBeaverton  OR 97005\nNonprofit Organization\nCAR-RT SORT\nUS Postage \nPaid \nPortland, OR\nPermit No. 2467\nVoters’ \nPamphlet\nwww.co.washington.or.us\nDated Election Material\nResidential Customer\nWashington County Elections\n3700 SW Murray Blvd  Suite 101\nBeaverton  OR 97005\nNonprofit Organization\nCAR-RT SORT\nUS Postage \nPaid \nPortland, OR\nPermit No. 2467\nVoters’\nPamphlet\nwww.co.washington.or.us\nDated Election Material\nResidential Customer\n2925 NE Aloclek Drive, Suite 170\nHillsboro, OR 97124-7523\nwww.washcovotes.org","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":15083.238313000038,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}